HT_DATA.load("detail", {"7IsMeKl-Sv0":{"wy":"HN keeps pointing to it as the clearest explanation of how low-density infrastructure is financed and why it is unsustainable.","q":{"x":"I can't recommend this video [1] by Not Just Bikes enough.","i":32597751,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32597751,"author":"ARandumGuy","month":"2022-08","story":"Too many Americans live in places built for cars – not for human connection","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hidden costs of low density development.","quote":"I can't recommend this video [1] by Not Just Bikes enough.","text":"> Do you have a citation for this? I don't have a citation for construction costs, and I will concede that there are many factors that may make some low density developments cheaper per unit then some higher density development. However, construction is only part of the picture. Low density development imposes terrible financial costs for cities that contain them. That's because the taxable value per square mile is much lower, but infrastructure (sewer, roads, etc) has the same per-mile maintenance and replacement costs. The infrastructure maintenance costs in many low density developments is often greater then the amount of tax revenue the development generates. For more information on the hidden costs of low density development, I can't recommend this video [1] by Not Just Bikes enough. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30842576,"author":"kd913","month":"2022-03","story":"We can do better than “same, but electric”","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains cost of maintaining infrastructure to your house.","quote":"Not Just Bikes did an excellent series on this specific problem.","text":"I don't think you understand how much it costs to maintain the infrastructure to your house. Not Just Bikes did an excellent series on this specific problem. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40676805,"author":"crote","month":"2024-06","story":"Rent in Cities Skylines 2 was too high, so the devs removed landlords","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to suburban subsidy and infrastructure costs.","quote":"the following NotJustBikes videos are a good introduction of the subject","text":"In large parts of the United States it should affect single-family homes. A lot of cities constructed their suburbs with one-term fees and subsidies, but are charging extremely low taxes on the houses once constructed. In fact, the taxes are so low that they don't pay for the upkeep of the infrastructure required to service those houses. They are losing money on every house and are trying to make up for it in volume! The sole reason they have been able to continue is because the dense urban core is subsidizing the suburbs. But it'll inevitably end up with decrepit and neglected infrastructure, and cities going bankrupt. The only way to solve this is to increase the revenue per land area, which means a higher land tax, which in turn means a bigger tax increase per-person for single-family homes than for multi-family dwellings. If you want to know more, the following NotJustBikes videos are a good introduction of the subject: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":38197564,"author":"kdmccormick","month":"2023-11","story":"Spain lives in flats: why we have built our cities vertically","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why suburbia can't pay for itself.","quote":"does a good just explaining why it'd be basically impossible for suburbia to ever pay for itself","text":"It is indeed the system, and you (to no fault of your own, probably) are part of that system. NotJustBikes, while a little anti-car-propogandaey for my tast, does a good just explaining why it'd be basically impossible for suburbia to ever pay for itself: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38313179,"author":"otikik","month":"2023-11","story":"280M e-bikes and mopeds are cutting demand for oil far more than electric cars","stance":"rec","why":"Explains suburban street funding and long-term costs.","quote":"Here's an explanation by Not Just Bikes","text":"> Those costs aren't really all that high, and are easily outweighed by the dramatically lower costs in the suburbs. Everything is cheaper there, land, buildings, repairs, goods and services. He's talking about infrastructure cost (how much it costs your municipality to provide the services that you use, like the roads) while you are talking about cost of life (how much it costs you to pay for other things that you need, like food or getting your car serviced). On the infrastructure front, suburbia simply cannot compete with cities. Infrastructure cost is bound to be more efficient where people live in more densely populated areas. The length of pipes that a municipality needs to lay down in order to serve a single family home in the suburbs is the same as a whole 6-story building in the city (the diameters of the pipes might differ). On a per-capita basis, there's no contest. A particular factor in the funding of suburban streets is that the initial paving is often subsidized by the federal government. Once the streets start crumbling down after ~20 years of usage, then the real cost hits. Here's an explanation by Not Just Bikes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35062911,"author":"karmelapple","month":"2023-03","story":"Zero energy ready homes are coming","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why the term Ponzi scheme is used.","quote":"Here is a video talking about why that term is being used","text":"I disagree that it’s naive. Here is a video talking about why that term is being used: [VIDEO] And the Strong Towns write up that has links to more reading that I believe informed that video: [LINK]"},{"id":32432204,"author":"fuoqi","month":"2022-08","story":"To meet demand, US needs 4.3M more apartments by 2035","stance":"rec","why":"Suburbia is inefficient and car-centric.","quote":"Here is a really good video about it","text":"Suburbia is TERRIBLE from long-term efficiency and sustainability point of view. They create car-centric living style which is very hard to get rid off after it settles in. Here is a really good video about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31794713,"author":"Night_Thastus","month":"2022-06","story":"Construction is life","stance":"rec","why":"City design flaws drive construction and repair needs.","quote":"I feel like this video on city growth is relevant, and was pretty interesting","text":"I feel like this video on city growth is relevant, and was pretty interesting: [VIDEO] It suggests that a lot of the construction/repair needs of major cities could be reduced significantly, but it's a flaw inherent to their design that needs correcting first."},{"id":31852982,"author":"btreesOfSpring","month":"2022-06","story":"Investigating the effects of bike lanes in Minneapolis","stance":"rec","why":"Third video covers where the making-ourselves-poor view comes from.","quote":"Take a look if you are curious about where the \"making ourselves poor\" point of view is hatched.","text":"The Strong Town series by Not Just Bikes on YouTube is often linked on HN but the third video[0] in the series covers your question. Take a look if you are curious about where the \"making ourselves poor\" point of view is hatched. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31055762,"author":"diebeforei485","month":"2022-04","story":"Tax the Land","stance":"rec","why":"Playlist on services not charged at full cost.","quote":"There is a whole playlist about this","text":"They don't charge you the full cost of providing those services. There is a whole playlist about this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30728565,"author":"thethethethe","month":"2022-03","story":"US schools can subscribe to an electric bus fleet at lower prices than diesel","stance":"rec","why":"Sprawl drains finances of small cities.","quote":"You could also just watch this smug video","text":"> It would significantly expand taxes How exactly? Do you have any examples? Also, have you ever seen the price of car infrastructure? I would be willing to bet that denser walkable urban infrastructure projects are waaaaay cheaper than building and maintaining sprawling road, sewage, and power transmission networks. Sprawl is seriously draining the finances of many small cities and towns in the US. There's a whole book about it: [LINK] You could also just watch this smug video [VIDEO]"},{"id":30247807,"author":"jabl","month":"2022-02","story":"The End of Uber with Cory Doctorow","stance":"rec","why":"Describes how suburban infrastructure is financed.","quote":"In particular this one describes the financing of suburban infrastructure","text":"Americans seems to abhor car/fuel/etc. taxes to the point that they vote out any politician who dares suggesting anything even approximating that, and as a result they have a huge infrastructure maintenance backlog waiting to pop. Not helped by the fact that suburban sprawl is incredibly expensive to maintain (per the tax revenue the municipality gets) compared to more densely built environments. The \"Not Just Bikes\" youtube channel has a series of videos on the strongtowns, uh, thingy, that goes into more details. In particular this one describes the financing of suburban infrastructure: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29223147,"author":"noahtallen","month":"2021-11","story":"Will real estate ever be normal again?","stance":"rec","why":"Series on sparse areas' infrastructure costs.","quote":"More info, and a very interesting video series here","text":"One comment on this is that less dense areas make it very hard to build infrastructure. For example, if there are 10 families living on a mile stretch of road, think of how much it costs to build a mile of road, along with (possibly) water and sewer there. And then the annual maintenance cost. And then the other road networks require to connect that one road, etc. Those 10 families don’t pay nearly enough in taxes to cover the cost of infrastructure maintenance. It relies on grants from state and federal governments, paid for by taxes in dense cities, to cover the expense. My point: sparse is not objectively better. I think the quality of life we have come to expect in suburbs and rural areas is not possible without the taxes gained from also having dense places. More info, and a very interesting video series here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28310533,"author":"flaque","month":"2021-08","story":"To save California, sacrifice single-family zoning","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of why low-density infrastructure costs exceed tax revenue.","quote":"Here's a good overview of that","text":"This argument is commonly thrown around, but makes no sense in practice. The denser the place, the more efficient the infrastructure use. 50 families in a single apartment building use massively fewer pipes & power lines than 50 families in single family homes. But the tax revenue for the city can still be the same. In a denser area, those families are less likely to own or need cars, and if they do, they drive less. On the other hand, low-density suburban neighborhoods are often so inefficient that the maintenance cost of their infrastructure is more than the tax revenue the residents bring in. (Here's a good overview of that: [VIDEO] ) It's more true to say that \"we don't have the infrastructure for single family homes\" than it is to say that we don't have the infrastructure for apartment buildings."},{"id":34991496,"author":"burlesona","month":"2023-03","story":"Chicago sold rights to 36k parking meters for $1.2B that generate $200M per year","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for excess road building problem.","quote":"","text":"Yes, but, the actual math matters. There are a lot of banal public infrastructure projects that cost much more than the value they create, and there are also theoretically productive public investments that are double-dipping into the same “surplus value.” To illustrate there are many roads that were built in hopes of spurring development and development never occurred, but the roads are maintained anyway at a substantial loss of taxes. And to make a simple hypothetical as you did, suppose the train subsidy creates some surplus, but then the city also runs a bus and a ferry and installs bike lanes all on the same route and all of these are gloriously uncrowded… at this point we’ve probably eaten up all the surplus value and then some. This is hopefully obvious, and yet the excess road building problem is ubiquitous in the US and costing us fortunes.[1] So we can’t just hand-wave and suggest that all infrastructure and all subsidies always create more value than they cost, we have to be critical and especially careful not to pretend that non-financial benefits can pay for real financial costs. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33450122,"author":"Akronymus","month":"2022-11","story":"Court overturns state ruling on San Francisco’s infamous 469 Stevenson project","stance":"reference","why":"Car-dependent places result in unsustainable neighbourhoods.","quote":"","text":"> Likewise, most places are completely car-dependent. This too is intentionally exclusionary. Which also results in unsustainable neighbourhoods. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32448948,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-08","story":"The Bay Area's lost streetcars","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of suburbs falling into disrepair.","quote":"","text":"> […] why didnt the roads collapse into a state of disrepair which no one was willing to pay for? Suburbs are slowing falling into a state of disrepair, or at least more and more budget being taken away form other things: * [LINK] * [VIDEO] * [LINK]"},{"id":31851586,"author":"bjustin","month":"2022-06","story":"Investigating the effects of bike lanes in Minneapolis","stance":"reference","why":"Large road networks are not financially sustainable.","quote":"Large networks of large roads are not financially sustainable [1].","text":"Large networks of large roads are not financially sustainable [1]. I can only assume this is more the case the more rural you get. Asking for more of the current American style of car infrastructure may not even work, depending on the specifics. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31534037,"author":"foepys","month":"2022-05","story":"A city fights back against heavyweight cars","stance":"reference","why":"Suburban house buying as Ponzi-esque scheme.","quote":"","text":"It will be interesting how the American suburbs will fare in the upcoming years. With interests rising and home buyers getting more careful, the Ponzi-esque scheme of buying a house in a suburb might collapse [1, 2]. American culture is partly unthinkable without cars because of planning and zoning failures made decades ago. Why don't have suburbs a lot of small super markets and other stores? Why does everybody need to drive miles and miles to get a gallon of milk? 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27719860,"author":"ajmadesc","month":"2021-07","story":"Could miniature forests help air-condition cities?","stance":"reference","why":"Suburban lifestyle depends on subsidies.","quote":"","text":"Oh but you can! Paris, London, and I'm sure countless other cities in Europe have pedestrianized or reduced traffic in large areas. The Netherlands has done this in most cities. 99.99% of suburbanites couldn't afford their lifestyle if it weren't for obsurd gov subsidies of infrastructure / petrol industry. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["CnMRePtHMZY",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["oOttvpjJvAo",1],["ciStnd9Y2ak",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["s9aCwCKgkLo",1],["LnzuMJLZRdU",1],["XfQUOHlAocY",1],["yY96hTb8WgI",1],["G-J86Ka9MkQ",1],["7G4ipM2qjfw",1],["8X7Ng75e5gQ",1],["akVL7QY0S8A",1],["F1Hq8eVOMHs",1],["2lisQQmWQkY",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1],["M8F5hXqS-Ac",1],["oHlpmxLTxpw",1]]},"LJ4W1g-6JiY":{"wy":"HN commenters post it under nearly every fusion breakthrough story as the standard reality check on Q-factor hype.","q":{"x":"Mandatory video by Sabine Hossenfelder","i":33954009,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33954009,"author":"amai","month":"2022-12","story":"US scientists boost clean power hopes with fusion energy breakthrough","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mandatory context on Q plasma versus Q total.","quote":"Mandatory video by Sabine Hossenfelder","text":"Mandatory video by Sabine Hossenfelder: [VIDEO] So they probably are talking again about Q_plasma, not Q_total ."},{"id":33976488,"author":"supergirl","month":"2022-12","story":"US Department of Energy: Fusion Ignition Achieved","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Puts the fusion ignition announcement in perspective against total system energy.","quote":"everyone should watch to put this into perspective.","text":"everyone should watch [VIDEO] to put this into perspective. for some reason, all the news articles are extremely misleading. previous output ratio for this fusion method was something like 70%, now it is 150%. it's a useful improvement, but not a major breakthrough. the whole system still consumes 100x more energy than it produces. 100MJ of energy is needed to power the laser. the laser generates only 2MJ of energy that powers fusion. fusion generates 3MJ of output energy. so all the articles are saying \"they put 2MJ energy and got 3MJ energy back\". no, they put 102MJ and got 3MJ."},{"id":30273453,"author":"limaoscarjuliet","month":"2022-02","story":"Major breakthrough on nuclear fusion energy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why Q total, not Q plasma, matters and is still below one.","quote":"After watching Sabine's video, I'm not so optimistic anymore.","text":"According to Sabine Hossenfelder, published numbers are frequently mis-stated on purpose. When you look at frequently reported Q Plasma (or plasma efficiency) we are not that far from it being > 1. However, we should look at Q Total (total efficiency), which is still way below < 1, even in the best plans. [VIDEO] For years, I was hoping fusion is close. After watching Sabine's video, I'm not so optimistic anymore."},{"id":29345233,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2021-11","story":"The chase for fusion energy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how Q factors mislead about ITER and net power.","quote":"All clearly explained in this great video.","text":"It’s true that ITER will not produce net power and that the administrators have misled the public about the difference in Q factors. You can have a reactor which produces more thermal power than you put in to it that is still a net power drain when you factor in the rest of the plant operations to keep it going. All clearly explained in this great video. [VIDEO] There is a real risk that by focusing on the wrong Q factor you sink all your budget in to plants which can never work as a real power plant. Still I am hopeful that ITER will be sufficiently valuable as a research system that we do move closer to commercial fusion power."},{"id":28840991,"author":"lisper","month":"2021-10","story":"Major nuclear fusion milestone reached as ‘ignition’ triggered in a lab","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why ignition results should not cause excitement about fusion power.","quote":"Here [1] is an excellent video by Sabine Hossenfelder about why you should not get too excited about this result.","text":"Neither. From TFA: \"While the latest experiment still required more energy in than it got out, it is the first suspected to reach the crucial stage of ‘ignition’, which allowed considerably more energy to be produced than ever before, and paves the way for ‘break even’, where the energy in is matched by the energy out.\" Here [1] is an excellent video by Sabine Hossenfelder about why you should not get too excited about this result. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33960249,"author":"mbar84","month":"2022-12","story":"U.S. to announce major breakthrough in zero-carbon nuclear fusion energy","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why fusion energy gain announcements can be misleading.","quote":"Based on the video by Sabine Hossenfelder, my guess would be, the announcement references Q-plasma.","text":"The question is, in terms of energy input, are we talking about Q-total or Q-plasma? Based on the video by Sabine Hossenfelder, my guess would be, the announcement references Q-plasma. That would mean that the announcement is a motte-and-baily miscommunication, as it promises much more than people are lead to read into it. Motte: We got more energy out than we put in. Bailey: Oh, what we really meant is, more energy was produced, than was put into the plasma, though we didn't actually capture any energy and also, there was actually a great deal of energy also needed to run all the ancillary systems to keep the plasma confined. How close is nuclear fusion power? [VIDEO]"},{"id":33969844,"author":"rogerallen","month":"2022-12","story":"DOE SEC. Granholm to Announce Major Fusion Energy Breakthrough 10:00 Am EST","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the difference between Qplasma and Qtotal.","quote":"explains the difference between Qplasma and Qtotal.","text":"[VIDEO] explains the difference between Qplasma and Qtotal. I hope this presentation will be as forthright."},{"id":32463054,"author":"planck01","month":"2022-08","story":"Three papers highlight results of record yield nuclear fusion shot","stance":"rec","why":"Reality check that fusion claims are wildly optimistic.","quote":"I usually get my reality checks from Sabine Hossenfelder, who a while back explained that all these fusion claims are wildly optimistic.","text":"I hope you win! I usually get my reality checks from Sabine Hossenfelder, who a while back explained that all these fusion claims are wildly optimistic. You can find her video here: [VIDEO] I am no scientist, so it is hard for me to know if team optimistic or team pessimistic is right. But even if it is the latter, I think we should put more money and research on it!"},{"id":30851595,"author":"chii","month":"2022-03","story":"HB11's hydrogen-boron laser fusion test yields groundbreaking results","stance":"rec","why":"Details how fusion energy reporting gets done by media and industry.","quote":"This video details some of the ways that nuclear fusion energy reporting gets done","text":"This video details some of the ways that nuclear fusion energy reporting gets done (both media, but also the industry) : [VIDEO] We are not near a viable fusion reactor yet. Not close."},{"id":30451311,"author":"jetru","month":"2022-02","story":"Nuclear-fusion reactor doubles record for energy made from fusing atoms","stance":"rec","why":"Clearly explains why many fusion research claims are dubious, focusing on ITER and JET.","quote":"This video by Sabine clearly explains why a lot of these fusion research claims are dubious with a focus on ITER and JET","text":"Skeptical. This video by Sabine clearly explains why a lot of these fusion research claims are dubious with a focus on ITER and JET: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30089037,"author":"nuccy","month":"2022-01","story":"Burning plasma achieved in inertial fusion","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent overview of misleading Q terminology in fusion research.","quote":"There is quite some misleading terminology being used in the field of nuclear fusion (see excellent overview here [1]).","text":"There is quite some misleading terminology being used in the field of nuclear fusion (see excellent overview here [1]). So I was genuinely curious what kind of Q is used in this paper, so here it is: > In this regime, self-heating from α-particle deposition exceeds the external heating input into the DT8; this ratio is denoted Qα, where the self-heating is taken relative to the heating power to the plasma—for inertial fusion this is the PdV compressional work on the fuel and not the total laser energy (P, pressure, dV, volume change). Qα > 1 is a burning plasma. So as far as I understand such Qα > 1 can be reached without sustaining neither pressure or volume, so one can think about it as an explosion rather that intuitive burning (somewhat continuous process). To summarize: Q_total = total_energy_out / total_energy_in is what matters, not any Q related to plasma energy. Since to build a power plant we just care what it consumes in comparison with what it produces. For this particular case even though Qα > 1 you likely can extract just a tiny fraction of that energy stored in plasma, while spending huge amounts of energy to heat it in the first place. 1. [VIDEO] or as an article [LINK] Edit: I'm a big proponent of nuclear fission and fusion since both are the best (current and future) way of getting carbon neutral energy, which can cover the spikes in the grid and sun/wind-less days. It is just important to be careful with the terminology, which can be used to make research r"},{"id":29121815,"author":"jahnu","month":"2021-11","story":"Helion","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the difference between Q plasma and net energy gain.","quote":"Sabine Hossenfelders explains here","text":"Can anyone tell me if this is a net + like the lay person would understand it or something like the net + qplasma thing often misleadingly used when talking about tokamaks? Sabine Hossenfelders explains here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29125800,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2021-11","story":"Helion","stance":"rec","why":"Tempers expectations about fusion power timelines.","quote":"This video by Sabine Hossenfelder has tempered my expectations.","text":"This video by Sabine Hossenfelder has tempered my expectations. How close is nuclear fusion power? [VIDEO]"},{"id":28774439,"author":"tylermauthe","month":"2021-10","story":"Can Nuclear Fusion Put the Brakes on Climate Change?","stance":"rec","why":"Sabine Hossenfelder explains why fusion power is further away than hyped.","quote":"Sabine Hossenfelder has a great video [1] on this topic.","text":"The claims on nuclear fission being just around the corner are wildly exaggerated. Sabine Hossenfelder has a great video [1] on this topic. I want fusion to be real too, but focussing on small scale fission and solar is probably a better idea. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":28841128,"author":"vfclists","month":"2021-10","story":"Major nuclear fusion milestone reached as ‘ignition’ triggered in a lab","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks fusion power hype.","quote":"Dr Sabine Hossenfelder's debunking and deflate the fusion power hype","text":"Funny how this story gets posted after soon after Dr Sabine Hossenfelder's debunking and deflate the fusion power hype - [VIDEO] with a genuine Hans Gruber accent :)"},{"id":30091089,"author":"belter","month":"2022-01","story":"Burning plasma achieved in inertial fusion","stance":"reference","why":"Points to a specific moment in the video to question an approximate calculation.","quote":"do you mind commenting briefly on the issue raised here: (At correct time)","text":"Read the references you provided, but do you mind commenting briefly on the issue raised here: (At correct time) [VIDEO] Edit: More specifically, would you argue the approximate calculation here is incorrect? (At correct time) [VIDEO]"},{"id":29686057,"author":"david_draco","month":"2021-12","story":"After years of doubts, hopes grow that nuclear fusion is near","stance":"reference","why":"Argues fusion PR omits true full-system efficiency.","quote":"Sabine Hossenfelder essentially called the nuclear fusion PR a fraud, because they do not report true (full-system) efficiencies.","text":"Sabine Hossenfelder essentially called the nuclear fusion PR a fraud, because they do not report true (full-system) efficiencies. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29213461,"author":"gabrielblack","month":"2021-11","story":"Nuclear fusion is close enough to start dreaming","stance":"reference","why":"Used to frame Q plasma versus Q total when judging Helion claims.","quote":"there are no words about Q plasma or Q total","text":"I read then link to the other article explaining the new technologies developed by Helion that should permit commercially viable fusion reactors, there are no words about Q plasma or Q total: [VIDEO] so the affirmation that \"Nuclear Fusion Is Close Enough to Start Dreaming\" could be other speculation of the press not supported by facts. Obviously I hope to be wrong."},{"id":28832164,"author":"david-gpu","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: What excites you today (technologically speaking)?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how the meaning of breakeven changes how close fusion appears.","quote":"Sabine Hossenfelder recently explained why breakeven fission is much further away than the public generally believes [0].","text":"Sabine Hossenfelder recently explained why breakeven fission is much further away than the public generally believes [0]. Without spoiling her explanation, it boils down to how you measure \"breakeven\". [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28846298,"author":"tuatoru","month":"2021-10","story":"Major nuclear fusion milestone reached as ‘ignition’ triggered in a lab","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a source for fusion Q-factor requirements.","quote":"Sabine Hossenfelder, How Close Is Nuclear Fusion? ,","text":"The mininum viable Qplasma would be in the neighborhood of 100.[1] Fusion may get competitive for electricity generation with a Qtotal > 500.[2] 1. Sabine Hossenfelder, How Close Is Nuclear Fusion? , [VIDEO] 2. Nicholas Hawker, A simplified economic model for inertial fusion , [LINK]"}],"r":[["uxykI30fS54",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["EpRrD58Ah3Q",1],["KkpqA8yG9T4",1],["zb7Bs98KmnY",1],["-s5szfPYKY4",1],["YSfCdBBqNXY",1],["CM0aohBfUTc",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["hx7BXih7zx8",1],["tTBWfkE7BXU",1],["OmJ-4B-mS-Y",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["S9JGmA5_unY",1],["vBiIDwBOqQA",1],["dW8Cy6WrO94",1],["cTsEzmFamZ8",1],["8S3B8a8N83k",1],["ZuvK-od647c",1]]},"prXuyMCgbTc":{"wy":"It is the go-to talk for anyone building falling-sand or pixel-physics simulations, and several commenters say they modeled their own engines on it.","q":{"x":"Highly recommended if you want to dive deeper into the topic.","i":31311072,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":49241865,"author":"hopfog","month":"2026-08","story":"Ask HN: What are you working on? (August 2026)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk instrumental in building the commenter's engine.","quote":"this talk by the Noita devs has been instrumental when building my engine","text":"Thank you! It's funny because I really wanted to introduce more things like that, but the very simple things I've added so far have received a bit of pushback (\"I want to play a factory game, not solve puzzles!\"). So now I'm reconsidering it, but I think there's a lot of room for really interesting Noita-style secrets when combined with the automation and processing tools. Overall it's a much simpler and more controlled experience than Noita though, so don't expect anything like it. By the way, this talk by the Noita devs has been instrumental when building my engine: [VIDEO] Highly recommended!"},{"id":40130294,"author":"hopfog","month":"2024-04","story":"Web Game Engines and Libraries","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic GDC talk used as architecture model.","quote":"I've modeled my architecture after this fantastic GDC talk by the Noita devs","text":"Thank you! If you haven't played Noita it's basically a \"falling sand\" or powder physics game where every pixel is simulated. You need a special cellular automata that is not your typical game physics engine, so I don't think Babylon.js would be a good choice but I may be wrong. I've modeled my architecture after this fantastic GDC talk by the Noita devs: [VIDEO] (\"Exploring the Tech and Design of Noita\")"},{"id":31311072,"author":"panzerboiler","month":"2022-05","story":"Making a falling sand simulator","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the Falling Everything Engine behind Noita.","quote":"Highly recommended if you want to dive deeper into the topic.","text":"Nice work, Jason. I enjoyed a lot a talk [1] at GDC by Petri Purho (Nolla Games and before that Kloonigames) that explains the \"Falling Everything Engine\" used in their game Noita (that I would love to play on my mac...). Highly recommended if you want to dive deeper into the topic. [1]: [VIDEO] \"Exploring the Tech and Design of Noita\""},{"id":46984509,"author":"thezipcreator","month":"2026-02","story":"Sandboxels","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting GDC talk about Noita.","quote":"I found this GDC talk about Noita pretty interesting","text":"this doesn't answer any questions about this game, but I found this GDC talk about Noita pretty interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31312859,"author":"bo0tzz","month":"2022-05","story":"Making a falling sand simulator","stance":"rec","why":"Creator explains Noita's tech, including multithreading from 9:20.","quote":"Here's a talk from the creator of Noita about the underlying tech.","text":"Here's a talk from the creator of Noita about the underlying tech. From 9:20 he talks about their multithreading implementation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27735458,"author":"Impossible","month":"2021-07","story":"The Powder Toy","stance":"rec","why":"Shows early prototypes and challenges of building in a falling-sand game.","quote":"If you haven't seen the GDC 2019 talk about tech and design of Noita I recommend checking it out.","text":"The Noita team explored some game ideas along those lines and had challenges. If you haven't seen the GDC 2019 talk about tech and design of Noita I recommend checking it out. They show some early prototypes focused on building [VIDEO]"},{"id":25240404,"author":"zubspace","month":"2020-11","story":"30 years later, QBasic is still the best","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Noita starting with QBasic physics simulations.","quote":"If you like a small challenge, watch a few minutes of this video about a game called Noita","text":"I owe Basic a lot. It got me into programming on our first family pc, a Compaq Presario around '94. QBasic was accessible on every windows installation from the command prompt. You could read the programming guide built into the editor and have your first running code shortly after. That was its true power. One of my first programs was teaching me french words, asking me to translate them in a random order until I got them all right. It was truly magical. I also made pixels move, trying to write games, but I quickly ran into limitations and problems organizing my code. If you like a small challenge, watch a few minutes of this video about a game called Noita, where in the beginning Q-Basic was used to create some simple physic simulations [1]. It was hacky yes, but I think if you're starting out, it's much more important to get something running quickly than learning how to do it the right way. When people speak of basic, they always seem to have the impression that you're spoiled for life after touching it, but I think that's wrong. You can always learn more and improve. It's breaking down barriers of entry, which is key. A simple editor, with an inbuilt help page and a run button is all you need. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22644216,"author":"endgame","month":"2020-03","story":"Lemmings 2: The Tribes","stance":"rec","why":"Good GDC talk on Noita's terrain deformation tech.","quote":"this is a good GDC talk about how it does so","text":"Noita does interesting things with terrain deformation, and this is a good GDC talk about how it does so: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41809562,"author":"itishappy","month":"2024-10","story":"Game Programming in Prolog","stance":"reference","why":"Noita described as a pixel-level chemistry engine game.","quote":"Noita is a game based around the concept of a chemistry engine running at the pixel level.","text":"Noita is a game based around the concept of a chemistry engine running at the pixel level. It uses a custom rules engine written in C++. It's one of the more interesting games I have ever played. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33747155,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2022-11","story":"Porting 58k lines of D and C++ to Jai","stance":"reference","why":"Noita cited as technically complex custom-engine game.","quote":"","text":"jai's author, Jonathan Blow, always creates a game from scratch. His games: Braid (highest rated XBox Live title when released, \"Xbox Live Arcade Game of the Year\", [LINK] ) and Witness (while not winning significant awards, regularly appears in Best of Decade games lists, [LINK] ). Besides that there are multiple games where people use their own engines: - Possibly among some of the most technically complex ones are Factorio (e.g. [LINK] ) and Noita (where every single pixel in the humongous world is simulated [VIDEO] ) - Among the AAA-level crowd there's Decima (Death Stranding [LINK] and Horizon Zero Dawn [LINK] ) and REDEngine (Witcher 3 [LINK] , Cyberpunk 2077 [LINK] ) - There are smaller ones like Monkey X (Crypt of the Necrodancer, [LINK] ) - There are bigger ones like Clausewitz (basically all of Paradox Interactive's games like Crusader Kings [LINK] and Stellaris [LINK] ) In general, once you know what you're doing it sometimes pays to develop your own engine that is specific to what you're doing. Unity and Unreal are generalist enines that you still to bend to your will to do what you need to do and may have opinionated setups of things that are hard to work around, or maybe are omitted from the engine, or just plain don't allow you to do."}],"r":[["W4SxKWwFhA0",1],["Uo08uS904Rg",1],["k1BneeJTDcU",1],["sG9UMMq2dz4",1],["rFejpH_tAHM",1],["TPpoJGYlW54",1],["s8nFqwOho-s",1],["87jfTIWosBw",1],["u9KNtnCZDMI",1],["-gPuH1yeZ08",1],["JWD1Fpdd4Pc",1],["W3aieHjyNvw",1]]},"MvgN5gCuLac":{"wy":"HN keeps linking it in threads on clutter, storage units, real estate and consumerism as a perfectly apt comic take.","q":{"x":"George Carlin put it quite eloquently","i":17283704,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":41757691,"author":"roland35","month":"2024-10","story":"So thieves broke into your storage unit again","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin's bit on accumulating stuff","quote":"George Carlin had a great bit on stuff","text":"What a story! Most people probably would just give up. Dealing with storage units is why I try to eliminate all the extra \"stuff\" in my life... George Carlin had a great bit on stuff: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23903761,"author":"faster","month":"2020-07","story":"Designing a New Old Home: Part 1","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin routine on houses as piles of stuff","quote":"George Carlin did a great routine on 'stuff'.","text":"George Carlin did a great routine on 'stuff'. [VIDEO] \"a house is ... a pile of stuff with a cover on it\"."},{"id":18252965,"author":"twiceaday","month":"2018-10","story":"Microplastics found in 90 percent of table salt","stance":"rec","why":"Older Carlin material with a lighter tone","quote":"If you are interested, check out his older stuff like Carlin on Campus.","text":"For what its worth, you are describing Carlins newer specials. His career spanned over 40 years. And yeah look at the shift in tone just from the names of his HBO specials 1977 On Location: George Carlin at USC 1978 George Carlin: Again! 1982 Carlin at Carnegie 1984 Carlin on Campus 1986 Playin' with Your Head 1988 What Am I Doing in New Jersey? 1990 Doin' It Again 1992 Jammin' in New York 1996 Back in Town ------------------------- 1999 You Are All Diseased 2001 Complaints and Grievances 2005 Life Is Worth Losing 2008 It's Bad for Ya If you are interested, check out his older stuff like Carlin on Campus. \"A Place for My Stuff\" [1] for example. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17283704,"author":"rashomon","month":"2018-06","story":"Toys ‘R’ Us Didn’t Have to Die","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin eloquently skewers consumer stuff culture","quote":"George Carlin put it quite eloquently","text":"Any source on that theory? I think you have something solid there. George Carlin put it quite eloquently: [VIDEO] We're definitely seeing a resurgence to simplicity after advertisers had performed scorched-earth on frontal cortex lobes."},{"id":11162761,"author":"cfallin","month":"2016-02","story":"Why I don't want stuff","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin's commentary on American attachment to possessions","quote":"Brilliant commentary on the American way, as always","text":"> \"you don't own stuff; the stuff owns you\" Reminds me of the George Carlin bit on \"stuff\". Brilliant commentary on the American way, as always: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6588781,"author":"adolph","month":"2013-10","story":"How to get rid of old stuff, sell it for more, and use Amazon as cheap storage","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin's classic routine on stuff","quote":"a link I think worthy of a listen","text":"If you haven't seen it, a link I think worthy of a listen, George Carlin Talks About \"Stuff\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":1709889,"author":"saurabh","month":"2010-09","story":"Ask HN: What's the big deal with startups anyways...","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"If you have some free time, do watch this.","text":"Have you noticed that their stuff is shit and your shit is stuff? - George Carlin If you have some free time, do watch this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47916784,"author":"Barrin92","month":"2026-04","story":"I Bought Friendster for $30k – Here's What I'm Doing with It","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">It can be argued that humans actually hate forgetting things I agree with you, I don't even think that needs to be argued, we without a doubt hate forgetting things, but we also hate eating our vegetables. We do hate a lot of things we probably shouldn't. We are perpetual hoarders, as a species we have the bad habit that we're not very grateful for the problems we don't have as a consequence of things we don't keep. We're not very good thinking in terms of absence. That's why Marie Kondo sold a ton of books and got a great Netflix deal simply by teaching people how to throw stuff into the garbage. Civilization is great at record keeping but not doing too well on the social bonding front, or in the words of George Carlin: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34890326,"author":"surprisetalk","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: How to protect blog from AI search?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is an extremely difficult question that I've been grappling with myself. My first strategy is to create writings that can't be summarized. I try to make essays that are dripping with idiosyncrasy. I can't win in an information war, but I can stay competitive for ~10 years in a creative clash: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] I myself draw lots of my inspiration from my \"sages of style\", in the same way that AIs will try to replicate my voice. None of these great thinkers can be summarized or replicated in any meaningful way: [6] David Foster Wallace: [LINK] [7] George Carlin: [VIDEO] [8] Derek Sivers: [LINK] [9] John Green: [LINK] [10] Roman Mars: [LINK] [11] Alan Kay: [LINK] [12] Alan Watts: [LINK] [13] Richard Feynman: [LINK] [14] Raymond Smullyan: [LINK] But style only takes you so far. My second strategy is to socialize IRL. This year, I'm starting a tech conference called \"Outland\" to foster community in an age where community might be all that matters. [15] [LINK]"},{"id":13555416,"author":"hsitz","month":"2017-02","story":"How Children Lost the Right to Roam in Just 4 Generations","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"this conversation reminded me of a George Carlin routine","text":"Well, people buy diamonds and they're basically useless, other than signalling that the purchaser has a certain amount of money. The price for them is much inflated over what it would be if priced purely for their practical value. Artificial diamonds are identical to the naked eye, yet the diamond industry somehow influences our culture to make people think they need to spend many thousands of dollars on useless \"real\" diamonds. On a (somewhat) related note, this conversation reminded me of a George Carlin routine: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10907728,"author":"KannO","month":"2016-01","story":"Stop Buying Real Estate in SF","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Evolve the idea of what a home is. Elio motors is offering a small 84MPG vehicle for $6,800. 30 year old Volkswagen Westfalia Campers maintain a high resale value to this very day. Why are all mobile homes so huge? How much money do people spend on rent, leases, mortgages, and buying homes? I'm sure college students would love a compact mobile home vehicle. Developing countries would likely eat such a thing up. The CEO of Zappos lives in a trailer park of Airstream trailers and Tumbleweed tiny mobile houses with a shared kitchen, living room and other common areas designed to encourage a true neighborhood / community. Or just consider a house as infrastructure. Real estate is assumed to be limited, but that's mostly the result of nimbyism and the gaming of developments sold off on the real estate market as \"investments\". The whole trend of gentrification will likely play out in less expensive regions of North America and developing countries, and presumably there will be large dense affordable housing somewhere someday and these areas tend to be fertile for creative businesses and cultural experimentation. New York is long stagnate, and it's amazing how much San Fransisco suffers from real estate leaching off of the working population and tech economy. A home isn't limited to being a house. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16711779,"author":"icebraining","month":"2018-03","story":"How warehouses for personal junk became a $38B industry","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Have you noticed that their stuff is shit and your shit is stuff?","text":"\"Have you noticed that their stuff is shit and your shit is stuff?\" George Carlin on Stuff: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8940273,"author":"carlio","month":"2015-01","story":"The hazards of too much stuff","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"George Carlin on stuff: [VIDEO] Dylan Moran on stuff: [LINK] I now feel obligated to contribute more to the conversation than some YouTube videos. I've been a kind of move-around-a-lot person for about 6 years now. I've lived in 4 cities. I guarantee you, if it's going to cost several thousand dollars to move your stuff, it's much easier to purge. I'm also glad that moving forced me to have a purge occasionally. I think you'd be surprised how many things you hold onto just in case. I've had a rare pang of lost nostalgia, but in general, I haven't missed anything."},{"id":4389178,"author":"zio99","month":"2012-08","story":"You Probably Have Too Much Stuff","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"A house is just a place to keep your stuff while you go out and get more stuff.","text":"There's a good bit on that: George Carlin: [VIDEO] \"A house is just a place to keep your stuff while you go out and get more stuff.\""}],"r":[["04ERSb06dOg",1],["oAHbLRjF0vo",1],["J9nv0Ol-R5Q",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["aY985qzn7oI",1],["YdOXS_9_P4U",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["y7h4OtFDnYE",1],["QEIUdMiColU",1]]},"oHf1vD5_b5I":{"wy":"HN brings it up in any thread about elevators or physical security as a fascinating, entertaining deep dive.","q":{"x":"If you liked the original post, you will probably LOVE this DEFCON talk about elevator hacking","i":35153613,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":49125299,"author":"cruffle_duffle","month":"2026-07","story":"Elevators","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Seminal talk covering many elevator modes","quote":"I watch it like once a year because it always tickles some part of me.","text":"If you like elevator hacking don’t forget the seminal DEF CON talk by Deviant Ollam and Howard Payne: [VIDEO] I watch it like once a year because it always tickles some part of me. Like all the different modes you can get an elevator into. The most fancy one people might encounter is when moving into or out of a building. The front office can give you a key to give exclusive control over an elevator so your movers aren’t waiting around on elevators. Put it in that mode and it will stop responding to calls from other floors. Only the person with the key can control the elevator. You get on, select the floor, door closes elevator goes, and then just chills there with the door open waiting for you. Annoying for the rest if the building (the building is down an elevator when in that mode) but is amazing for the person using it! But there are way, way more depending on the installation and function. Fun fact: most elevator shafts are sealed at the top as tight as possible to prevent them from becoming a giant chimney in a fire. It never even occurred to me until I was in a mechanical room wondering “where is the hatch to look down the shaft?” The answer is “there is none, and it’s a feature not a bug.” You want to block all airflow so fire doesn’t chase up the shaft into neighboring floors. Who knew!"},{"id":35153613,"author":"alexpotato","month":"2023-03","story":"The Door Close Button","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating details on elevators and their operating modes","quote":"If you liked the original post, you will probably LOVE this DEFCON talk about elevator hacking","text":"If you liked the original post, you will probably LOVE this DEFCON talk about elevator hacking: [VIDEO] It also gives a lot of fascinating information about elevators in general and specific operation modes. e.g. there is a \"riot mode\" where the elevator goes to every floor except the first (or ground in EU) floor aka to the lobby. The idea was that, during a riot, a luxury building in NYC would lock the stairwell access from the lobby but people could still take the elevator to the other floors to visit their neighbors etc"},{"id":47266675,"author":"simlevesque","month":"2026-03","story":"Let's Get Physical","stance":"rec","why":"Pentesting stories about elevator security","quote":"It reminded me of Deviant Ollam's stories such has his elevator security talk w/ Howard Payne","text":"I love pentesting stories. Great blog post, I was smiling while reading most of it. It reminded me of Deviant Ollam's stories such has his elevator security talk w/ Howard Payne: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27778782,"author":"lozf","month":"2021-07","story":"List of Special Elevator Modes","stance":"rec","why":"Great Def Con talk on elevator hacking","quote":"Deviant Ollam & Howard Payne did a great talk on Elevator Hacking at Def Con 22.","text":"Deviant Ollam & Howard Payne did a great talk on Elevator Hacking at Def Con 22. You can catch it at: [LINK] or [VIDEO] [01:00:15]"},{"id":18741370,"author":"lathiat","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"rec","why":"Deviant Ollam elevator talk suggested among best talks","quote":"Also not 2018 but his elevator with Howard Payne talk: (1h version)","text":"Deviant Ollam - The Hotel Room Gourmet [LINK] Also not 2018 but his elevator with Howard Payne talk: [VIDEO] (1h version) [LINK] (2h version) And the search for the perfect door: [LINK]"},{"id":15198605,"author":"lathiat","month":"2017-09","story":"Evan Doorbell's Classic Phreaking Tapes","stance":"rec","why":"Related Deviant Ollam talk on physical access","quote":"This is a good related talk - goes outside the box on all the ways you can often get into commercial buildings","text":"I realise the thread is phone phreaking related and that was kind of the point of your post; but in the spirit of the talk below to think more outside the box... There's a pretty good chance in many cases you can just open the external box with some easy to get (and relatively standard) key. And hit a button. Related: [LINK] This is a good related talk - goes outside the box on all the ways you can often get into commercial buildings without picking (or even in many cases, interacting with) the lock \"The Search for the Perfect Door\" by Deviant Ollam [LINK] Also good: [LINK] [Howard Payne & Deviant Ollam - This Key is Your Key, This Key is My Key] [VIDEO] [DEF CON 22 - Deviant Ollam & Howard Payne - Elevator Hacking - From the Pit to the Penthouse]"},{"id":43996281,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2025-05","story":"Malicious compliance by booking an available meeting room","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies the funny DefCon elevator video","quote":"is Deviant Ollam's talk on elevators.","text":"> funny DefCon video on elevator hacking For those wondering, is Deviant Ollam's talk on elevators. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29369045,"author":"Twisol","month":"2021-11","story":"Saintcon: Lock Picking Lawyer Keynote","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for elevator security weakness","quote":"let's not talk about elevator security. [0]","text":"> You can break a window open or just find an unlocked door if you are looking to do some bad shit. > A higher security lock on your home isn’t going to make your flimsy door harder to kick down, or your window harder to break From the keynote, that's why LPL puts a heavy focus on bike locks, gun safe locks, etc. The audiences for those locks have a more vested interest in physical security than mere \"social conventions\". A well-locked bike makes it more difficult for a thief to get all the / enough value from the target. A well-locked gun safe prevents accidents and saves lives. Also, I live in an apartment on an upper floor. No accessible windows. The only viable way into my residence is through the front door. (There are like two RFID-gated doors before mine, but tailgating renders them pretty ineffectual, and let's not talk about elevator security. [0]) It's not worth it for me to put a better lock on my door, but I'm also not kidding myself about its effectiveness. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11746607,"author":"DyslexicAtheist","month":"2016-05","story":"Reverse Engineering a Mysterious UDP Stream in My Hotel","stance":"reference","why":"Elevator hacking talk on whether elevators can be controlled remotely","quote":"","text":"yeah and deliver some badass mixes of The girl from Ipanema. :-) Though I wonder if he had time to look at the packets long or careful enough. Would have been interesting to inspect all these devices closer too. Were there also other sessions established maybe that could hint at controlling them? E.g. such as volume of the sound? I doubt that the actual elevator would be controlled could be controlled remotely: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rufnWLVQcKg",1],["nJPERZDfyWc",1],["wfYbgdo8e-8",1],["X9RYuvPCQUA",1],["8dhp_20j0Ys",1],["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["NmGaXEmfTIo",1]]},"Q1lL-hXO27Q":{"wy":"HN returns to it as a model of intellectual honesty and good explanation, often linking it in discussions of why-questions and science communication.","q":{"x":"his riff for 8 minutes on this is just a joy to watch.","i":37936452,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46981267,"author":"rimunroe","month":"2026-02","story":"Who Smeared Feynman","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman's famous interview answering why magnets do what they do.","quote":"if anyone else missed it I highly recommend it","text":"I linked the \"why do magnets do that?\" interview elsewhere in the comments, but if anyone else missed it I highly recommend it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41628851,"author":"itishappy","month":"2024-09","story":"What Is a Particle? (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman describing the problem of explaining what things are made of.","quote":"one of my favorite physics videos of all time","text":"I disagree! I think fields are very intuitive and it's everything else that's nonsensical! Magnetism isn't made of anything. It is a field, and it's one your can interact with directly. Compare that with, say, table salt. \"What's table salt made of?\" Uh. It's made of atoms entirely unrelated to salt, like sodium and chlorine. \"What are atoms made of?\" Uh. They're made of tiny electric particles zipping around at relativistic speeds but bound together by forces like magnetism. Richard Feynman does a better job than me of describing this exact problem in one of my favorite physics videos of all time: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37936452,"author":"dimator","month":"2023-10","story":"Richard Feynman explains neutron stars","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman's mesmerizing riff on why magnets are hard to explain without context.","quote":"his riff for 8 minutes on this is just a joy to watch.","text":"i can't help but tack on my favorite Feynman video, on magnets: [VIDEO] i'm just mesmerized by his answer, that without an understood context, it's not at all easy to explain how magnets work. his riff for 8 minutes on this is just a joy to watch."},{"id":40100596,"author":"lisper","month":"2024-04","story":"Daniel Dennett has died","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman on why-questions and limits of explanation in physics.","quote":"You should watch this","text":"> But the relationship between brain chemistry and consciousness is at best correlating certain conscious states with certain neurological states. How ironic that you would say that in a thread about Daniel Dennett's passing. You have obviously not read \"Consciousness Explained.\" > And QFT explaining laws of nature would be strange considering QFT is included in the laws of nature. You should watch this: [VIDEO] > there are philosophical arguments as to why there must be an ultimate, necessary explanation Yes, obviously. There are arguments for why the earth must be flat too. Why should I care about either? They're just wrong ."},{"id":28348992,"author":"RichardCA","month":"2021-08","story":"NP Complete 3rd Grade Math Problems (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman's careful take on explaining things correctly.","quote":"Feynman was fanatical about correct pedagogy. This is always good","text":"It's an easy mistake to make, to have incorrect pedagogy around the concept of temperature, and Feynman was fanatical about correct pedagogy. This is always good: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49041416,"author":"Jach","month":"2026-07","story":"Fields Medals 2026","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman refusing to give a false simplified explanation of magnets.","quote":"Feynman's refusal to lie about the nature of magnets","text":"At least you are aware of the lies. I'm reminded of Feynman's refusal to lie about the nature of magnets ( [VIDEO] ): \"You'd soon ask me about the nature of the [rubber] bands.\" Even in his digression about \"why\" questions, in giving a very high level reason for why ice is slippery he adds \"they say \", since I suspect he knew at the time the usual explanation had problems, and only recently do we have better explanations ( [LINK] ). But at the same time, I've thought, it can be fun to try and come up with nice sounding lies, or analogies, or oversimplifications, especially for some types of people who won't leave you alone about something until they think they've understood something (even if a lie), or who want something repeatable to tell their friends about so they can brag about you. Still I think honesty is a better policy, even if it's not as satisfying, and if departed from at least making sure both sides know there are convenient lies and oversimplifications. As a student I would be (and have been) very put out if the goal is to understand something on a technical level and the teacher starts with lies."},{"id":46977572,"author":"rimunroe","month":"2026-02","story":"Who Smeared Feynman","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I know what you mean but this framing is dismissive. I think the larger change is that it's become a bit more acceptable in the society as a whole to acknowledge that many men we've held up on pedestals were actually flawed, or at the very least to give more credence and attention to stories told by contemporaries. In the case of Feynman, I think the way he writes about his relationship with women gives clear examples of misogyny. From an article[1] on this subject: > Among his many accomplishments, he contributed to several key conceptual breakthroughs in quantum physics, and his role in developing the field of quantum electrodynamics led to a Nobel Prize in 1965, which he shared with Julian Schwinger and Shin’ichirō Tomonaga. [...] He came off as a fun, likeable guy who just liked to do math, play pranks, and bang on the bongos. > These things are true. But it’s also true that throughout his career, Feynman reveled in blatant misogyny and sexism. In “Surely You’re Joking”, Feynman details how he adopted the mindset of a pick-up artist (an outlook he also claims to have eventually abandoned) by treating women as if they were worthless and cruelly lashing out at them when they rejected his advances. He worked and held meetings in strip clubs, and while a professor at Cal Tech, he drew naked portraits of his female students. Even worse, perhaps, he pretended to be an undergraduate student to deceive younger women into sleeping with him. Mythologizing or overly condemning figur"},{"id":42194284,"author":"soulofmischief","month":"2024-11","story":"Coincidences that make our existence possible","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman's refusal to answer a why question about magnets.","quote":"Richard Feynman went off on an interviewer for asking","text":"Not quite. It was declared useless to think about precisely because physics can't answer the most fundamental question of \"Why\". Richard Feynman went off on an interviewer for asking \"Why are they doing that?\" (regarding magnetic attraction/repulsion) precisely for this reason. [0] He considers it a waste of time to consider, because you fundamentally can't make meaningful progress on the problem with our current tools and understanding, and it's possible that no amount of tools or understanding can reach the bottom-most turtle. There are some things we may simply never know. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39681519,"author":"michaelsbradley","month":"2024-03","story":"Webb and Hubble confirm Universe's expansion rate","stance":"reference","why":"Makes the point that explaining things via the familiar has limits.","quote":"Makes the same point on a related matter","text":"> If photon is not waving something, then it's not a wave. No, you’ve simply hit the limits of needing/wanting to understand something in terms of something else similar or more familiar. Makes the same point on a related matter: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26349658,"author":"RichardCA","month":"2021-03","story":"Consciousness Is Just a Feeling","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on how scientists assign meaning to why questions.","quote":"he spends almost 10 minutes talking about how a scientist assigns meaning to a question","text":"You asked the question, \"Why is the hard problem a problem?\" There's a documentary where someone asked Richard Feynman why magnets work, he spends almost 10 minutes talking about how a scientist assigns meaning to a question: [VIDEO] \"When you explain a 'Why', you have to be in some framework where you allow things to be true.\" So what I would say is that people generally allow Cartesian Dualism to be \"true\" and that's the thing that needs to be challenged. Sometimes this is called Nondualism or Nonduality. If you look at it that way, the idea that the consciousness problem is an appropriate area for inquiry seems like a straightforward idea. As far as Carl Sagan being wrong, he was big on the idea that our subjective minds are just a thing that hydrogen atoms do, if you give them billions of years to do it. But he never came out as a Strict Materialist. He also liked to poke holes in religion but never came out as an Atheist. Yes, he was being clever and cute about it. But I don't think that makes him wrong, just that he was onto something more subtle about the nature of what we are. Human beings are a way for the universe to know itself."}],"r":[["5_KcQt0z-eE",1],["QEJpZjg8GuA",1],["dEaecUuEqfc",1],["1MGjFKs9bnU",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["4Q7FTjhvZ7Y",1],["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1],["FjHJ7FmV0M4",1],["k_5M-z_RUKA",1],["l7rce6IQDWs",1],["Q2aEzeMDHMA",1],["MO0r930Sn_8",1]]},"_ivqWN4L3zU":{"wy":"HN recommends the series as a thoughtful account of engineering as rule-of-thumb problem solving that predates formal science.","q":{"x":"I encourage everyone to watch this series of videos.","i":37778126,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":48729631,"author":"sublinear","month":"2026-06","story":"Open Source Low Tech","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Engineering improvisation is impressive, not inferior","quote":"It's too important of a topic to not drive this point home crystal clear by someone who is far more respected than me.","text":"As other comments have implied, but not explicitly said, they do their own engineering already with what they have. Since materials can be scarce and inconsistent, much of it is improvised. That in no way diminishes their efforts, results, or knowledge. If anything, that's way more impressive. Lots of engineers in the first world will throw a tantrum if they can't have things exactly their way and probably still make something that doesn't work as well. Entire businesses have shit their pants and gone bankrupt the moment a part is discontinued. Trying to do better than the people who live there is not only arrogant, but it's own variation of Chesterton's Fence. EDIT: I can't help myself and have to post an engineerguy video. It's too important of a topic to not drive this point home crystal clear by someone who is far more respected than me. \"Building a Cathedral without Science or Mathematics: The Engineering Method Explained\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":37778126,"author":"empath-nirvana","month":"2023-10","story":"Pythagorean Theorem Found on Clay Tablet 1k Years Older Than Pythagoras","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series on engineering using rules of thumb","quote":"I encourage everyone to watch this series of videos.","text":"What everyone is talking past here is that the Babylonians discovered what engineers call a \"rule of thumb\", and engineering is focused on getting things done using rules of thumb. The best possible rule of thumb is a mathematical proof or a scientific discovery, but it's by no means necessary. . Observing that something always holds and even having a formula for it is not the same thing as having a proof, and the proof is what makes it mathematics and geometry and not \"just\" engineering. The babylonians had a rule of thumb -- the greeks discovered the theorem -- and more than that, they seem to have invented the mathematical/geometrical proof as a concept, along with formal logic. Without that mental framework, it's hard to say that the babylonians proved anything or had any theorems at all, only collections of rules of thumb. It's quite likely that lots of babylonians sort of independently and intuitively understood _why_ it must be true, but they don't seem to have ever written it down. Not that there's anything wrong with having rules of thumb -- it's a huge achievement to even notice and collect and teach those things, all the stuff around you is built relying on them. I encourage everyone to watch this series of videos. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40562766,"author":"oscare","month":"2024-06","story":"Ask HN: What was your most humbling learning moment?","stance":"rec","why":"Defines the engineering method as rules of thumb","quote":"this excellent video from the 'engineerguy'","text":"Lately I've come to understand the term engineer somewhat differently from this excellent video from the 'engineerguy': [VIDEO] He defines the Engineering Method as: 'Solving problems using rules of thumb that cause the best change in a poorly understood situation using available resources.' In that sense a Software Engineer would not necessary need to know all the complexities of the complete system to make something work as specified. And I would equate a 'Programmer' more like the Scientist or Mathematician in this case. Funny how there are so many different ways to look at a title."},{"id":37438737,"author":"mcpackieh","month":"2023-09","story":"China's ancient water pipe networks","stance":"rec","why":"Engineering preceded Newtonian physics","quote":"Bill Hammack aka engineerguy has a good video about this","text":"> or building slender towers supported on intricate networks of arches without engineering calculations or even Newtonian physics. In fact engineering solutions came long before Newtonian physics. Bill Hammack aka engineerguy has a good video about this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35964316,"author":"mayormcmatt","month":"2023-05","story":"The Sourdough Framework","stance":"rec","why":"Ancient structures built with rules of thumb, not science","quote":"Truly fascinating.","text":"Just yesterday I started watching the video series promoting Bill Hammack's book \"The Things We Make\" in which he discusses how many incredible ancient structures were built using rules of thumb rather than an actual understanding of the science /math underpinning their strength. Truly fascinating. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45847563,"author":"empath75","month":"2025-11","story":"Is Software the UFOlogy of Engineering Disciplines?","stance":"reference","why":"Engineering existed before government manuals","quote":"How do you think all of those engineering organizations came up with those manuals and regulations","text":"Government did not invent the discipline of engineering. This is just completely backwards. How do you think all of those engineering organizations came up with those manuals and regulations, were they not doing any engineering until they published a manual? Complete nonsense. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36197214,"author":"tobr","month":"2023-06","story":"A Prompt Pattern Catalog to Enhance Prompt Engineering with ChatGPT","stance":"reference","why":"Definition of the engineering method","quote":"Solving problems using rules of thumb that cause the best change in a poorly understood situation using available resources.","text":"Matches well with the definition of the engineering method provided by Bill Hammack, “the engineer guy” [1]: > Solving problems using rules of thumb that cause the best change in a poorly understood situation using available resources. 1: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hBmhDf8cl0k",1],["YGLNyHd2w10",1]]},"iHzzSao6ypE":{"wy":"HN cites it as a concise, well-illustrated explanation of traffic flow, in threads on congestion, self-driving cars and driving habits.","q":{"x":"CGP Grey did a really nice, concise video on the topic of traffic","i":21355338,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":46960770,"author":"maerF0x0","month":"2026-02","story":"Hard-braking events as indicators of road segment crash risk","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why following space prevents cascading hard-braking events.","quote":"This youtube talks about it","text":"> You don’t own the asphalt in front of you. Correct, but you _need_ the asphalt in front of you for both safe driving and also to avoid cascading hard braking events. I also don't own the asphalt under me or behind me too, so it's kinda a silly statement tbh. This youtube talks about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41203275,"author":"pchristensen","month":"2024-08","story":"Robocars improve traffic even when most cars around them are driven by people","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates how tailgating cascades into braking for nonexistent obstacles.","quote":"This video illustrates the principle","text":"This video illustrates the principle: [VIDEO] Basically, people following too closely cause more braking, which cascades to more braking for an obstacle that was never actually there."},{"id":28855856,"author":"mjevans","month":"2021-10","story":"AI predicts accident hot-spots from satellite imagery and GPS data","stance":"rec","why":"Simple solution to traffic with good visuals.","quote":"CGP Gray's simple solution to traffic and good visuals.","text":"CGP Gray's simple solution to traffic and good visuals. [VIDEO] The real simple solution to traffic is no more monkeys driving cars. Also, implicitly, fully isolate cars from human powered forms of transportation."},{"id":21355338,"author":"Timpy","month":"2019-10","story":"Ants Are Practically Immune to Traffic Jams","stance":"rec","why":"Concise video arguing coordination, not cars, is the problem.","quote":"CGP Grey did a really nice, concise video on the topic of traffic","text":"CGP Grey did a really nice, concise video on the topic of traffic and he addresses your point in the first 30 seconds. Coordination, not cars, is the problem. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19672535,"author":"yreg","month":"2019-04","story":"Microsimulation of Traffic Flow","stance":"rec","why":"Advises keeping equal distance between cars ahead and behind.","quote":"Your goal, as a driver, should be to try to maintain equivalent distance","text":"[CGP Grey did a video on this]( [VIDEO] ). Your goal, as a driver, should be to try to maintain equivalent distance between your car and cars in front of you and behind you."},{"id":18985538,"author":"heimatau","month":"2019-01","story":"Queueing theory: The science of waiting in line","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why gliding beats stop-and-go traffic.","quote":"CGP Grey has a great video on traffic flow","text":"Stop/go is bad. Gliding is better for the collective and for your fuel efficiency. CGP Grey has a great video on traffic flow: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46952846,"author":"chausen","month":"2026-02","story":"Hard-braking events as indicators of road segment crash risk","stance":"reference","why":"Supports leaving space and smoothing slowdowns to reduce congestion.","quote":"All of the people tailgating are contributing to the congestion.","text":"It brings me peace to see other people thinking this way. You should be an active participant on the highway, making decisions to maximize flow. Leaving space so people can merge, controlling speed to smooth slowdowns, anticipating traffic patterns, etc. All of the people tailgating are contributing to the congestion. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36077181,"author":"orpheansodality","month":"2023-05","story":"Whistleblower Drops 100 GB of Tesla Secrets to German News Site","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for how following gaps helps prevent traffic.","quote":"Also has the benefit of helping prevent traffic to begin with[1]","text":"if you think about the speeds involved, a single additional car in front of you on the freeway (or even any additional cars) adds pretty miniscule time to the total commute. Let's compare a few situations. In the baseline you're tailing the car in front of you with a focus on not letting anyone cheat and get in front of you, let's say 50 feet away. Your commute is 30 miles, and in this frictionless sphere of traffic you're going 60mph the whole time. You get to work in 30 minutes flat. In the second scenario you're following the 3-second rule[0]. This would put you ~285 feet behind the car in front of you. Let's say over the course of your commute 20 cars move in front of you. If the average car length is 15 feet, and they all are 50 feet away from each other, when all 20 cars are in place you're a net -(20 * 65) feet away from the original car, or 1300 feet total. At 60 mph that adds ~15 seconds to your total commute time. Well worth having an easier time avoiding a potential crash IMO! Also has the benefit of helping prevent traffic to begin with[1] 0: [LINK] . 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30556422,"author":"fouc","month":"2022-03","story":"Tesla driver dies after his Model 3 stops on highway, gets hit by two other","stance":"reference","why":"Covers keeping gaps and steady driving to reduce congestion.","quote":"This CGP video covers some of this","text":"In addition to increasing the gap, you have to drive steadily, with no braking. This makes you become the most predictable car on the road, people behind you can follow steadily instead of constantly accelerating & braking. This leads to reduced congestion. This CGP video covers some of this [VIDEO]"},{"id":29358486,"author":"LordDragonfang","month":"2021-11","story":"California port truckers ‘drowning’ in supply chain inefficiencies","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how selfish close-following causes slower traffic than leaving a buffer.","quote":"","text":"The video that the above animation is from [1] is about how bad (or alternatively, \"selfish\") driving behaviors where individuals inch up as close to the car in front of them as quickly as possible (only to have to slam on their brakes) actually lead to worse and slower traffic than if everyone just left a buffer of space in front of them and drove at a moderate pace. So it's actually a lot more analogous than you'd think. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20833865,"author":"mjevans","month":"2019-08","story":"The Mobility Trap: Why We'll Never Fix Congestion by Speeding Up Traffic","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in a thought experiment about self-driving cars easing congestion.","quote":"","text":"This isn't what they studied, but a simple thought experiment shows a limited use case where traffic absolutely can be helped by speeding things up. Let's imagine a world where self driving cars are required for freeway driving; this allows them to respond in a predictable and timely manor with respect to directives to avoid lanes (and not jump the line) as well as to go at an exact speed. An event (accident, whatever) obstructs One of Five possible lanes for use (including the HOV lane) on a section normally rated at 60 MPH. This reduces the capacity of the road by 1/5th, or alternately, one lane of flow should be re-distributed across 4 lanes (1/4th per lane). Idealized solution: A temporary bypass zone is communicated to the self-driving cars. There is a lead up zone where the speed is expected to ramp up to the new target, followed by a merge zone where the lanes reduce and the flow is re-shaped to balance traffic evenly. The cars would then pass the incident zone in this higher speed lane-compressed flow, before expanding out and then slowing back down to normal speed. Due to the unexpected nature of the incident there has also been a buildup of volume before the incident, so to alleviate that slightly higher flow will be allowed to drain the high pressure area. In the above hypothetical case, for 4 out of 5 lanes nornally at 60MPH, I would prefer to imagine: (1 + 1/4) * 60MPH => 75, but round up another 5 MPH for the bypass flow for a total of 80MPH in the corrected flo"},{"id":19923927,"author":"joshuak","month":"2019-05","story":"Almost every speed limit is too low (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for driving-with-gaps strategies.","quote":"People always talk about driving with gaps[1]","text":"People always talk about driving with gaps[1], but I've yet to see a simulator show how detrimental/beneficial these strategies are to individual drivers. It feels like a hawks and doves style ESS (evolutionary stable strategy) problem. It appears to me that the strategy that eliminates the overall negative affect for the group (avoid creating phantom stop signs, by gapping or \"always in the middle\" strategies) is necessarily unstable. The benefit to any single driver who does not follow that rule becomes increasingly high as more and more others do follow the rule. Even to the point of ignoring a beeping car I should think. I certainly feel the negative effect driving behind someone who creates a gap as more and more cars merge in front of them. In the pathological case the driver who insists on a safe interval between cars will make no progress at all. 1: (CGP Grey) [VIDEO]"},{"id":15940249,"author":"bryananderson","month":"2017-12","story":"Elon Musk’s Ideas About Transportation are Boring","stance":"reference","why":"Linked incidentally in an argument about driverless, shared vehicle ecosystems.","quote":"","text":"I am on the same page with everyone here about the problems with car-centered infrastructure, but I think perhaps we haven’t fully internalized the extent to which driverless, electric, shared vehicles will change this equation. It’s an entirely new paradigm - and even more so if cheap tunneling can be achieved. While Musk’s initial ideas about how to reduce the cost may be wrong, at least he’s trying to! His initial ideas about electric cars and rockets weren’t completely right either, because he was not an expert in those areas. He hired the most talented people in those fields and learned and changed over a period of years until he found what worked. It’s a good algorithm, and it helps to be wealthy so that you can survive the failures and dead-ends without running out of money. There are two arguments in the article: that Musk is going about solving the problem in the wrong way, and that he is solving the wrong problem altogether by trying to put cars in tunnels. The author may well be right about the first, but I think he/she is wrong about the second. Let’s think about everything that changes in a fully driverless, electric, shared, and tunnel-augmented vehicle ecosystem. No more greenhouse gas emissions directly from vehicles (and none at all once the grid is fully renewable). No more need to dedicate huge amounts of valuable land in our cities and suburbs to parking (cars can drive themselves to outlying areas to park or, better yet, can be shared). Dramatically highe"},{"id":14510936,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2017-06","story":"Electric vehicles reached 2M cars in 2016","stance":"reference","why":"Describes the stay-halfway-between algorithm that would reduce congestion.","quote":"If every driver implemented the \"stay halfway between\" algorithm","text":"EV cars do not solve congestion Autopilot cars can do a lot for congestion. If every driver implemented the \"stay halfway between\" algorithm mentioned in CGP Grey's \"The Simple Solution to Traffic\" video, there would be far less congestion. [VIDEO] EVs would also enable simpler tunnel construction and better tunnel economics. This is a part of Elon Musk's new proposals."},{"id":14196138,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2017-04","story":"Alphabet's Self-Driving Cars to Get Their First Real Riders","stance":"reference","why":"Describes the stay-between algorithm that improves traffic flow.","quote":"It would only take a smallish fraction of cars implementing the \"stay between\" algorithm","text":"what is the validation strategy to show where driverless cars can quantifiably improve? One big way in which automated drivers could improve traffic: By not being a jackass. I remember at one point driving down Westheimer, which is four lanes wide and one of the major thoroughfares of Houston, when I had to slow down for someone making a right turn to merge into traffic into a middle lane while their head was buried deep into the passenger side footwell looking through their stuff. Then, there are the people who feel like they have to tailgate you within 6 feet during rush hour traffic on the highway. Also, the people who won't let you in for some personal justice you can't possibly understand. It would only take a smallish fraction of cars implementing the \"stay between\" algorithm that CGP Grey mentions in his video to significantly improve traffic. [VIDEO] I've implemented this algorithm manually. (Much easier to do since I have the instant accelerator response of an electric car.) It does seem to improve traffic flow. Also, jackass tailgaters are sometimes confused by this, and decide to pass."},{"id":12544527,"author":"tstrimple","month":"2016-09","story":"Self-Driving Cars Must Meet 15 Benchmarks in U.S. Guidance","stance":"reference","why":"Supports the claim that software drivers reduce congestion from reaction delays.","quote":"It's delays in human reaction times which cause cars to back up on roads","text":"The more human drivers that are replaced by software, the less congestion there will be on the roads. It's delays in human reaction times which cause cars to back up on roads and take forever to get through intersections. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["dngqR9gcDDw",1],["kZA9Hnp3aV4",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["hFL6qRIJZ_Y",1],["ZWQ0591PAxM",1],["ysa5OBhXz-Q",1],["2Q26XIKtwXQ",1],["7vsCAM17O-M",1],["Erp8IAUouus",1],["Pld8wTa16Jk",1],["8YUWDrLazCg",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["kCc8FmEb1nY",1],["WZskjLq040I",1]]},"3VZSJKbzyMc":{"wy":"Shared in climate and land-use threads as a vivid example of how letting land regrow can restore forest.","q":{"x":"This is a great short film that goes into detail about the project and the challenges faced in getting it off the ground","i":28732729,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":45451322,"author":"Aromasin","month":"2025-10","story":"Warming climate–not overgrazing–is biggest threat to rangelands, study suggests","stance":"rec","why":"Hugh Wilson's rewilding of herding land back to native rainforest.","quote":"it's a very interesting video that really highlights the impact that over-grazing has","text":"There's a guy in New Zealand I met who spent the last 30 years rewilding what was previously agriculture herding land. He said his biggest frustration with land owners wasn't the push back on what he was doing, but the ignorance as to what could be achieved. Every single person he spoke to told him explicitely if the land was left to go to nature, it would be nothing but gorse and it was a waste of good grazing land. The actual result was a return of native rain forest with levels of species diversity that were almost 60% of untouched rainforest, which is pretty incredibly in such a short amount of time. This is the guy for context - it's a very interesting video that really highlights the impact that over-grazing has: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31271891,"author":"Aromasin","month":"2022-05","story":"Historic fertilizer crunch threatens food security","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the dramatic impact of simply leaving land alone to regrow.","quote":"This video is especially poignant, and really shows the dramatic impact simply doing nothing has","text":"Frankly, yes, they do fix themselves. You'd be utterly shocked at what landscapes look like if we leave them. Having been part of forest regeneration efforts across the UK for the past 8 years - from southern heathlands to rocky Welsh and Scottish outcrops, good for \"nothing but sheep\" - I can say with all certainty that every part of the country except the most extreme alpine regions is suitable for reforestation or some form of plantation dense rewilding, mooring, wild flower meadow, etcetera. The same can be said of much of Canada and America, with my peers over there having the same results when grazing stops. Hell, the Mongolian steppes are turning to results of goat herding, and the Gobi desert is though to have been cause by the same. In the UK for example, people are completely ignorant to how the place looked before we levelled it for grazing, viewing the 70% ecologically barren agricultural land (of which 80% of that is used for grazing or agriculture to feed cattle) as the norm and the 2.5% old growth forest as a more novelty, and something strangely \"unique to that area\". The country was 60-80% temperate rainforest before human habitation, depending on what research you go by. This video is especially poignant, and really shows the dramatic impact simply doing nothing has: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28732729,"author":"dfcowell","month":"2021-10","story":"Downsides to planting trillions of trees","stance":"rec","why":"Details the forest project and challenges faced getting it started.","quote":"This is a great short film that goes into detail about the project and the challenges faced in getting it off the ground","text":"This is a great short film that goes into detail about the project and the challenges faced in getting it off the ground: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24972896,"author":"tastyfreeze","month":"2020-11","story":"Is it better to plant trees or let forests regrow naturally?","stance":"rec","why":"Short film on land returning to forest in New Zealand over 30 years.","quote":"I enjoy sharing the short film in this vein","text":"I enjoy sharing the short film in this vein, \"Fools and Dreamers\", that follows a group that bought some land in New Zealand and let it return to forest over 30 years. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21022310,"author":"elijahparker","month":"2019-09","story":"Bird populations have declined since 1970 across nearly all habitats","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on Hugh Wilson regenerating native forest at Hinewai reserve over 30 years.","quote":"Hugh oversees 1500 hectares resplendent in native forest, where birds and other wildlife are abundant","text":"Here’s an encouraging story for New Zealand: [VIDEO] “Fools & Dreamers: Regenerating a Native Forest is a 30-minute documentary about Hinewai Nature Reserve, on New Zealand’s Banks Peninsula, and its kaitiaki/manager of 30 years, botanist Hugh Wilson. When, in 1987, Hugh let the local community know of his plans to allow the introduced ‘weed’ gorse to grow as a nurse canopy to regenerate farmland into native forest, people were not only skeptical but outright angry – the plan was the sort to be expected only of “fools and dreamers”. Now considered a hero locally and across the country, Hugh oversees 1500 hectares resplendent in native forest, where birds and other wildlife are abundant and 47 known waterfalls are in permanent flow. He has proven without doubt that nature knows best – and that he is no fool.”"},{"id":37268703,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-08","story":"Global risk of heat stress to cattle from climate change","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in a long argument about animal agriculture and land use.","quote":"","text":"> My argument was simply that it's not enough on its own There is not a magic bullet at this point ... we have to do many things to correct the overshoot. > reforestation can help, but that's not synonymous with stopping animal ag, although of course there is overlap Animal agriculture occupies as much space as all our forests do. The overlap and deforestation that have occurred over millennia are highly significant. [LINK] > doing so at the necessary scales would be incredibly disruptive to world cultures and economies There are many reasons why we need that. Without abundant biodiversity we might see collapse of animal populations, and we might lose remaining pollinators, for example. We won't have any economy without those services we take for granted from the nature. [LINK] If you're ethical vegan, you'll know that culture is just stories we tell ourselves, and those stories are/were very often wrong (like slavery, for example). I don't have to tell you that with animal ag. we're taking something from those animals that isn't ours to take. I'll suppose from now on that you're a vegan and I don't have to explain this to you anymore. [LINK] > is not going to go vegan or vegetarian It's something we have to do, no matter what anyone thinks. We don't have enough space to feed everyone a meat and dairy diet in the quantities that Westerners consume. We could attempt to do so, but that would result in the total collapse of ecosystems and our own demise. [LINK] One study estimat"},{"id":29340479,"author":"Aromasin","month":"2021-11","story":"Greening of the Earth Mitigates Surface Warming (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as New Zealand example in argument for reforestation over grazing.","quote":"","text":"Same in the UK (barring the mammals point). The country was 60-80% temperate rainforest before human habitation, depending on what research you go by. I grew up in the countryside. I cannot tell you how impossible it is to convince someone, who is dead set on consuming animal products, that we could regenerate that land if only we got rid of the cattle/fodder though. It's always \"that land can't be used for anything else\" or \"having animals grazing there is good for the land\", and that is just demonstrably false in every way. It's baffling that there can literally be old growth forest just on the other side of the hill, and the farmers will be adamant that the land they're on would be useless and barren if it weren't for them - as they continue to staunchly cut back the hedgerows and mow their fields every few months. Having been part of forest regeneration efforts across the UK - from southern heathlands to rocky Welsh and Scottish outcrops, good for \"nothing but sheep\" - I can say with all certainty that every part of the country except the most extreme alpine regions is suitable for reforestation or some form of plantation dense rewilding. People are completely ignorant to how the country looked before we levelled it for grazing, viewing the 70% ecologically barren agricultural land (of which 80% of that is used for grazing or feeding cattle) as the norm and the 2.5% old growth forest as a more novelty, and something strangely \"unique to that area\". If you're from New Zeal"},{"id":28020232,"author":"archibaldJ","month":"2021-07","story":"The Metaverse Has Always Been a Dystopian Idea","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Hugh Wilson as an example of achieving impact differently.","quote":"","text":"Yup that's the other approach;) Can achieve impact in another way too (e.g. Hugh Wilson [VIDEO] ) But then it gets harder/costlier to access opporunities, facilities (eg health care) and infrastructures, as well as social and monetray capitals and (economical and politcal) power."}],"r":[["15R2pMqU2ok",1],["URJ_qSXruW0",1],["RUwS1uAdUcI",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["o0fG_lnVhHw",1]]},"GTQnarzmTOc":{"wy":"Offered as a quick, entertaining primer on Keynesian versus Austrian economics, especially for newcomers to Hayek.","q":{"x":"this throwback to the 2008 crisis is an easily consumed primer on Keynes vs Hayek","i":39675667,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39675667,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2024-03","story":"Rethinking my economics","stance":"rec","why":"Fun, quick primer on Keynes versus Hayek.","quote":"an easily consumed primer on Keynes vs Hayek","text":"If anyone wants a fun quick summary of Hayek this throwback to the 2008 crisis is an easily consumed primer on Keynes vs Hayek: Keynes vs Hayek rap battle 1: [LINK] Keynes vs Hayek rap battle 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23687304,"author":"miles","month":"2020-06","story":"Friedrich Hayek on Evolution and Spontaneous Order (1983) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining overview for newcomers to Hayek.","quote":"For those new to Hayek, here is an entertaining overview","text":"There have been a number of Hayek posts, though sadly few of them gained much traction: [LINK] For those new to Hayek, here is an entertaining overview: Fear the Boom and Bust: Keynes vs. Hayek [LINK] Fight of the Century: Keynes vs. Hayek [VIDEO]"},{"id":9315119,"author":"smoyer","month":"2015-04","story":"Remembering My Cousin, Ludwig Wittgenstein (1977)","stance":"rec","why":"Keynes and Hayek debate the 2008 crisis.","quote":"Another little treat - Keynes and Hayek discussing the 2008 crisis in the US","text":"I don't always agree with him but I always enjoy reading Hayek's writing. Another little treat - Keynes and Hayek discussing the 2008 crisis in the US: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4917263,"author":"nickik","month":"2012-12","story":"Milton Friedman's Thermostat","stance":"rec","why":"Rap shows Hayek's view of war and the New Deal.","quote":"Watch this modern Rap-Video of Hayek vs. Keynes and listen closly when the talk about the war.","text":"You have very strang opinions. One the one hand you speak well of hayke on the other point you use the exact opposit of what hayek belived to explain the recovery from the great depression. I dont want to go into the hold Great Depression discussion, I just wanted to point out that hayek would have made the argument that the New Deal made the GD much longer, same with the WW2. The idea that a war can help the economy would made hayek cry. Watch this modern Rap-Video of Hayek vs. Keynes and listen closly when the talk about the war. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2852894,"author":"ramanujan","month":"2011-08","story":"United States loses AAA credit rating from S&P ","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the Hayekian view in contrast to Keynes.","quote":"the Hayekian view is well explained by these two videos","text":"You take the Keynesian view. It's difficult to discuss this in more than a soundbite, but the Hayekian view is well explained by these two videos: [LINK] [VIDEO] Well worth watching if you haven't seen them. In short, the opposite/Hayekian view contends that taxation is seizure of resources from profitable/efficient entities and redistribution towards unprofitable/inefficient entities. We saw this in extremis in the bank bailout, and more controversially in microcosm with individual redistribution. If you accept this -- that \"profitable\" correlates with \"efficient\", and conversely, though the correlation is not perfect -- then taxation is not a Robin Hood sort of thing that increases well being, but rather a move that ties the legs of the efficient sectors of the economy -- those that can post a profit even in tough times -- to those that are already underwater."},{"id":37513128,"author":"passion__desire","month":"2023-09","story":"Intermediate sci knowledge associated with overconfidence and negative attitudes","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as loosely relevant to a Sokal-hoax question.","quote":"","text":"Well is it possible that an Alan Sokal kind of trick be pulled of in economics if it is a social science. Relevant : [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22149170,"author":"miles","month":"2020-01","story":"Failure to recognize J. M. Keynes's work guarantees further crises","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to support anti-Keynesian argument.","quote":"it is our slavish devotion to Keynesianism that promises a continued future of boom and bust cycles","text":"On the contrary; it is our slavish devotion to Keynesianism that promises a continued future of boom and bust cycles: Keynes vs. Hayek, Round 1 & 2: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11615375,"author":"bhauer","month":"2016-05","story":"This Tech Bubble Is Bursting","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes a lyric to support a free-market point.","quote":"The economy is us. We don't need a mechanic. Put away the wrenches—the economy's organic.","text":"Good point. \"The economy is us. We don't need a mechanic. Put away the wrenches—the economy's organic.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":10499856,"author":"caseysoftware","month":"2015-11","story":"Advanced economies are so sick we need a new way to think about them","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as another take on Keynesian economics.","quote":"For another look at it, check out the Keynes vs Hayek rap battle","text":"\"Standard new Keynesian macroeconomics essentially abstracts away from most of what is important in macroeconomics.\" Yes, that has been the criticism for decades. There is nothing new here. The only time Keynesian economics has \"fixed\" an economy was WW2 when we (the US, specifically) had full employment because of the draft and all of our competition had been literally flattened and/or killed. For another look at it, check out the Keynes vs Hayek rap battle: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2528060,"author":"olalonde","month":"2011-05","story":"Bitcoin money ≠ the Gold Standard.","stance":"reference","why":"Rap-battle summary of Keynesian versus Austrian economics.","quote":"it all boils down to Keynesian economics[1] (steaming rant) vs Austrian economics[2] (this submission)","text":"I'm oversimplifying here, but it all boils down to Keynesian economics[1] (steaming rant) vs Austrian economics[2] (this submission) if anyone wants to dig deeper in the subject. tl;dr: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["d0nERTFo-Sk",3],["YAtLTLiqNwg",1],["2y5Pv4yN0b0",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["cidchWg74Y4",1],["-4FXLQoLDBA",1],["rv-mFSoZOkE",1],["ROor6_NGIWU",1],["GNG6ZzDh9C8",1],["xguam0TKMw8",1],["PJy8vTu66tE",1],["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["9WcHsT97suU",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["9tocssf3w80",1],["Eghl19elKk8",1],["N1CBDTgGtOU",1],["NM51qOpwcIM",1]]},"XfcvX0P1b5g":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a compelling, if not definitive, argument for Adam Back as Satoshi whenever the identity question comes up.","q":{"x":"painted quite a compelling story of why Adam Beck is Satoshi","i":29024707,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":47690500,"author":"4oo4","month":"2026-04","story":"Who Is Satoshi Nakamoto? My Quest to Unmask Bitcoin's Creator","stance":"rec","why":"Earlier convincing case identifying Satoshi.","quote":"Not definitive but it's a very convincing case.","text":"Someone already found this years ago: [VIDEO] I haven't read the full article yet but I'm guessing they didn't give credit, as the New York Times tends to do. Not definitive but it's a very convincing case."},{"id":39712349,"author":"qweqwe14","month":"2024-03","story":"Digital forgeries are hard","stance":"rec","why":"Strong arguments that Adam Back is Satoshi.","quote":"Pretty strong arguments as to why Adam Back is Satoshi","text":"Surprised that noone mentioned Barely Sociable video on Satoshi Nakamoto[1] (last of a 3-part series). Pretty strong arguments as to why Adam Back is Satoshi, the amount of coincidences is just way too many IMO. Craig Wright is definitely not Satoshi, the guy has some issues and so keeps claiming that he is. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32376546,"author":"syklep","month":"2022-08","story":"Another court case fails to unlock the mystery of Bitcoin’s Satoshi Nakamoto","stance":"rec","why":"Part 3 making the case that Adam Back is Satoshi.","quote":"I'm still convinced its him.","text":"Don't see anything on HN mentioning Adam Back getting called out as being Satoshi and the massive campaign launched to disprove this. One of the only people who isn't attempting to capitalize on the claim, but who directly benefits from Bitcoin's flaws. In a case of a white knight turning to the dark side. Part 3: [VIDEO] The conspiracy YouTuber making the claim was attacked and slandered heavily after making the claim despite convincing evidence that wasn't disproven: 1 Same English typing mannerisms 2 Misspelling the same words 3 A conspicuous gap in his career while Bitcoin was being developed 4 entering the bitcoin scene in forums very early acting authoritative Not to mention 5 A history of working on bitcoin-like technologies: \"hashcash\" 6 early involvement with the cypherpunks I'm still convinced its him."},{"id":29024707,"author":"CTDOCodebases","month":"2021-10","story":"Federated E-Cash as a Bitcoin Scaling Technology","stance":"rec","why":"Compelling case that Adam Back is Satoshi.","quote":"painted quite a compelling story of why Adam Beck is Satoshi","text":"I think the US government would already know who Satoshi is due to bulk email collection via PRISM and stylometry. I don’t know why you think Satoshi broke any US laws. The following video painted quite a compelling story of why Adam Beck is Satoshi: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28615128,"author":"n0mad01","month":"2021-09","story":"Guy claims to have evidence of who Satoshi Nakamoto is.","stance":"rec","why":"Argues Adam Back is likely Satoshi.","quote":"There are a few more references that it is more likely that Adam Back could be Satoshi.","text":"I was sure too about Hal Finney until i saw this video [0]. There are a few more references that it is more likely that Adam Back could be Satoshi. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43083763,"author":"entrepy123","month":"2025-02","story":"Jack Dorsey called out as Satoshi Nakamoto","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among sources on Satoshi's identity.","quote":"","text":"Who invented Bitcoin? Paul LeRoux - \"He [Paul LeRoux] also invented E4M the predecessor to Truecrypt, the encryption software NSA still can't crack. E4M was released the same exact way the BTC whitepaper was released. Fun fact: he is from Zimbabwe and was there during the '08 hyperinflation that created the $100 Trillion Dollar Bill, BTC was created the next year.\" [0] Shinichi Mochizuki - Ted Nelson speculated that Sinichi Mochuzuki is Santoshi. Len Sassaman - \"I'm pretty certain Satoshi's identity is known, although not publicly stated and/or confirmed by anyone. I believe a handful of the early developers had very strong suspicion as to who was behind the name, but never pursued a confirmation of it. My money is on Len [1]. The sheer number of coincidences, crossing of rare skill sets and interests, time, place, penchant of pseudo anonymously publishing things and happened to take his own life <2 months after Satoshi's final gloom-filled message, \"I’ve moved on to other things and probably won’t be around in the future\" [1, 2]. Also, \"her [Len's] wife probably has the keys.\" [3, 4]. Also \"Len Sassaman (died 2011)\". [5] Nick Szabo - \"...if it's one person, it's gotta be Szabo\", \"Only person in the world who wouldn't cite his work would be himself.\", and \"If it's more than one person, of course it could include Len. [6] Also, \"There is plenty of direct evedence [sic] that points to him [Szabo].\" [7] \"Here is Szabo last post about Bitgold before he renamed it to Bitcoin: [LIN"},{"id":39966981,"author":"spense","month":"2024-04","story":"New theory on Satoshi Nakamoto","stance":"reference","why":"Interview where Adam Back describes himself, supporting the theory.","quote":"","text":"honestly, i thought we settled on Adam Back as Satoshi: - adam was extremely active during the early cypherpunk days, when - he invented Hashcash, which bitcoin cites in its whitepaper and used as its PoW - he was the first to take Wei Dai's b-money proposal seriously and discusses combining it with hashcash (the 2 core elements of bitcoin) - he disappeared and reappeared professionally during the exact timeframe of bitcoin development - his writing closely matches satoshi's (british english + style/punctuation) - he had no public involvement with bitcoin until he joined Bitcointalk forums in 2013 and suddenly had intimate details of unknown bugs in bitcoin saying \"I thought I'd fixed them\". - he started Blockstream with the mission to ensure the survival of bitcoin (funded lighting, paid core devs, etc) - he recently helped nail the coffin on Craig Wright's case in court proving he was not satoshi, during which - he revealed the earliest known email correspondence with satoshi watch the interview were he uncomfortably describes himself [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31823016,"author":"pcthrowaway","month":"2022-06","story":"Are blockchains decentralized?","stance":"reference","why":"More detail on the block size dispute and Blockstream incentives.","quote":"This video goes into a bit more detail","text":"I think you're missing some context about (what is now called) Bitcoin Cash. Look up the debate between big blockers and small blockers if you're interested in learning more; a quick summary, many people wanted larger block size so bitcoin could process more transactions. I believe several newer core developers staged a coup to prevent this from happening, despite several of the longer-term contributors being in favour of it. The original contributors (sans satoshi, who was mostly inactive at this point), had their access to the git repositories revoked. This video goes into a bit more detail ( [VIDEO] ) but basically the people opposing a larger blocksize had financial incentives.. a lot were in bed with a company called Blockstream which benefits from their development on sidechains (now the lightning network). Had the block-size been increased, Bitcoin would be much more useful as a means of exchange (more transactions would be processed, and they would be cheaper). This is where Bitcoin Cash is now, but it doesn't get the BTC ticker symbol because Bitfinex and other CEXes have a lot of influence as well."}],"r":[["GZTr1-Gp74U",1]]},"v3pYRn5j7oI":{"wy":"Recommended as a short, joyful example of Feynman's accessible way of explaining physics, which a transcript cannot capture.","q":{"x":"I really recommend them. You just can't capture his joy and passion with a transcript.","i":3440716,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4529618,"author":"elteto","month":"2012-09","story":"Bill Gates: Books I Read this Summer","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman lecturing on physics as a great educator.","quote":"if you have not seen Feynman lecturing on Physics then you haven't seen a great educator","text":"I am a big time Feynman fan, sure we've had our share of great thinkers and awe inspiring intellects, but Feynman is just the perfect combination of that and a likable character that remains human for the rest of us. Einstein was also like that in many ways but he was the child of another time and while he was always humane and simple, Feynman was much more likable, full of funny and witty anecdotes that connect with people. Also if you have not seen Feynman lecturing on Physics then you haven't seen a great educator, see [1] and [2], just plain wonderful. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":3440716,"author":"dirtyaura","month":"2012-01","story":"Feynman on explanations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman's joy and passion that a transcript cannot capture.","quote":"I really recommend them. You just can't capture his joy and passion with a transcript.","text":"If you haven't watched these \"Fun to imagine\" with Feynman videos from YouTube, I really recommend them. You just can't capture his joy and passion with a transcript. \"Fun to Imagine\" playlist: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6526092,"author":"furyofantares","month":"2013-10","story":"Ten Hundred Words of Science","stance":"rec","why":"Remarkably accessible explanations of physics.","quote":"I am extremely impressed with how accessible his explanations are","text":"If you haven't seen it before you should search YouTube for Feynman Fun to Imagine. I am extremely impressed with how accessible his explanations are, and I would have loved to see what he could have done with the given restriction. Probably not as well as he did without it but probably better than most could do with it. edit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3135041,"author":"simonsarris","month":"2011-10","story":"My Heroes","stance":"rec","why":"Short Feynman explanations from the Fun to Imagine series.","quote":"They are short and most of them are very good","text":"There's also a small series of Feynman that has been put on Youtube called \"fun to imagine.\" They are short and most of them are very good: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5eBT6OSr1TI",1],["DkGMY63FF3Q",1],["AdKNCBrkZQ4",1],["LikxFZZO2sk",1]]},"34LGPIXvU5M":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a quick, accessible introduction to the propaganda model from Manufacturing Consent.","q":{"x":"Manufacturing Consent (there's a documentary form of it too) changed my entire outlook on media. Highly recommend.","i":25572138,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25572138,"author":"tehjoker","month":"2020-12","story":"CIA vs. Wikileaks [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short video on Manufacturing Consent's ideas.","quote":"Manufacturing Consent (there's a documentary form of it too) changed my entire outlook on media. Highly recommend.","text":"Manufacturing Consent (there's a documentary form of it too) changed my entire outlook on media. Highly recommend. Short vid based on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49097616,"author":"tehjoker","month":"2026-07","story":"Delayed Gratification – Proud to Be 'Last to Breaking News'","stance":"rec","why":"Simplified explanation of the propaganda model.","quote":"are you familiar with manufacturing consent’s propaganda model of the mass media? A simplified explanation","text":"Dropsite news is good, but are you familiar with manufacturing consent’s propaganda model of the mass media? A simplified explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27595774,"author":"jhabdas","month":"2021-06","story":"Types of propaganda, propaganda techniques, and propaganda strategies (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Chomsky's five filters of mass media.","quote":"Consent is being manufactured around you all the time.","text":"Five filters. One big media theory. Consent is being manufactured around you all the time. Noam Chomsky - The 5 Filters of the Mass Media Machine [VIDEO]"},{"id":23304486,"author":"tehjoker","month":"2020-05","story":"A No. 1 hit vanished from Poland’s charts – it’s not going quietly","stance":"rec","why":"Brief animated introduction to the propaganda model.","quote":"For those that don't like to read there's a brief animated YouTube introduction voiced by Amy Goodman of \"Democracy Now!","text":"[LINK] For those that don't like to read there's a brief animated YouTube introduction voiced by Amy Goodman of \"Democracy Now!\": [VIDEO] This theory, backed by enormous volumes of evidence and case studies, is very much worth a read. It explains how propaganda is instituted in the US without overt censorship."},{"id":29132727,"author":"tehjoker","month":"2021-11","story":"A photo on Wikipedia can ruin your life","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates institutional incentives that distort news.","quote":"the news is also molded by a set of institutional incentives that often results in a highly distorted picture of the world.","text":"Most people that have knowledge of a news story are often incredulous reading one due to incorrect framing or outright mistakes. More insidiously, the news is also molded by a set of institutional incentives that often results in a highly distorted picture of the world. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27877736,"author":"TrispusAttucks","month":"2021-07","story":"A reporter’s fight to expose Epstein’s crimes, and earn a living","stance":"reference","why":"Chomsky's view of media as outrage machine.","quote":"","text":"If Chomsky [1] is to be believed, the media is designed to keep the masses outraged and fighting amongst themselves while keeping those in power out of the spotlight. If the news gets to inconvenient for those in power it gets \"corrected\" by the media complex. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19400352,"author":"herbstein","month":"2019-03","story":"Facebook, Axios and NBC Paid to Whitewash Wikipedia Pages","stance":"reference","why":"Animation covering the propaganda model's filters including non-governmental influences.","quote":"This is talked about in the animation Al-Jazeera made.","text":"I gave this some more thought, and I think you're wrong. Wrong in the sense that Chomsky and Herman did focus on these things. One of the five filters is \"Flack\". In this filter \"wrong\" opinions are given flack from institutions with power. The focus on government comes from them being, arguably, the most powerful institution. The propaganda model does account for other non-governmental and non-media influences. This is talked about in the animation Al-Jazeera made. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16240343,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2018-01","story":"George Soros: Facebook and Google are a menace to society","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I agree with your description of populism, but it does not argue against decentralisation. I guess you think of the single leader as the \"centralised\" focal point of a populist movement. I agree, but it's not what the word centralised refer sto. Instead, it refers to the media being centralised or not. Any leader needs a platform to broadcast their message from. Centralised media have (some) selective power over the narrative, and therefore the role of gatekeepers or enablers (like when controversial figures get disproportionate attention because it causes more clicks - see also [0][1]). The decentralisation aspect of the internet undermines that, which in itself is value-neutral. This is not something that never happened before; the printing press allowed Luther to bypass the gatekeeping powers of the Catholic Church[2]. As I mentioned elsewhere, this lead to massive social upheaval ending in the Thirty Year's War, so it's not all positive (worth noting is that Luther's biggest issue was with the so-called \"indulgences\", which allowed people to buy off their sins[3][4]. How is that for unfair wealth inequality?) The point is that the printing press meant that a lot more voices could be heard by a lot more people. The Internet takes that to the next level. A lot more of those \"single charismatic leaders\" suddenly have a potential audience (this might explain why I feel disdain for most YouTubers: a lot of them want an audience for the sake of having an audience, without havin"}]},"eU5Dn-WaElI":{"wy":"Recommended as a fun, in-depth look at how sampling and music production work, and as proof that skilled humans can reconstruct tracks from scratch.","q":{"x":"I'm amazed at the level of musical knowledge needed to do this.","i":43591735,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":14303848,"author":"guruz","month":"2017-05","story":"Get started making music","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Recreating a Prodigy song in Ableton Live","quote":"I think I've watched this video a ton of times","text":"I think I've watched this video a ton of times: [VIDEO] That guy is using Ableton Live to re-create a popular song of The Prodigy."},{"id":43591735,"author":"dash2","month":"2025-04","story":"Recreating Daft Punk's Something About Us","stance":"rec","why":"Recreation of a Prodigy track in Ableton showing deep musical knowledge","quote":"I'm amazed at the level of musical knowledge needed to do this.","text":"Related: this recreation of Prodigy's Smack My Bitch Up. I'm amazed at the level of musical knowledge needed to do this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41385909,"author":"xpl","month":"2024-08","story":"Judge dismisses majority of GitHub Copilot copyright claims","stance":"rec","why":"Hand-made reconstructions of Prodigy tracks that sound identical","quote":"There are legendary Prodigy tracks reconstructions by Jim Pavloff","text":"There are legendary Prodigy tracks reconstructions by Jim Pavloff, see: 1. \"Smack my bitch up\" [VIDEO] 2. \"Voodoo people\" [LINK] Those pieces sound exactly the same as the original, but it does not violate copyright because they've been produced by hand. Mind blowing!"},{"id":7013737,"author":"bigsassy","month":"2014-01","story":"How Daft Punk Created One of Their Most Famous Samples","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth remake of Smack My Bitch Up showing its samples","quote":"My mind was blown when it shows how \"Bulls on Parade\" was sampled","text":"Here's a similar, but more in-depth, video on making \"Smack my Bitch Up\" from The Prodigy. My mind was blown when it shows how \"Bulls on Parade\" was sampled :D [VIDEO]"},{"id":7014745,"author":"klochner","month":"2014-01","story":"How Daft Punk Created One of Their Most Famous Samples","stance":"rec","why":"Fun remake of a Prodigy track","quote":"(and fun to watch)","text":"A little older but similar idea (and fun to watch) - remaking of The Prodigy's \"smack my bitch up\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":32038964,"author":"0x0","month":"2022-07","story":"Kate Bush’s “Running Up That Hill” synth sounds","stance":"reference","why":"Breakdown of a Prodigy track","quote":"Was this the breakdown you saw?","text":"Was this the breakdown you saw? \"Making of \"The Prodigy - Smack My Bitch Up\" in Ableton by Jim Pavloff\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":21503287,"author":"szastupov","month":"2019-11","story":"Ableton Connection Kit (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I've also realized how many popular songs use sampling","text":"Keep in mind that Lite version barely has any instruments so you'll have to either use 3rd-party plugins or rely on sampling. A few month ago I decided to start fresh and removed all pirated DAWs, plugins, purchased Lite version and started learning it in and out. I was surprised how far you can go with just sampling. It was so much fun discovering new sample packs, chopping and remixing songs, trying to make tonal instruments out of weird noises. I've also realized how many popular songs use sampling (for example [VIDEO] ). So yeah, setting creative constraints is important ;) I'm at the point when I'm super at home using Ableton and ready to spend more money on Suit, but holding hopes for black Friday discounts. Also, shameless plug [LINK]"}],"r":[["6ZYLp5uX9Yw",1],["PUv66718DII",1]]},"klTjiXjqHrQ":{"wy":"Listed alongside his other talks as foundational context for Dynamicland and visual-programming ideas, and he is the most frequently cited speaker on such threads.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend Bret's 2 talks for context","i":15565912,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15565912,"author":"skadamat","month":"2017-10","story":"The HARC First Year Report","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Context for Bret Victor's research vision","quote":"Highly recommend Bret's 2 talks for context","text":"Highly recommend Bret's 2 talks for context: [1] Seeing Spaces: [VIDEO] [2] Humane Representation of Thought: [LINK]"},{"id":34487952,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Has anyone fully attempted Bret Victor's vision?","stance":"rec","why":"One of Bret Victor's frequently cited talks","quote":"he gets more mentions than anyone else, usually.","text":"For the people asking who is Bret Victor: When \"What are your favourite tech talks of all time?\" is asked on HN, he gets more mentions than anyone else, usually. Some of his talks: Inventing on Principle [LINK] The Future of Programming [LINK] The Humane Representation of Thought [LINK] Media for Thinking the Unthinkable [LINK] Seeing Spaces [VIDEO] Drawing Dynamic Visualizations [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":18223107,"author":"mitchtbaum","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among related Bret Victor talks","quote":"see also Seeing Spaces","text":"on one level, yes on another, they're down right scary and overwhelming, or at least dumbfounding and perplexing ..thinking specifically about The Humane Representation of Thought [LINK] I had to stop it half way through.. :-/ see also Seeing Spaces [VIDEO] and Media for Thinking the Unthinkable [LINK]"},{"id":14509064,"author":"skadamat","month":"2017-06","story":"Ask HN: Why does visual programming suck?","stance":"rec","why":"Thinking behind Dynamicland's RealTalk project","quote":"Watch his 2 talks that highlight the thinking and inspiration behind the RealTalk project","text":"It's going to be entirely different I feel. Instead of manipulating graphics on a flat screen, focus will probably be on manipulating physical objects on a table / in a room. Watch his 2 talks that highlight the thinking and inspiration behind the RealTalk project: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":41871638,"author":"skadamat","month":"2024-10","story":"Show HN: Sava OS – A desktop interface for your web browser","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as spatial environment inspiration","quote":"","text":"This is pretty neat! I wish web browsers on Mac could span to 2-3 monitors because then you could really build a great spatial environment for web browsing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16461989,"author":"fao_","month":"2018-02","story":"Ergonomics of the Symbolics Lisp Machine (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Reminds of specialized tools lecture","quote":"Programmers don't have more specialized tools","text":"Mm. I've been wondering recently why is it that Programmers don't have more specialized tools (Which incidentally reminds me of this lecture: [Bret Victor -- Seeing Spaces] [VIDEO] ). It's interesting to think that high-performance traders have an entire market dedicated to specialized computer interfaces, terminals, etc. all focused on improving their productivity and ability to reason about the stock exchange. [LINK] As the top poster says about the Bloomberg terminals: \"\"1. You have to have a Bloomberg keyboard to work in finance. It's not even a question. It's a COGS for anybody who wants to work in finance or trading.\"\""}],"r":[["agOdP2Bmieg",2],["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["PFc379hu--8",1]]},"oHeehYYgl28":{"wy":"HN links it whenever humidifiers come up as the go-to explainer favoring simple evaporative designs over ultrasonic ones.","q":{"x":"Technology connections covers quite a bit of ground on various types of humidifiers and their up/downsides","i":26288850,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":32712891,"author":"bcjordan","month":"2022-09","story":"Interest in heat pumps has increased dramatically in recent years","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how humidifiers work; swamp cooler style is ideal","quote":"The TC channel is definitely a hacker's eye view in to all the technologies that we take for granted every day.","text":"Came here to post this :D The TC channel is definitely a hacker's eye view in to all the technologies that we take for granted every day. Tons of fascinating tech history covered, too. Other fun ones: - a defense of the simple drip coffee maker [LINK] - why do the turn signals not sync up when you're at a stop light? [LINK] - how humidifiers work and ehy the simple swamp cooler style are pretty ideal [VIDEO] - why dishwasher detergent packs are stupid (use powder/liquid) and why you should use the pre-wash slot and not bother pre rinsing your plates [LINK] - why do light switches click? [LINK]"},{"id":30680281,"author":"cheschire","month":"2022-03","story":"Why is it hard to buy things that work well?","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on humidifier quality","quote":"Technology Connections had a good video on the quality of humidifiers","text":"Were your humidifiers just not good at making air moist? Or were they completely non-functional? Technology Connections had a good video on the quality of humidifiers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26968369,"author":"xaduha","month":"2021-04","story":"The health benefits of better air","stance":"rec","why":"Guidance on choosing humidifiers","quote":"Watch this Optional follow-ups","text":"Ultrasonic one is my first, only started paying attention to all this stuff because of 2020 and went all in, just trying to make my home a less crummy place. Watch this [VIDEO] Optional follow-ups: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":26102675,"author":"dirktheman","month":"2021-02","story":"Dyson air purifier outperformed by cheap DIY box fan filter in Marketplace test","stance":"rec","why":"Humidifier comparison; fan and filter size matter most","quote":"Technology Connections did an episode on air humidifiers a while ago","text":"Technology Connections did an episode on air humidifiers a while ago: [VIDEO] The fan and filter size matter the most. Most of these things are hugely overpriced for what they are."},{"id":26288850,"author":"riggsdk","month":"2021-02","story":"Humidifier: Healthy or Harmful?","stance":"rec","why":"Covers types of humidifiers and their pros and cons","quote":"Technology connections covers quite a bit of ground on various types of humidifiers and their up/downsides","text":"Technology connections covers quite a bit of ground on various types of humidifiers and their up/downsides: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":47309905,"author":"schiffern","month":"2026-03","story":"Microplastics and nanoplastics in urban air originate mainly from tire abrasion","stance":"reference","why":"Supports using evaporative pad humidifiers","quote":"Just use an evaporative pad humidifier.","text":">I think not using an ultrasonic humidifier would be better stated as only using distilled water with ultrasonic humidifiers The common misconception is that they're \"safe\" if you only ever use distilled water. In reality they still atomize gunk and bacteria from the tank, because nobody ever cleans & sterilizes their dehumidifier tank often enough. Distilled water users still report high indoor particle counts when it runs, which is how you know it's ineffective as a mitigation). The cost of distilled water every year (even making it at home) means ultrasonic humidifiers are the most expensive option too. Just use an evaporative pad humidifier. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29559411,"author":"an-internet-guy","month":"2021-12","story":"Avoid Ultrasonic Humidifiers","stance":"reference","why":"Supports preferring evaporative over ultrasonic humidifiers","quote":"And of course, this guy has a video for this!","text":"Yes. We recently moved up to a place with real winters, and I got a series of humidifiers. I run air quality sensors indoors (yes, multiple), and indeed the ultra sonic ones kicked up a CRAZY amount of PM2.5s, PM1s, and PM10s. We now only use evaporative humidifiers. And of course, this guy has a video for this! [VIDEO]"},{"id":26974963,"author":"garfieldnate","month":"2021-04","story":"The health benefits of better air","stance":"reference","why":"Evaporative humidifiers not slower than ultrasonic","quote":"evaporative ones weren't actually slower than the ultrasonic ones","text":"Technology Connections did a video on humidifiers, and if I remember correctly the evaporative ones weren't actually slower than the ultrasonic ones: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2z5A-COlDPk",1],["Sp9H0MO-qS8",1],["Nq2HkAYbG5o",1]]},"7HKbjYqqPPQ":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an excellent talk on notation problems, useful when reading papers or designing a new metalanguage.","q":{"x":"an excellent talk by Guy Steele that goes over issue of notation and balkanization","i":24650353,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":34721879,"author":"porcoda","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: Please Review My Metalanguage","stance":"rec","why":"Useful for thinking about proposing new notations.","quote":"It’s an interesting talk and is useful in thinking about proposing new notations.","text":"You might be interested in Guy Steele’s talk “It’s time for a new old language”. He talks about similar notations and the problem of inventing new ones (although most of his talk is about things other than BNF-like notations : he just talks about them for a bit in the beginning). It’s an interesting talk and is useful in thinking about proposing new notations. YouTube: [VIDEO] If you search for the talk title you can find the PDF of the slides."},{"id":24650353,"author":"seg_lol","month":"2020-10","story":"Programming language notation is a barrier to entry","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent talk on notation issues and balkanization.","quote":"an excellent talk by Guy Steele that goes over issue of notation and balkanization","text":"Or ideas in general, should we ever gate keep the dissemination of ideas and knowledge? Innovation is more likely to occur when people cross disciplines and they can only do that if it is approachable, it is gradual and discoverable. Simple is hard, simplistic is easy. Someone is going to come back with essential vs accidental complexity [1] but we can always strive for simpler, more cohesive ways to exchange knowledge. How much of our current notation systems are essential? And when do they diverge for non-essential reasons? Computer systems form primarily around notation (language and syntax), so notation is definitely important [z] In the post the author mentions an excellent talk by Guy Steele that goes over issue of notation and balkanization titled, \"It's Time for a New Old Language\" where he outlines \"the history and current status of computer science metanotation\". [2] Notation, like syntax is important, but like a plethora of ill defined and specified Domain Specific Languages (DSL), notation also needs to be defined, regular and learnable. For notation takes words and turns them into pictures that compose into thought structures that cannot be expressed in human written language to the same specificity. Think Feynman [3] and Railroad [4] Diagrams, or musical notation [5] The abstract explains it beautifully, > The most popular programming language in computer science has no compiler or interpreter. Its definition is not written down in any one place. It has changed "},{"id":24093343,"author":"chubot","month":"2020-08","story":"Formulog: ML + Datalog + SMT","stance":"rec","why":"Even experts struggle with notation.","quote":"I also recommend this 2017 talk by Guy Steele It's Time for a New Old Language","text":"(Nice to see your work shared here :) ) I went through the corresponding videos last year: [LINK] [LINK] It was a great refresher as someone who once liked math but hasn't done much of it in ~20 years :) I had seen the blog posts, but there was some \"color\" in the videos that helped. For example I didn't realize that the fonts sometimes matter! Honestly, I still don't really read the notation, as I haven't had a strong reason to, but I feel it would be useful at some point. ---- For others, I also recommend this 2017 talk by Guy Steele It's Time for a New Old Language [VIDEO] Because even people in the field seem to have problems with the notation. He also was asked about this work a few days ago here and said he was still working on it in the background (being a \"completionist\"): [LINK] ----- FWIW as you know, Oil is more static than shell, and that was largely motivated by tools and static analysis (and negatively motivated by false positives in ShellCheck [LINK] ) I would like to go further in that direction, but getting the basic functionality and performance up to par has taken up essentially 100% of the time so far :-( My use of Zephyr ASDL was also partly motivated by some vague desire to get the AST into OCaml. However I haven't used OCaml in quite awhile and I get hung up on small things like writing a serializer and deserializer. I don't want to do it for every type/schema, so it requires some kind of reflection. And my understanding is that there are a bunch of pac"},{"id":16238044,"author":"chubot","month":"2018-01","story":"A practitioner’s guide to reading programming languages papers","stance":"rec","why":"History of metalanguage notations and their inconsistencies.","quote":"He gives a history of metalanguage notations (for both language semantics and syntax, including regexes and CFGs).","text":"This is great! I wish I had seen something like this years ago. Related: \"It's Time for a New Old Language\" by Guy Steele. [VIDEO] He gives a history of metalanguage notations (for both language semantics and syntax, including regexes and CFGs). And then he criticizes the inconsistency that has cropped up in these notations over the years. The irony is that people who are formalizing precise semantics of programming languages do not agree on the syntax/semantics of the metalanguage. I guess this is what you notice when you read hundreds of programming language papers, as I'm sure Guy Steele has. ----- Also, does anyone know if what he refers to as \"progress and preservation\" are the same / related to \"liveness and safety\" in distributed algorithms? It sounds like the same kind of idea."}],"r":[["xguam0TKMw8",1],["lHLpKzUxjGk",1],["R3xX37RGJKE",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["DJtef410XaM",1],["KINIAgRpkDA",1],["CuD7SCqHB7k",1],["TgmA48fILq8",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["gFEE3w7F0ww",1],["IeSu_odkI5I",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1]]},"HfNIiitVFtc":{"wy":"HN commenters strongly recommend it as a thoughtful talk on tech worker ethics that has not aged.","q":{"x":"Hasn't aged one bit. If anything Patricia is spot on pretty much everything.","i":25537621,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25537621,"author":"marios","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2020?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Keynote that has not aged and is spot on","quote":"Hasn't aged one bit. If anything Patricia is spot on pretty much everything.","text":"I watched this in 2020 even though it's from EuroBSDCon 2019. I'm talking about Patricia Aas keynote \"Embedded Ethics\": [VIDEO] Hasn't aged one bit. If anything Patricia is spot on pretty much everything. If you don't have time to spend on this, you may want to take a look at the slides: [LINK]"},{"id":21437645,"author":"notaplumber","month":"2019-11","story":"Update: UniFi Phone Home/Performance Data Collection","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Keynote on embedded ethics aimed at tech workers","quote":"Watch this keynote talk by Patricia Aas. It's called Embedded Ethics.","text":"For engineers reading this who work at a tech company, or well, any company. Watch this keynote talk by Patricia Aas. It's called Embedded Ethics. And it is crystal clear many of you need to watch it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21603595,"author":"simpss","month":"2019-11","story":"Slack’s new WYSIWYG input box is terrible","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Engineer explains tech worker ethics, how we got here, and fixes","quote":"It really resonated with my thoughts, how I've viewed the tech world and my (tiny) part in it.","text":"On another topic, someone recently posted this youtube video of a Norwegian engineer explaining her views on tech worker ethics, how we've got here and maybe a way to fix it or atleast improve on the current situation. I'd recommend showing it to your commute buddies. It really resonated with my thoughts, how I've viewed the tech world and my (tiny) part in it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21859459,"author":"marios","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2019?","stance":"rec","why":"Steps back from technical details to the bigger picture","quote":"sometimes you need to worry less about the gritty details of the tech and take a step back and look at the bigger picture.","text":"EuroBSDcon 2019 Keynote by Patricia Aas: Embedded Ethics ( [VIDEO] / slides: [LINK] ) Because, sometimes you need to worry less about the gritty details of the tech and take a step back and look at the bigger picture."}]},"GFosQtEqzSE":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an approachable, numbers-driven overview of what renewable energy would take.","q":{"x":"The author also gave a talk a few years ago that is very approachable[2].","i":12609139,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":9471465,"author":"IkmoIkmo","month":"2015-05","story":"Tesla Energy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"MacKay's Harvard talk on land area required for renewable energy","quote":"I very much recommend David Mackay's Without the Hot Air, a great talk he did at Harvard","text":"I felt the 'area needed in the US' to be a bit disingenuous. I very much recommend David Mackay's Without the Hot Air, a great talk he did at Harvard on area required can be found here: [VIDEO] Beyond that, very glad to see this happen. It's one of the factors necessary to push a sustainable electrified future forward."},{"id":13994513,"author":"spodek","month":"2017-03","story":"Eigg – A small Scottish isle that runs on 90-95% renewable energy","stance":"rec","why":"MacKay's Harvard talk on sustainable energy","quote":"If you haven't read Sustainable Energy Without the Hot Air , by David Mackay, I recommend it.","text":"If you haven't read Sustainable Energy Without the Hot Air , by David Mackay, I recommend it. Free download: [LINK] About the author: David MacKay FRS is the Regius Professor of Engineering at the University of Cambridge. He studied Natural Sciences at Cambridge and then obtained his PhD in Computation and Neural Systems at the California Institute of Technology. He returned to Cambridge as a Royal Society research fellow at Darwin College. He is internationally known for his research in machine learning, information theory, and communication systems, including the invention of Dasher, a software interface that enables efficient communication in any language with any muscle. He was appointed a Lecturer in the Department of Physics at Cambridge in 1995 and was a Professor in the Department of Physics from 2003 to 2013. Since 2005, he has devoted much of his time to public teaching about energy. He is a Fellow of the Royal Society. Nine months after the publication of 'Sustainable Energy - without the hot air', David MacKay was appointed Chief Scientific Advisor to the Department of Energy and Climate Change. TED talk: [LINK] Harvard talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12609139,"author":"yan","month":"2016-09","story":"Atmospheric carbon dioxide passes 400ppm","stance":"rec","why":"Approachable talk by the author of Sustainable Energy Without the Hot Air","quote":"The author also gave a talk a few years ago that is very approachable[2].","text":"I recently started reading \"Sustainable Energy Without The Hot Air\"[1], which is available as a free book, and it's been a wonderful introduction to reasoning about alternative energy. The author also gave a talk a few years ago that is very approachable[2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9516810,"author":"IkmoIkmo","month":"2015-05","story":"Output of Dutch solar bike lane exceeds expectations","stance":"reference","why":"Surface area is a tricky challenge for fully sustainable energy","quote":"surface area is a very tricky challenge [0]","text":"Very good point. Plus even if the marginal cost is lower than a dedicated solar facility, we don't necessarily have that luxury. We do if we want just 5% sustainable energy, not a problem. But if we want 99% sustainable energy, surface area is a very tricky challenge [0] and so it'd be a matter of the one and the other, instead of the one or the other. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["FlApbxLz6pA",1],["XpQMgbDJPok",1],["pX8BXH3SJn0",1],["OaGp5_Sfbss",1],["X8aWBcPVPMo",1],["nUBtKNzoKZ4",1],["3nwwKbM_vJc",1],["5zkC9IMQmYA",1]]},"KbB0FjPg0mw":{"wy":"Recommended on Ask HN threads as a high-quality free probability course for relearning math or building an ML foundation.","q":{"x":"I have used and can vouch for the high quality of all of them","i":14360161,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14360161,"author":"pixelperfect","month":"2017-05","story":"Ask HN: What's the best way to relearn math?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Harvard probability course lectures, vouched for as high quality, with a companion problem book.","quote":"I have used and can vouch for the high quality of all of them","text":"Here are some excellent YouTube playlists for the subjects you mentioned. I have used and can vouch for the high quality of all of them: Linear Algebra: [LINK] Probability: [VIDEO] Calculus: [LINK] Be sure to work through problems and not just watch lectures, because otherwise you'll forget the content. For example, the linear algebra course has a companion site with problems, and the professor of the probability course wrote an excellent book with problems."},{"id":18429429,"author":"pedrodelfino","month":"2018-11","story":"Ask HN: Book which comprehensively covers probability theory?","stance":"rec","why":"Full free Harvard probability lecture series accompanying the Blitzstein and Hwang book.","quote":"You can watch all classes from Harvard here for free","text":"I recommend \"Introduction to Probability\" by Joseph K Blitzstein and Jessica Hwang. This book is tough. Really tough. However, you are really going to do \"deliberate practice\" while having a go on the assignments. It bothers me that the author does not provide the answer to most of the questions. This is specially bad in this field of Mathematics (Probability). As an example, in calculus you can always plot the curve, the derivative and see if the result makes sense, in probability theory it is hard to have a simple and safe sanity check. You can watch all classes from Harvard here for free: [VIDEO] If you want, there is also a MOOC in EdX with the same material: [LINK] Sheldon Ross' book is also a good one. It was mentioned here. I specially like the section on theoritical problems, only with exercises on proofs. Every chapter has one. However, Joe Blitzstein problems are more challenging and will train you more on intuition."},{"id":16168374,"author":"neel8986","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: How to Seriously Start with Machine Learning and AI","stance":"rec","why":"Harvard probability course as part of a math foundation for machine learning.","quote":"Probability (Harvard )","text":"This is kind of a masters degree course i created for myself to get knowledge of Machine Learning from bottoms up First, you need a strong mathematical base. Otherwise, you can copy paste an algorithm or use an API but you will not get any idea of what is happening inside Following concepts are very essential 1) Linear Algebra (MIT [LINK] ) 2) Probability (Harvard [VIDEO] ) Get some basic grasp of machine learning. Get a good intuition of basic concepts 1) Andrew Ng coursera course ( [LINK] ) 2) Tom Mitchell book ( [LINK] ) Both the above course and book are super easy to follow. You will get a good idea of basic concepts but they lack in depth. Now you should move to more intense books and courses You can get more in-depth knowledge of Machine learning from following sources 1)Nando machine learning course ( [LINK] ) 2)Bishops book ( [LINK] ) Especially Bishops book is really deep and covers almost all basic concepts. Now for recent advances in Deep learning. I will suggest two brilliant courses from Stanford 1) Vision ( [LINK] ) 2) NLP ( [LINK] ) The Vision course by Karparthy can be a very good introduction to Deep learning. Also, the mother book for deep learning ( [LINK] )is good"}],"r":[["NfnWJUyUJYU",1],["OQQ-W_63UgQ",1],["dEaecUuEqfc",1],["T647CGsuOVU",1]]},"PBFPJ3_6ZWs":{"wy":"HN links it in any LED Christmas light discussion as the explanation of why they look off and how to fix it.","q":{"x":"we can't talk about LED christmas lights without linking to this video by tech connections","i":42361921,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42361921,"author":"beala","month":"2024-12","story":"Ask HN: What are the best programmable holiday lights?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why narrowband LED lights look radioactive versus incandescent.","quote":"we can't talk about LED christmas lights without linking to this video by tech connections","text":"This is a tangent, but I feel like we can't talk about LED christmas lights without linking to this video by tech connections: [VIDEO] the tl;dw is that most color LED lights work by emitting a single frequency of light. Compared to classic christmas lights, which are incandescent filaments filtered through dyed glass, the LED lights look a bit... radioactive. My personal pet peeve are white LED lights. If you're not careful to buy the ones labeled \"warm\" they look cold, and imo, not very festive and inviting. Of course, this is all a matter of taste, but once I became privy to the difference, I could no longer ignore it. I guess I'm just a millennial nostalgic for the lights of my youth."},{"id":39319642,"author":"bseidensticker","month":"2024-02","story":"It was almost impossible to make the blue LED [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why blue LEDs look too blue; white light in blue plastic is better.","quote":"White light in blue plastic is where it's at.","text":"The blue is nicer if you do that. Technology Connections has made 3 videos over the last 5 years mostly centering around how he hates the blue led lights used in holiday lights. I think I've only seen the 2 yr old one, but now that I have I can't unsee it. The blue is just too blue. If you see a set that is all blue instead of multi-color it's unbearable. It's just too blue. White light in blue plastic is where it's at. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":27861474,"author":"nyanpasu64","month":"2021-07","story":"Why we're blind to the color blue","stance":"rec","why":"Filtering white LEDs to make less garish holiday lights.","quote":"he filtered white ones","text":"Technology Connections did some videos on \"Making Holiday Lights Less Garish\", where instead of using narrowband colored LEDs he filtered white ones: [VIDEO]"}]},"bZQun8Y4L2A":{"wy":"Recommended as the clearest overview of how LLM assistants are built and how to use them well.","q":{"x":"It changed the way I use GPT-4.","i":36207905,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36207905,"author":"sagarpatil","month":"2023-06","story":"GPT Best Practices","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how LLMs work with good prompting ideas","quote":"It changed the way I use GPT-4.","text":"I would highly recommend watching - State of GPT video by Karpathy at Microsoft Build to get an idea of how LLM’s work. There are some really good prompting ideas. It changed the way I use GPT-4. Video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42913862,"author":"tintor","month":"2025-02","story":"RLHF Book","stance":"rec","why":"Beginner-friendly explanation of the stages of LLM training","quote":"Explains many details in a form suitable for beginners.","text":"Thanks. Here is presentation by Karpathy explaining different stages of LLM training. Explains many details in a form suitable for beginners. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42259017,"author":"tikkun","month":"2024-11","story":"Ask HN: Recommendation for a SWE looking to get up to speed with latest on AI","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among resources for getting up to speed on LLMs","quote":"State of GPT by Karpathy","text":"It sounds like you want more broad stuff, not necessarily learning how to train models. More like learning to use them and how they work. [LINK] and Hacker's Guide to LLMs by Jeremy from Fast.ai - [LINK] State of GPT by Karpathy - [VIDEO] LLMs by 3b1b - [LINK] Visualizing transformers by 3b1b - [LINK] How ChatGPT trained - [LINK] AI in a nutshell - [LINK] How Carlini uses LLMs - [LINK] For staying updated: X/Twitter & Bluesky. Go and follow people that work at OpenAI, Anthropic, Google DeepMind, and xAI. Podcasts: No Priors, Generally Intelligent, Dwarkesh Patel, Sequoia's \"Training Data\""},{"id":36188527,"author":"mcint","month":"2023-06","story":"It’s infuriatingly hard to understand how closed models train on their input","stance":"rec","why":"Deep summary of GPT training phases and techniques","quote":"provides a through introduction to the process used to train","text":"I share many of your concerns and frustrations, although I suspect what you're asking for their consider a moat along the lines of a trade secret, rivaled only by the collection of performance improvement techniques they've amassed in 1000s-10,000s of training runs, 100s of engineers, and (hundreds of?) millions spent on compute. People are hired and praised in the community for their skill in cleaning data. A non-answer for you, but for curious others, [State of GPT] 10 days ago provides a through introduction to the process used to train, Karpathy speaking at a Microsoft event providing a deep summary review of concepts, training phases, and techniques proving useful in the world of Generative Pretrained Transformers. [State of GPT]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36197725,"author":"valgaze","month":"2023-06","story":"A Prompt Pattern Catalog to Enhance Prompt Engineering with ChatGPT","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long Karpathy talk listed among good prompting guides","quote":"(~hr) Karpathy talk","text":"I recognize there's plenty of catnip here when it comes to calling this \"engineering\" or not, however, whatever you want to call it (prompt fiddling?), the techniques are crucial if you want to achieve reasonably consistent output from current-state LLMs. As models improve concerns about context window limitations will be reduced and it will be easier to discern user intent. These are good straight-to-the-point guides: - Prompt Engineering by BrexHQ: [LINK] - OpenAI guidance: [LINK] - [LINK] - (great examples): [LINK] - (~hr) Karpathy talk: [VIDEO] tl;dr: - Begin with the best and most forgiving model available, optimizing it later if necessary. - Write your prompts in a clear, specific, and detailed manner - Be mindful, however, of the tradeoff between including more detail and increasing latency or cost. - If you're doing any complex reasoning, ask the model to show its work, help \"stretch out\" computation over more tokens - \"Escape\" any included or quoted text properly to avoid confusing the model. - Keep track of your token budget, considering the context window in conversations and response length. - Employ an iterative process of measuring, adjusting, and improving your prompt engineering techniques."},{"id":36528862,"author":"jsight","month":"2023-06","story":"The future of AI according to thousands of forecasters","stance":"rec","why":"Karpathy explains LLM reasoning and prompting well","quote":"Karpathy explains it far better than I did in his","text":"Karpathy explains it far better than I did in his \"State of GPT\" talk about a month ago: [VIDEO] Yes, language can do that, but books and other texts, on average, leave out a lot of steps that humans have learned to do naturally. They can be prompted to do them, and their answers improve, indicates that there is definitely some level of \"reasoning\" there. But it has weird limitations in its current forms. I expect that to improve pretty quickly, tbh."},{"id":39074086,"author":"xianshou","month":"2024-01","story":"Self-Rewarding Language Models","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy suggested AlphaGo-style tree search for LLMs","quote":"","text":"Last May, Karpathy mentioned that the next step in LLM performance would likely take inspiration from AlphaGo's Monte Carlo-style tree traversal to enable deeper and more self-correcting reasoning: [VIDEO] But if this approach holds up, it suggests that the most valuable part of the AlphaGo project that applies to LLM development is in fact reinforcement learning through self-play. Why not create a leaderboard of reward-generating language models that constantly \"play\" against each other, where model selection frequency is based on ELO, and update on the results? What if the Open LLM leaderboard ( [LINK] ) evolved into a constantly improving pool of such models? This also alleviates data scaling issues by providing a diverse and continuously changing distribution of new training inputs."},{"id":38639017,"author":"sk11001","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: Give 8 year old access to Chat GPT?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows extra training phases that make base models assistants","quote":"","text":"> They just produce something that looks like a human wrote it real good. That's what the base model does. To get an LLM assistant there are additional training phases to make it conversational, helpful and more correct - [VIDEO]"},{"id":37294081,"author":"stared","month":"2023-08","story":"Google Gemini Eats the World","stance":"reference","why":"Training process matters beyond compute","quote":"","text":"Promises are cheap - even if made by researchers and engineers deeply believing that they can beat OpenAI. Let alone any assurances given by PR or marketing departments. And don't fool ourselves - everything announced publicly is prepared or at least approved by such. Computational power alone is not the only resource. It is also the training process itself (see this talk by Andrej Karpathy, [VIDEO] , at 1 min) and, obviously, data and its quality. I will be convinced only after Google demonstrates that Gemini is better than GPT4 (in some, or all, tasks)."},{"id":36397293,"author":"ra","month":"2023-06","story":"AI: First New UI Paradigm in 60 Years, Jakob Nielsen","stance":"reference","why":"Source for LLMs retrospectively judging their own output","quote":"","text":"Kaparthy explained how LLMs can retrospectively assess their own output and judge if they were wrong. Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36134831,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2023-05","story":"Ask HN: Is it just me or GPT-4's quality has significantly deteriorated lately?","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy outlined GPT-4's training stages","quote":"At Build 2023, Andrej Karpathy outlined[2] how they trained GPT 4.","text":"Chat GPT 4 has ongoing training, such as using Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) to tune it to provide \"better\" responses, \"safer\" answers, and to generally obey the system prompts. There's a release every few weeks. Yes, I've noticed too that recently it has become very \"cagey\", qualifying everything to death with \"As an AI model...\". A paper[1] that took snapshots monthly mentioned that as the initial bulk self-supervised learning went on, the model became smarter, as expected. However, once the \"clicker training\" was imposed on top to make it behave, its peak capabilities were reduced. I'm not sure if it's in the paper or the associated video, but the author mentioned that the original unrestricted model would provide probability estimates using percentages, and it was a very accurate predictor. The later versions that were adjusted based on human expectations used wishy-washy words like \"likely\" or \"unlikely\", and its estimation accuracy dropped significantly.[3] At Build 2023, Andrej Karpathy outlined[2] how they trained GPT 4. Essentially, the raw model during training builds its own intelligence. Then there's three stages of \"tuning\" to make it behave, and all three are based on human input. Essentially, they had contractors provide samples of \"ideal\" output. Similarly, end-users could up-vote or down-vote responses, which also got fed in. My personal theory is that the raw models can get about as intelligent as the average of the consistent and coheren"}],"r":[["jzERXJgi5vQ",1],["P1IcaBn3ej0",1],["UzxYlbK2c7E",1],["IJEaMtNN_dM",1],["zjkBMFhNj_g",1],["jkrNMKz9pWU",1]]},"kYYJlNbV1OM":{"wy":"Recommended as part of a long lecture series on psychology, complementing biology lectures, and named when HN asks for the best lecture series.","q":{"x":"the original Personality Lectures (2017) of Jordan Peterson are really great.","i":34594625,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":21658982,"author":"kleer001","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: Why the Hate?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Part of 120 hours of lectures the commenter watched and digested","quote":"I can recommend over 120 hours of learning in the following (which I've watched and digested)","text":"Because the world, for the most part, is (and always has been) a dangerous place and hate has proven to be a very useful (highly conserved through evolutionary time) adaptation to help keep small and delicate bands of our ancestors safe for one more day. You can see this bias towards negativity easily in the structure of basic emotions. There is one positive affect (happiness) and waaaaay more negative affects (fear, anger, disgust, sadness) and only one neutral (surprise). And those negative emotions are all very distinct. If you want more detail I can recommend over 120 hours of learning in the following (which I've watched and digested). They'll walk you through the details from the bottom to the top. - Watch Robert Sapolsky's lectures: [LINK] Watch Jordan Peterson's Personality lectures: [VIDEO] and his Maps of Meaning Lectures: (or read the book or listen to the audio book) [LINK]"},{"id":34594625,"author":"lysecret","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What's the best lecture series you've ever seen?","stance":"rec","why":"Great original 2017 Personality lectures","quote":"the original Personality Lectures (2017) of Jordan Peterson are really great.","text":"I am not a big fan of his work anymore but the original Personality Lectures (2017) of Jordan Peterson are really great. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20932045,"author":"mrdoops","month":"2019-09","story":"Ask HN: How do you keep your programming motivation up?","stance":"rec","why":"Psychology perspective on personality, complementing biology lectures","quote":"Jordan Peterson's has lectures on Personality","text":"Trial and error as a programmer dealing with depression and anxiety accounts for a lot, but for resources I'd point you to Robert Sapolsky's lectures on Behavioral Biology (here: [LINK] ). Sapolsky's lectures are great for getting a Biology perspective to how human's operate. Jordan Peterson's has lectures on Personality (here: [VIDEO] ) which are also great and echo similar ideas from a Psychology perspective."}],"r":[["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["I8Xc2_FtpHI",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["WEDIj9JBTC8",1]]},"oI_X2cMHNe0":{"wy":"HN cites it for the field-based picture of power transfer, noting it was controversial at release but the experiment largely backed the creator.","q":{"x":"And why magnets work due to relativity","i":40772059,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":37573249,"author":"blueplanet200","month":"2023-09","story":"Why does water change the speed of electricity? [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Veritasium on how electricity actually works","quote":"from Veritasium is related and also a fantastic video","text":"\"How Electricity Actually Works\" ( [VIDEO] ) from Veritasium is related and also a fantastic video."},{"id":49106407,"author":"leonidasrup","month":"2026-07","story":"A.I. Companies Are Recruiting Electricians and Carpenters by the Thousands","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to how electricity works","quote":"Good intrpduction are Veritasium videos","text":"Good intrpduction are Veritasium videos: \"How Electricity Actually Works\" [VIDEO] \"The Biggest Misconception About Electricity\" [LINK]"},{"id":40772059,"author":"lugoues","month":"2024-06","story":"Why does current flow the opposite way from the electrons?","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of electromagnetic waves guided by wires","quote":"It was pretty controversial at release but it gives a good overview.","text":"Sort of, electricity and magnetism are two sides of the same coin, electromagnetism, so it's waves in the electromagnetic field with the wire acting as a wave guide. (as far as I understand) Veritasium has a video that goes over this. It was pretty controversial at release but it gives a good overview. [VIDEO] And why magnets work due to relativity [LINK]"},{"id":37638444,"author":"jerf","month":"2023-09","story":"How the Most Useless Branch of Math Could Save Your Life","stance":"rec","why":"Controversial electricity video later vindicated by experiment","quote":"the electricity videos he's done in the last year","text":"The video in question for reference: [LINK] Interestingly, when I saw that I figured this channel for a goner because he was going to turn to the darkside, but quite contrary to expectations I feel like his channel has actually blossomed since then. His older videos are fine, but often just explaining concepts that you can find a lot of other explanations on, but some of his recent videos have rocked the boat even among physicists and IMHO he was ultimately proved to be correct for them. I particularly point at his videos about how nobody has measured the one-way speed of light ( [LINK] ), which was well-known among people very very deep in the field of relativity but not very well known outside of that (even amongst physicists in other fields, let alone lay people) and the electricity videos he's done in the last year ( [LINK] , [VIDEO] ) which attracted a whole whackload of \"debunking\" videos, which were themselves wrong (the best \"bunking\" video, if I may call it that, is probably AlphaPheonix's: [LINK] , since he actually set up the experiment, albeit on a smaller scale). They aren't all of that caliber, but the knot theory video was very good too and not something I've seen a hundred times elsewhere, and the airships video that preceded that one clearly had a high degree of research put into it beyond just the hype."},{"id":44742529,"author":"ChadNauseam","month":"2025-07","story":"Problem solving using Markov chains (2007) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment showing the light turns on as claimed","quote":"he set up an experiment to test it and the light does turn on instantly as claimed","text":"> suffice to say that the lightbulb does not turn on immediately like he claims Many people said this, but he set up an experiment to test it and the light does turn on instantly as claimed: [VIDEO] > There second one takes a mathematical model for the path integral for light and portrays it like that's actually what is happening I know nothing about this. Is there a more accurate mathematical model available than the one he uses? Otherwise, I think it seems sensible to portray our best mathematical model as \"what's really going on\". And I didn't get the sense that light was \"choosing\" anything when watching the video, I got the sense that the amplitudes of all possible paths were cancelling out except for the shortest path (or something along those lines)"},{"id":43955906,"author":"westurner","month":"2025-05","story":"Brandon's Semiconductor Simulator","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment on signal delay in circuits","quote":"I learned this gap in our understanding of electron behavior from this experiment","text":"Thanks, quite the useful simulator; I hadn't found that page yet. Additional considerations for circuit simulators: What does the simulator say about signal delay and/or propagation in electronic circuits and their fields? How long does it take for a lightbulb to turn on after a switch is thrown, given the length of the circuit and the real distance between points in it? (I learned this gap in our understanding of electron behavior from this experiment, which had never been done FWIU: \"How Electricity Actually Works\" (2022) [VIDEO] ) FWIW, additionally: Hall Effect and Quantum Anomalous Hall Effect; \"Tunable superconductivity and Hall effect in a transition metal dichalcogenide\" (2025) [LINK] ScholarlyArticle: \"Moiré-driven topological electronic crystals in twisted graphene\" (2025) [LINK] NewsArticle: \"Anomalous Hall crystal made from twisted graphene\" (2025) [LINK] From \"Single-chip photonic deep neural network with forward-only training\" [LINK] : \"Fractional quantum anomalous Hall effect in multilayer graphene\" (2024) [LINK] \"Coherent interaction of a-few-electron quantum dot with a terahertz optical resonator\" (2023) [LINK] .. [LINK] > \"Room-temperature quantum coherence of entangled multiexcitons in a metal-organic framework\" (2024) [LINK] Electrons (and photons and phonons and other fields of particles) are more complex than that though."},{"id":41552476,"author":"ninetyninenine","month":"2024-09","story":"How to succeed in MrBeast production (Leaked PDF)","stance":"reference","why":"Controversial video where the creator turned out right","quote":"generated a lot of controversy and he ended up being right","text":"agreed. This one: [VIDEO] generated a lot of controversy and he ended up being right. Hence the criticism. It's almost like the Monty Hall problem for Marilyn vos savant where even people with PhDs derided her for being wrong when in fact they were all wrong themselves."},{"id":40012439,"author":"westurner","month":"2024-04","story":"Replacing Wires Could Double How Much Electricity the US Grid Can Handle","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment testing signal timing over long cable","quote":"Veritasium's experimental video with CalTech with like a mile of cable","text":"Transmission line > Matrix parameters > Variable definitions: [LINK] But, FWIU how this applies to line length and transmission time hadn't been tested until Veritasium's experimental video with CalTech with like a mile of cable: [VIDEO] So, it doesn't need multiple length parameters."},{"id":38780801,"author":"antihipocrat","month":"2023-12","story":"Veritasium: The SAT Question Everyone Got Wrong [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Follow-up recreating the experiment and apologising","quote":"In response to all the controversy he made a follow up video [0].","text":"In response to all the controversy he made a follow up video [0]. In the video he recreated the experiment, explaining the effect in a more technically correct manner and also apologised for not doing so on the original. He also made it clear that the light globe in the original video did not receive enough current to light up.. it was hooked up to an alternative power source and an oscilloscope and upon receiving any signal above baseline would switch on the globe. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38444062,"author":"ben_w","month":"2023-11","story":"Metals: Where Electricity May Flow Without Electrons","stance":"reference","why":"Power transfers via electric fields","quote":"TL;DW: the power is transferred by the electric fields","text":"There was a whole mess of STEM YouTubers arguing about this last year, e.g.: [VIDEO] TL;DW: the power is transferred by the electric fields, it's the electric fields which move the electrons, if there's a break in the circuit the electrons necessarily accumulate in the places that minimise the electric fields."},{"id":36979314,"author":"cogman10","month":"2023-08","story":"LK99: Team of Southeast University observed zero resistance below 110 K","stance":"reference","why":"Counterintuitive effects of magnetic fields in conductors","quote":"Here's a video discussing some of the implications of this effect","text":"> Is there any understanding as to why superconductivity breaks down at higher amperage? This is a good read [1] > As long as the induced magnetic field at the edges is less than the critical field, the material remains superconducting, but at higher currents, the field becomes too strong and the superconducting state is lost. This limit on current density has important practical implications in applications of superconducting materials – despite zero resistance they cannot carry unlimited quantities of electric power. Tl;Dr (and probably wrong) as current flows through any conductor it creates a magnetic field. In superconductors when that magnetic field gets too strong it impedes current from being able to flow. A little like a traffic wave [2]. Everything works fine so long as there's enough space between cars to allow for them to speed up and slow down, but as the density of the cars increases if someone slows down that has a reverberating effect down the chain. The magnetic field on a superconductor in turn induces a current on the conductor in the opposite direction. Here's a video discussing some of the implications of this effect in a way that seems counter intuitive :) [3] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36929047,"author":"H8crilA","month":"2023-07","story":"U.S. Marine Corps Antenna Handbook (1999) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment on how energy flows in circuits","quote":"You can invalidate the circuit theory with, well perhaps not $50 worth of hardware, but a bit more","text":"You can invalidate the circuit theory with, well perhaps not $50 worth of hardware, but a bit more: [VIDEO] I'm not sure how do you plan to invalidate Maxwell's theory, which shows that energy flows entirely outside of wires, with $50. It would be very much welcome in the physics community. This misconception is even more widespread than \"planes fly because of Bernoulli\" because it's so incredibly useful when designing most circuits/PCBs. Though it is, fundamentally, a lie. PS. Circuit theory is made to match reality for the case showed in the video via the concept of a transmission line."}],"r":[["bHIhgxav9LY",2],["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"p8u_k2LIZyo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an approachable explanation that makes the famous algorithm finally make sense.","q":{"x":"This video is approachable and explains it pretty well","i":28452935,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27713767,"author":"greyfox","month":"2021-07","story":"Copilot regurgitating Quake code, including sweary comments","stance":"strong_rec","why":"More interesting video about the famous Quake code","quote":"Here's the very fun video if anyone wants to take a look","text":"Very interesting that this was posted as I literally JUST watched an even MORE interesting youtube upload about this very bit of code just last weekend. Here's the very fun video if anyone wants to take a look: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28452935,"author":"infogulch","month":"2021-09","story":"Bit Patterns of Float","stance":"rec","why":"Approachable explanation of why fast inverse square root works","quote":"This video is approachable and explains it pretty well","text":"Apparently the bit pattern of a float interpreted as an int is sorta-kinda it's own logarithm, which is why the famous fast inverse square root from quake 3 works: log(x^-½) = -½log(x) =~ -log(x) >> 1. This video is approachable and explains it pretty well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28270822,"author":"colejohnson66","month":"2021-08","story":"One does not simply calculate the absolute value","stance":"rec","why":"Explains float bits as logarithm, making Q_rsqrt make sense","quote":"Once that coincidence(?) was explained, Q_rsqrt finally made sense.","text":"> The bits of a float as an integer literal are proportional to its log2 value (plus a constant), … I watched a video[0] a while back about that. Once that coincidence(?) was explained, Q_rsqrt finally made sense. [0]: [VIDEO] (~20 minutes)"},{"id":32644901,"author":"bitwidget","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: What's a small example of some of the best code ever written?","stance":"reference","why":"Famous algorithm cited as great code","quote":"I've heard good things about the Fast Inverse Square Root Calculation algorithm","text":"print(\"Hello World\") /s But I've heard good things about the Fast Inverse Square Root Calculation algorithm in the Doom 3 source code. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5MxH1sfJLBQ",1],["2A2NY29iQdI",1],["j5v8D-alAKE",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["Ecy1FBdCRbQ",1],["htYh-Tq7ZBI",1],["Y9FZ4igNxNA",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["ne99laPUxN4",1],["ayh0qebfD2g",1],["cIQ9IXSUzuM",1],["w2A8q3XIhu0",1]]},"r0plv_nIzsQ":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend the channel as a clear, well-made source on quantum mechanics and common misconceptions about Bohmian mechanics.","q":{"x":"I love this series, Looking Glass Universe, on the subject [0,1].","i":17772219,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17772219,"author":"kjeetgill","month":"2018-08","story":"Do electrons think? (1949)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series covering common misconceptions about Bohmian mechanics.","quote":"I love this series, Looking Glass Universe, on the subject [0,1].","text":"I'm not super knowledgeable but I love this series, Looking Glass Universe, on the subject [0,1]. Specifically, she goes into some common but wrong beliefs about Bohemian Mechanics including that there is NO quantum weirdness. There is - it's called contextually. I don't know more than that. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":24091280,"author":"nmca","month":"2020-08","story":"How to become a good theoretical physicist","stance":"rec","why":"Good source for quantum and linear algebra.","quote":"Another good source for quantum and linear algebra in particular is \"Looking glass universe","text":"Another good source for quantum and linear algebra in particular is \"Looking glass universe\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":15106547,"author":"erikpukinskis","month":"2017-08","story":"Rumours swell over new kind of gravitational-wave sighting","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended physics channel as alternative to PBS SpaceTime.","quote":"Another great channel is looking Glass Universe -","text":"I really wanted to like PBS SpaceTime. So many interesting subjects and I love physics videos on YouTube. But there's something about Matt O'Dowd that makes them unwatchable for me. Something about the pacing, I just can't stay focused. Another great channel is looking Glass Universe - [VIDEO] And Udiprod doesn't have a habit of making physics vids, but this is my all time favorite physics videos on YouTube which explains quantum waves better than anything else I've seen - [LINK]"}],"r":[["wvfR3XLXPvw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["TH9VCN6UkyQ",1],["p7bzE1E5PMY",1],["9dKiLclupUM",1],["lqJUIqZNzP8",1]]},"u5um8QWWRvo":{"wy":"HN uses it as a go-to short critique of mandatory positivity in workplaces, startups and corporate culture.","q":{"x":"I strongly recommend you to watch Smile or Die, a talk by Barbara Ehrenreich.","i":8738068,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8738068,"author":"alecco","month":"2014-12","story":"Negative comments","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ehrenreich on delusional positivity culture","quote":"I strongly recommend you to watch Smile or Die, a talk by Barbara Ehrenreich.","text":"As a writer of critical comments of \"hacker\" school, I think this is typical of the times. If you call out somebody you are dismissed as a debbie-downer, cynical asshat, or other things like that. This comes from a culture now dominated by PR/marketing/sales, getting crazier by the growing viral marketing and astroturfing craze. I strongly recommend you to watch Smile or Die, a talk by Barbara Ehrenreich. [VIDEO] The parallel with that is a delusion SOLD to the community. In the case of \"hacker\" school I criticize it as I did with the \"MBA\" program by Seth Godin. And I consider that I might be _completely_ wrong in both cases, but what is telling is how aggressive people got with me for speaking up my opinion."},{"id":4870214,"author":"ak39","month":"2012-12","story":"Apple - Think Twice","stance":"rec","why":"Argues against forced positivity toward suffering","quote":"Watch this","text":"I disagree. The reason why we are often too eager to dismiss is because kindness takes effort. Empathy, and certainly sympathy, is hard work. We get lazy, we become exhausted and our reactions become snappy. Almost always the \"dry your eyes\" advice is inappropriate and insensitive. And it never really achieves its intended objective. I'm not saying we should all participate in crying orgies whenever someone shows signs of weakness (though, as I've aged, I think that is better than showing a cold shoulder) or that we readily invest in \"idiot compassion\". What I'm saying is: we are all trapped in this existential reality together and we all need to show kindness with all forms of suffering. Being cruel to be kind as a philanthropic philosophy has never worked (for me). Watch this: [VIDEO] (RSA Animate on \"Smile or Die\" a talk by Barbara Ehrenreich)"},{"id":2724567,"author":"MostAwesomeDude","month":"2011-07","story":"Dealing With (Not Dealing With) the Open Source Assholes","stance":"rec","why":"Critique of excessive positive thinking","quote":"for a much better summary of the problem","text":"My issue wasn't with the \"retarded\" line, actually; I was more concerned by this: \"If someone says something negative about your project in an unreasonable way, don’t take it to heart. There’s something good in every project (it’s open source, it already has one thing going for it), no single project is complete crap, keep the good things and learn from the criticism. All this boils down to basically 'surround yourself with good people.'\" This is the kind of thinking that lets people write bad code. \"Unreasonable\" is subjective here and lets people be very thin-skinned and defensive about their code. There is code which is complete crap and serves only as a warning to others to pay heed, lest they write code just as bad. (Feel free to consider that hyperbolic and point out that \"complete\" and \"serves only as\" are contradictory.) It's fine to surround yourself with \"good people,\" who I presume are people that are Good-aligned, but that's never going to reveal systemic weaknesses or flaws in your code; you're just going to end up as another group of people that relies on black hats to point out where your code was flawed. In other words, this entire post feels like thinly veiled positive thinking in excess. See [VIDEO] for a much better summary of the problem."},{"id":31326712,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2022-05","story":"Welcome to the era of the hyper-surveilled office","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for forced workplace happiness","quote":"","text":"> based on their facial expression Phrenology is still pseudo=scientific bullshit even when the calipers[1] are replaced with an \"AI\" black box of software. > Just need software that punishes employees who aren't happy enough at work, then we'll have a proper nightmare. \"RSA ANIMATE: Smile or Die\"[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19978413,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-05","story":"Gmail really wants me to say yes","stance":"reference","why":"Animation on forced positivity and the financial meltdown","quote":"RSA Animate made a short animation about how a culture of forced positivity in white color jobs","text":"> wage laborers are forced to wear a mask of positivity RSA Animate made a short animation about how a culture of forced positivity in white color jobs, and how it contributed to the late-2000s financial meltdown. \"Smile or Die\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":11464944,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-04","story":"Congratulations You’ve Been Fired","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as related to magical thinking","quote":"","text":"> when you got fired, it was called “graduation.” \"Smile or Die\" (RSA Animate) [VIDEO] This seems to be a variation on the Wall Street style of magical thinking."},{"id":7265825,"author":"alecco","month":"2014-02","story":"The Powerlessness of Positive Thinking","stance":"reference","why":"Explains problems of positivity as policy","quote":"explains problems arising when positivity is used as corporate or government policy.","text":"The TED talk \"Smile or Die\" explains problems arising when positivity is used as corporate or government policy. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6340266,"author":"jamesmiller5","month":"2013-09","story":"All LinkedIn with Nowhere to Go","stance":"reference","why":"Elaborates on the positive-thinking industry","quote":"are also brought up and elaborated on in the RSA Animate: Smile or Die","text":"The points covered about \"postive-thinking industry\" are also brought up and elaborated on in the RSA Animate: Smile or Die[0] I can't speak for all Influencers authors but Jeff Weiner in particular has a great and realistic commentary style while speaking person and to the employees at LinkedIn. It's a shame as I read the both \"Managing Compassionately\" and \"Three Pieces of Career Advice That Changed My Life\" that neither has the same impact compared to hearing those points as answers to difficult questions. As a fan of the slow-web I would like to see Influencer articles find a sweet spot between timely and long-form. For example, a lot of the posts give good information but don't weave that information into a memorable narrative of when and how to apply said advice. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5954099,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2013-06","story":"How Rap Genius Raised $1.8M in Seed Funding Without Knowing What We Were Doing","stance":"reference","why":"Cited against the idea that positivity is always needed","quote":"","text":"I'm sorry, I'm all in favour of loving what you do and agree that this generally leads to good results, but that interpretation frames the sentence in a completely different context than the original story, which is the following: > So fundraising is a psychologically trying experience that depends very little on any sober analysis of the quality of your product Smile or die, motherfucker! [VIDEO] > It's unquestionable that you need energy, excitement and confidence to be good at something. Anything from being a doctor, lawyer to professional athlete. Oh, that is VERY questionable. The first two are focussing on positive emotions and ignore the important role negativity plays. Smilarly, confidence does not in any way equate capability . > Projection (The act of projecting or the condition of being projected.) is going to be the only thing people can fall back on. No, that's just our intuitive mind being too lazy to do a rigorous analysis of the situation. I recommend \"Thinking Fast and Slow\" by Kahneman to fix this."},{"id":5677180,"author":"eliasmacpherson","month":"2013-05","story":"Why Anti-Authoritarians are Diagnosed as Mentally Ill (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Links sidelining of dissent to the financial crisis","quote":"Touches on the same idea of those who are anti authoritarian being sidelined without reason","text":"RSA Animate - Smile or Die [VIDEO] Touches on the same idea of those who are anti authoritarian being sidelined without reason, and enabling and exacerbating the recent global financial crisis."},{"id":4234438,"author":"b0rsuk","month":"2012-07","story":"Pay Too Much for everything","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as a tangential illustration of unpopular critical thinking","quote":"","text":"1. It's not always true that things that are more expensive are also higher quality. But the reverse is true: things of higher quality are more expensive. Because they're made of better materials, because they were grown naturally, because they were made of natural ingredients, because they were tested... better stuff is usually made with more effort or better material. 2. Because of #1, distinguishing genuine high quality stuff from a simple ripoff takes critical thinking. People don't like thinking too much, don't like analyzing, and critical thinking is unpopular especially in United States of America. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3018734,"author":"alecco","month":"2011-09","story":"Google+ Opens to All","stance":"reference","why":"Warns about the dark side of positivity","quote":"There is a dark side to overly positive thinking.","text":"There is a dark side to overly positive thinking. I'd settle for mostly positive with reservations and open discussion. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hpgK51w6uhk",1],["Z7Wl2FW2TcA",1],["kLiwvnr4L80",1],["ZWQ0591PAxM",1],["PJy8vTu66tE",1],["A4UMyTnlaMY",1],["gRRvjZ_XNog",1]]},"x7drE24geUw":{"wy":"Recommended as an excellent first education on CRDTs and their limits.","q":{"x":"I found this video from him to be an excellent first education on CRDTs","i":25776341,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25776341,"author":"rkangel","month":"2021-01","story":"Decentralised Content Moderation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent first education on CRDTs, their uses and limits.","quote":"I found this video from him to be an excellent first education on CRDTs","text":"The author is a researcher on distributed systems. I found this video from him to be an excellent first education on CRDTs, how they work, what they're good for and what they aren't (yet) good for: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42344977,"author":"williamstein","month":"2024-12","story":"Lies I was told about collab editing, Part 1: Algorithms for offline editing","stance":"rec","why":"Shows examples of technically correct but bad merge results.","quote":"This beautiful talk has some examples","text":"What they claim is that if all editing stops, then after a period of time everybody will be looking at the SAME document. This is what is meant by \"eventual consistency\". Achieving this in general is indeed a difficult problem, but (some of) these algorithms do solve it, though it can be tricky to correctly prove that they do. I agree that it is not possible to ensure that the document everybody is looking at is what they actually wanted it to be. However, there are some options for what happens, where some results may be technically correct -- we are all looking at the same thing -- but obviously really bad. This beautiful talk has some examples: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39134489,"author":"iamwil","month":"2024-01","story":"CRDTs Turned Inside Out","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among helpful introductory material.","quote":"Here's some introductory material I found helpful","text":"Ah, I'll put it in as a note in the beginning. You can see a more basic introduction in a previous post I wrote: [LINK] [LINK] Here's some introductory material I found helpful: - [An interactive intro to CRDTs]( [LINK] ) - [An introduction to state-based CRDTs]( [LINK] ) - [CRDTs for non-academics]( [LINK] ) - [CRDT: The Hard Parts]( [VIDEO] ) - [Readings in CRDTs]( [LINK] ) - [crdt.tech]( [LINK] )"},{"id":29434113,"author":"thomasikzelf","month":"2021-12","story":"Where is the CRDT for syntax trees","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses missing move operation for CRDTs.","quote":"Martin Kleppman (from automerge) talks about this in his talk \"CRDTs: the hard parts\"[0].","text":"We are almost there, for lists and structs there are CDRT's but a move operation across the data structure is missing. Martin Kleppman (from automerge) talks about this in his talk \"CRDTs: the hard parts\"[0]. I also think a range move or range delete is missing from many of these algorithms. A submission here on HN did implement a range delete on rich text (but I forgot what the name was). [0]: [VIDEO] EDIT: Ah actually Peritext (which you linked to) was the article I was thinking of. I think the range delete would be useful for regular lists as well."},{"id":25403289,"author":"sambroner","month":"2020-12","story":"Lessons learned from creating a rich-text editor with real-time collab (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on why solving rich text editing does not generalize.","quote":"","text":"Disclosure: I work on a related project (Fluid Framework) that makes application agnostic CRDTs easily available. The CKEditor folks did a great job building an OSS text editor and they did an especially strong work building a text editor that works well with collaboration. I've made a few OSS rich text editors work (mostly) with Fluid [0, 1] and the design of the text editor really changes how easily collaboration can be added. Luckily the text editor accepts remote changes, integrating an OT or CRDT solution is approximately the same. They also did a great job explaining the problem. My experience has been that people don't intuitively understand why real time collaboration is a tricky problem. I didn't. First of all, for a bunch of use cases websockets + redis + a last write wins conflict resolution (or some similar solution) is totally enough. Second, it's not immediately obvious why rich text editing requires more complex conflict resolution. Third, it's not obvious why solving the RTE problem doesn't immediately lend itself to other problems [2]. Because we started building in mid-late 2017, right as CRDTs really started to get competitive with OT, we chose a CRDT approach, but the collab problem space in general is much deeper I realized before getting into it. Anyway, I always appreciate seeing people explain their journey. Especially when it has to do with collaboration. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24619267,"author":"benrbray","month":"2020-09","story":"CRDTs are the future","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as a good CRDT reference.","quote":"Some more good references are [5,6,7]","text":"CRDTs seem very promising, but we still have a long way to go. The most exciting work in this area is being done by Ink&Switch [0]. They have a number of interesting real-world app prototypes based on CRDTs. - An interesting case where CRDTs failed is Xi-editor, where they tried to use CRDTs as the basis for a plugin system [1,2]. - One of the biggest problems with CRDTs is the overhead needed to keep track of the full document history. The automerge [3] project has been working on efficient compression of CRDTs for JSON datatypes. - The idea of monotonic updates is really appealing at first, but I was disappointed when I realized there's no good solution to handle deletions. Tombstones, to me, seem like kind of a hack, albeit a necessary one. Practically, CRDTs aren't the silver bullet they might seem like at first. - Another lesson learned is that when ten people are editing the same paragraph, there's not really a right answer. I think the key to implementing CRDTs is doing it at the correct level of granularity. - ProseMirror intentionally chose NOT to use CRDTs [4]. - Some more good references are [5,6,7] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] and [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] Kleppmann 2020, \"CRDTs: The Hard Parts\" [VIDEO] with HN Discussion: [LINK] [6] Kleppmann 2019, \"Interleaving Anomalies in Text Editors\" [LINK] [7] [LINK]"}],"r":[["5IUj1EZwpJY",1],["ZSRHeXYDLko",1],["R2Aa4PivG0g",1]]},"PaCmpygFfXo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a long but worthwhile way to demystify how language models work technically.","q":{"x":"It's long (2h), but well worth the time if anyone is curious about the technical workings of LLMs.","i":39224425,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40015385,"author":"georgehill","month":"2024-04","story":"Show HN: Next-token prediction in JavaScript","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Highly recommended pointer for next-token prediction.","quote":"Highly recommend this one","text":"You've made something interesting. Many people have already answered this here [1], so there's no point in me explaining it again. Dropping some pointers that will be helpful for you: - [LINK] - Highly recommend this one [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK] 1: ex: [LINK]"},{"id":39224425,"author":"islewis","month":"2024-02","story":"Markov Chains are the Original Language Models","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Builds a basic language model from scratch, demystifying LLMs.","quote":"It's long (2h), but well worth the time if anyone is curious about the technical workings of LLMs.","text":"I resource I found invaluable in demystifying LLM's was Andrej Karpathy's Youtube series. In particular, his \"Makemore\" video built a basic LLM in the same vein as OP's. It's long (2h), but well worth the time if anyone is curious about the technical workings of LLMs. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40988312,"author":"caprock","month":"2024-07","story":"The n-gram Language Model","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy's bigram project that the new course generalizes.","quote":"It appears to be a generalized version of the bi-gram project, which he links to here","text":"For reference, this project is from Eureka Labs, which is the new education startup from Andrej Karpathy. * [LINK] It appears to be a generalized version of the bi-gram project, which he links to here: * [VIDEO] It's cool to see him putting in even more effort on the edu work. It's a real gift he has, and was visible all the way back in his grad student projects."},{"id":39488921,"author":"chrsig","month":"2024-02","story":"GPT in 500 Lines of SQL","stance":"reference","why":"Moves from counting bigrams to a neural net at the one hour mark.","quote":"At the 1hr mark or so, he switches from counting to using a nn","text":"This is beautiful. I'd actually been going down this same rabbit hole with sqlite, I hadn't gotten far enough to bring a neural net into it. I'd been inspired by the makemore lecture series[0]. At the 1hr mark or so, he switches from counting to using a nn, which is about as far as I've gotten. Breaking it down into a relational model is actually a really great exercise. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"UGhfB-NICzg":{"wy":"HN uses it as the canonical example of rapid application development and object-oriented tooling productivity, though many note it is biased marketing.","q":{"x":"I don't know how realistic is this video but it was interesting","i":42129681,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41868911,"author":"lproven","month":"2024-10","story":"Hofstadter on Lisp (1983)","stance":"rec","why":"NeXT's controlled comparison of programming productivity against Sun","quote":"this 17min one is worth it.","text":"So very much this. I think programming languages are also long overdue for some controlled trials. They can't be blinded: any experimental subject bright enough to learn to program is probably going to know what language they are programming in. But trials comparing the effectiveness of different languages. How long they take to attain a specified level of proficiency, how long to learn enough to produce working code, and importantly, readability: for instance, how long it takes to find intentionally-planted bugs in existing, unfamiliar code. NeXT did this, way back in the 1980s, and Sun lost badly: [VIDEO] There is a writeup of some of it here: [LINK] But speaking as a non-video-liker, this 17min one is worth it."},{"id":33776765,"author":"rileyphone","month":"2022-11","story":"SwiftUI Is Convenient, but Slow","stance":"rec","why":"Biased but interesting NeXT marketing comparing AppKit with Sun C development","quote":"Obviously biased, but still an interesting look into the development environment","text":"There's a fun bit of marketing [0] NeXT did when AppKit was the new thing, comparing building a reasonably non-trivial application with it to a Sun workstation using C. Obviously biased, but still an interesting look into the development environment that among other things brought us the Web and DOOM. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13851064,"author":"indymike","month":"2017-03","story":"Was object-oriented programming a failure? (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows OO tooling productivity advantage over traditional development","quote":"Probably the watershed moment for OO was this NeXT video that showed the difference","text":"Started programming in 1982. Object oriented programming was a huge advance - especially in developer productivity. A side effect was the dramatic improvement in developer tools: gui builders, IDEs, etc all improved dramatically. Probably the watershed moment for OO was this NeXT video that showed the difference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48322692,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2026-05","story":"Let's compile Quake like it's 1997","stance":"reference","why":"1991 marketing video of NeXTSTEP development environment","quote":"Also here is what NeXTSTEP development environment looked like","text":"Many of those features were already available in MS-DOS and Windows 3.x IDEs from Borland for Turbo Pascal and C++. Which is why when I got into UNIX development felt like going into the stone age of development tools, thankfully XEmacs was already there. Which by the way, it was born for Energize C++, in 1993! [LINK] Also here is what NeXTSTEP development environment looked like, used for Quake tooling development, in a 1991 marketing video. [VIDEO] Which is why, I usually assert I cannot understand the nostalgia of CLI and TUI, being there at the time, and not being able to use some of these systems, due to the amount of money they required."},{"id":42129681,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2024-11","story":"Steve Jobs, NeXTSTEP, and early object-oriented programming (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Sun versus NeXT rapid development challenge","quote":"I don't know how realistic is this video but it was interesting","text":"I don't know how realistic is this video but it was interesting [VIDEO] A sun vs next rapid development challenge."},{"id":39848917,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2024-03","story":"Infinite Mac: Turning to the dark side","stance":"reference","why":"Glimpse of NeXT's Smalltalk-like code editing","quote":"You can also get a glimpse of it on the famous NeXT vs Sun commercial.","text":"Besides the sibling comment, that was kind of Smalltalk like, in the sense how code navigation and code editing was implemented. You can also get a glimpse of it on the famous NeXT vs Sun commercial. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36377996,"author":"sedatk","month":"2023-06","story":"Rust vs Zig Benchmarks","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a developer-productivity competition","quote":"Similar to that Sun vs NeXT competition back in the day","text":"We should have developer benchmarks for programming languages such as \"time to first successful compile\", \"time to first running copy\", \"time to find the root cause of a certain bug\". Similar to that Sun vs NeXT competition back in the day: [VIDEO] Maybe platforms like Leetcode and HackerRank can publish such statistics. I'd find such benchmarks way more interesting than comparisons of milliseconds for solutions to some arbitrary algorithms that we'll never use in real life. I'm not saying they aren't useful, but they aren't interesting."},{"id":32431180,"author":"rileyphone","month":"2022-08","story":"Byte Magazine Special Issue: Smalltalk (1984)","stance":"reference","why":"NeXT staged competition with Sun showcasing rapid development","quote":"NeXT promotional materials really liked to showcase its suitedness to rapid application development","text":"Still, the Web might have never existed without Objective-C, which is moreso my point. From [0]: > A second phase would almost certainly involve some programming in order to set up a real system at CERN on many machines. An important part of this, discussed below, is the integration of a hypertext system with existing data, so as to provide a universal system, and to achieve critical usefulness at an early stage. > (... and yes, this would provide an excellent project with which to try our new object oriented programming techniques!) TBL March 1989, May 1990 Objective-C had a wonderfully pragmatic approach to OOP, though it remained tied to the systems it was on because of the standard object libraries that enabled it. NeXT promotional materials really liked to showcase its suitedness to rapid application development, whether Jobs himself doing so [1] or in a staged competition with Sun [2] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25889957,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-01","story":"VisualAge for Java 1.0 – Let the Future Begin (1997)","stance":"reference","why":"NeXT's commercial hints at Solaris lacking a GUI builder","quote":"","text":"Just like the Web supports responsive design and not everyone actually uses it. For Motif I am aware of the one provided with Motif++, now gone from the Web, and the one provided by ICS ( [LINK] ). I guess Solaris might have lacked one indeed, given NeXT's commercial. [VIDEO] Sun was never good at desktop software anyway, killed NeWS, Swing while powerful requires graphics programming knowledge to make cool UIs and then there was F3 (JavaFX 1.0), and the Java Desktop Framework Integration project went nowhere."},{"id":25409741,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-12","story":"ARM MacBook vs. Intel MacBook: A SIMD Benchmark","stance":"reference","why":"Commercial emphasizing frameworks over UNIX tooling","quote":"","text":"What mattered on A/UX for application developers on Apple platforms was the System APIs layer ported to run on top of X Windows integration. Likewise, anyone doing NeXT development was focused on Objective-C frameworks all the way down to driver kit. As Application developer on a NeXT, the tune was all about WebObjects, Renderman, EOF. Applications like Lotus Improv, Wingz, and those being put out by Omni Group were the meat of the kind of applications that people considered to use NeXTSTEP, not BSD command line utilities. Pretty much patent on commercials like NeXT vs Sun. [VIDEO] Or Steve Jobs opinion on UNIX users, [LINK] and later when back at Apple [LINK] AIX is definitly UNIX, because it isnt' like A/UX, NeXTSTEP, OS X or iOS, where the UNIX layer is there more to bring stuff into the platform, while the main developer stack is something else."},{"id":24509116,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-09","story":"Nova by Panic","stance":"reference","why":"NeXT marketing piece building on Xerox-era concepts","quote":"You can see how NeXT builds on many of these concepts on the famous \"NeXT vs Sun\" marketing piece","text":"It is a bit hard to explain in an HN comment something that requires live experience, You can start by having a look at, [LINK] \"Eric Bier Demonstrates Cedar\" [LINK] \"Alto System Project: Dan Ingalls demonstrates Smalltalk\" [LINK] \"SYMBOLICS CONCORDIA ONLINE DOCUMENTATION HYPER TEXT MARKUP 1985\" [LINK] You can see how NeXT builds on many of these concepts on the famous \"NeXT vs Sun\" marketing piece, [VIDEO] Sun also had some ideas along these lines with NeWS, \"NeWS: A Networked and Extensible Window System,\" [LINK] Naturally OS/2, BeOS, Windows, macOS, iOS, and even Android share some of the ideas. Now, before I proceed note that actually modern Linux distributions have all the tooling to make these concepts happen, but it fails short to have everyone agree on a proper stack. So basically, the main idea is to have a proper full stack in place for developing a Workstation computer as one single experience, from bottom all the way to the top. On Xerox's case, they used bytecode with in-CPU execution via programmable microcode loaded on boot, and later on just a thin native glue on top of host OS. The environments had frameworks / modules for the whole OS stack, supported distributed computing, embedded of data structures across applications (OLE can trace its roots back to these ideas), REPLs that not only could interact with the whole OS (commands, modules, running applications), it was also possible to break into the debugger, change the code and redo the failed instructions."},{"id":23280792,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2020-05","story":"MacOS Catalina: Slow by Design?","stance":"reference","why":"NeXT commercial hardly touches UNIX-style development","quote":"Their famous commercial against Sun, hardly touches on UNIX like development.","text":"By writing system level macOS software , although I think you mean old style POSIX UNIX stuff. Here is a thing, already with NeXTSTEP, UNIX support wasn't never something worthwhile looking for, NeXTSTEP was used for its Objective-C tooling and frameworks, like Renderman and Improv. The UNIX stuff was just a solution for having a quick ramp up for their OS development, and just like Microsoft with Windows 3.1 NT, to have a tick in the box when selling to the government, Their famous commercial against Sun, hardly touches on UNIX like development. [VIDEO] You aren't going to see a CLI on that NeXTSTEP screen. Just like the SDK is all about Objective-C related stuff, even the device drivers were written in Objective-C. [LINK] The only fouls here are those that keep giving their money to corporations instead of supporting Linux OEMs, as Microsoft cleverly discovered. In fact, had either A/UX not been discontinued or Microsoft seriously supported their POSIX personality, Linux would never taken off, as the same crowd would be happily using these systems."},{"id":13783294,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2017-03","story":"Apple’s Devices Lose Luster in American Classrooms","stance":"reference","why":"NeXT commercial against Sun cited in UNIX heritage debate","quote":"","text":"> which is further evidence of the amazing foresight Apple had in basing macOS on an open-source core (BSD): NeXT did it, not Apple. And the goal of the time was just to help bring UNIX software into NeXTSTEP as they were competing against the likes of Solaris for market share. [VIDEO] The whole NeXT architecture has very little UNIX on it. [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":13950048,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2017-03","story":"Building a Command-Line Application with Crystal","stance":"reference","why":"One of several old videos on historic systems","quote":"","text":"If you enjoy reading documents, BITSAVERS.ORG is a treasure mine full of lost knowledge. [LINK] Here you will find Xerox PARC, DEC, Burroughs, Apple, Sun, Borland,... old research documents, papers, manuals. In the context of static compilation, for example in MS-DOS, take Turbo Pascal 6.0 programmer's guide. [LINK] For some 8 bit love you can check ZX Spectrum and Atari archives. [LINK] [LINK] Next step is to get an emulator for any of those old OS/computers and try to follow along one of those books. Also there are some videos on YouTube showing those systems. \"The Smalltalk-80 Programming System\" [LINK] \"NeXT vs Sun\" [VIDEO] \"Steve Jobs internal demo of NeXTSTEP 3\" [LINK] \"Amiga Programming\" [LINK] \"Amiga Hardware Programming\" [LINK] The Mother of All Demos\" [LINK] Just a very tiny sample of videos"},{"id":12438338,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2016-09","story":"An experience with Ubuntu Touch development","stance":"reference","why":"NeXT RAD tooling versus traditional UNIX development","quote":"Another good example is the NeXT vs Sun duel","text":"Another good example is the NeXT vs Sun duel, regarding the RAD tooling of NeXTStep vs the traditional UNIX development (1991). [VIDEO]"},{"id":12442868,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2016-09","story":"An experience with Ubuntu Touch development","stance":"reference","why":"1991 example of NeXT GUI development tooling","quote":"I would have rather used the GUI tooling of a NeXT","text":"You mean like this in 1991? [VIDEO] I would have rather used the GUI tooling of a NeXT, which eschews the UNIX traditions even though it builds partially on top of them, than the Solaris way of programming GUIs, if given the option. Just because I could only access green phosphor terminals, doesn't mean I wasn't aware of other, more expensive, options."},{"id":9341265,"author":"frik","month":"2015-04","story":"BeOS Demo Video (1998) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"NeXT versus Sun programming competition from 1991","quote":"Another interesting video is","text":"BeOS was great, but compare Steve Jobs' NeXT presentation (1992) to the BeOS video: [LINK] NeXTSTEP is the predecessor of OS X, the rest is history. BeOS filesystem \"BeFS\" had extended attributes (metadata) with indexing and querying features similar to a relational database and Bill Gates' vision of \"information at your fingertips\". Though Microsoft failed to complete Cairo-OS as well as WinFS. The BeFS main developer wrote a book about it, as it is out of print now he released it as PDF for free: [LINK] , [LINK] Check out HaikuOS, an open source BeOS reimplementation: [LINK] . And there were two BeOS inspired OS: ZETA-OS and SkyOS with some shady history: [LINK] , [LINK] Some of the BeOS vs. NeXTSTEP history is documented in Apple Copland OS article: [LINK] Another interesting video is: [VIDEO] ( In 1991 Steve Jobs' company commissioned an head-to-head programming competition to show how much faster and easier it was to program a NeXT computer vs a Sun workstation. The NeXT operating system went on to be the foundation for Apple's Macintosh OS-X about a decade later. )"},{"id":12333195,"author":"mistersquid","month":"2016-08","story":"NeXT vs. Sun (1991) [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the video's intended audience are managers and executives of software development teams","text":"@12:19: [VIDEO] > At this point, we threw the programmers an unexpected but not unrealistic curve. > Like a typical user, we asked them to add a feature not in the original spec. In this case, a button would recall all the trouble logs for a particular customer. > Using NeXT Step, the NeXT programmer completed the task in about 20 minutes. > [NeXT progammer discussing some details] > The sun programmer also estimated a time of 20 minutes, but it took about 45 before he was ready to test his version. This was about 20 hours in, which means that since they started on Wed, 30 October 1991 and assuming maximum 8-hour days, they have missed Halloween and would be at least halfway into Friday. The video states that the programmers finished on Sat, 2 November. The video goes on to state that the NeXT system enabled the NeXT programmer to add a number of features such as system fonts and button icons \"for free\", which goes to show that the video's intended audience are managers and executives of software development teams who would of course want faster development, ad hoc requests, and cost-free features. I personally would have been tempted to (╯°□°)╯︵ ┻━┻ once they announced a planned \"unexpected\" feature request because rather than train users and customers to frontload requirements in order to benefit from the design decisions that would be made as a result, the video encourages customers to believe their out-of-spec requests will be more quickly accommodated if only their progr"}],"r":[["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["3ka4KY7TMTU",1]]},"VLxTHYGLKY0":{"wy":"Recommended in threads on chip emulation as a look at how hard it is to emulate vintage sound hardware accurately.","q":{"x":"This video shows some insight into the development process","i":38562051,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38562051,"author":"H1Supreme","month":"2023-12","story":"Chipsynth C64 is an emulation of the SID so good, it can replace hardware","stance":"rec","why":"Insight into chipsynth MD's development process","quote":"This video shows some insight into the development process","text":"I have the Chipsynth MD, which is a Sega Mega Drive (aka Genesis) based synth from Plogue and it's one of my favorite plug-in synths. It's pretty remarkable how much it nails the sounds from those games. This video shows some insight into the development process: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36995081,"author":"andrewf","month":"2023-08","story":"Nuked-MD-FPGA – accurate Sega Genesis re-implementation based on decapped chips","stance":"rec","why":"Author describes difficulty of accurately recreating the Mega Drive sound chip","quote":"one of the authors goes into the difficulty of recreating things at","text":"chipsynth MD is a musician-targeted emulator of the MegaDrive's sound chips; one of the authors goes into the difficulty of recreating things at [VIDEO] (From memory: they sourced a variant of the sound chip with digital output, characterized the DAC that way, then built a test suite to compare emulator behavior with a real chip)"},{"id":33951826,"author":"dimatura","month":"2022-12","story":"Recreating the Sounds of the ’90s with a YM3812 Synthesizer","stance":"rec","why":"Creator explains emulating the Mega Drive's ladder distortion in chipsynth MD","quote":"The creator has a really cool video on it","text":"I'm an FM junkie and own various pieces of gear based on old yamaha FM chips, as well as actual yamaha FM keyboards. I think the various emulations out there are actually pretty good, and I have to admit I'd probably have a tough time with blind A/B tests. Probably the most noticeable (and sought after) out of the \"lo-fi\" effects is the ladder distortion effect on the old Sega megadrive YM2612 chips. I own a MegaFM synth with one of those and I do love the sound. I'm not sure if most emulators actually try to emulate it, but the plogue chipsynth MD plugin does. The creator has a really cool video on it [VIDEO] ."}],"r":[["MVEKt_H3FsI",1]]},"pdmODVYPDLA":{"wy":"HN links it as the accessible, hands-on demo behind the claim that Magic is Turing complete.","q":{"x":"Because Science did a thorough breakdown and demonstration, using actual cards","i":22970390,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":48500242,"author":"mckn1ght","month":"2026-06","story":"MTG Bench: Testing how well LLMs can play magic","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the cards used with extra explanation.","quote":"if you want to see the cards being used and a little more explanation","text":"Someone made a video based on the paper, if you want to see the cards being used and a little more explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38647206,"author":"bentona","month":"2023-12","story":"Magic: The Gathering Is Turing Complete (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Demo showing deterministic machine game state.","quote":"Here's a demo","text":"Here's a demo: [VIDEO] Didn't realize this before watching, but it's interesting that there's an incredibly complicated board state but the game state / actions are deterministic, e.g. the players don't have any choices about what to do once the machine is set up."},{"id":22970390,"author":"lukifer","month":"2020-04","story":"Accidentally Turing-Complete","stance":"rec","why":"Thorough breakdown and demo of a Turing machine in MtG.","quote":"Because Science did a thorough breakdown and demonstration, using actual cards","text":"For anyone curious about the details of building a Turing machine in MtG, Because Science did a thorough breakdown and demonstration, using actual cards: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21484946,"author":"cyberfart","month":"2019-11","story":"My Kid Lost a Game of 'Magic' to Its Creator But Scored a Piece of Its Art","stance":"reference","why":"Video on building a computer in Magic.","quote":"Because Science recently made a video about how such a game would look like","text":"Related to your comment, here's the paper [0] and Because Science recently made a video about how such a game would look like [1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["tIuj-oMN-Fk",1],["VPBH1eW28mo",1],["-6BsiVyC1kM",1],["LkH2r-sNjQs",1],["klpDHn8viX8",1]]},"xtvT2XYlcOY":{"wy":"Shared as a striking example of a fan car using powered ground effect, with an eye-popping launch, in EV and motorsport threads.","q":{"x":"Take a look at the Speirling at Goodwood Festival of Speed for an awfully mean fan car.","i":33184822,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":36002405,"author":"sokoloff","month":"2023-05","story":"If It Can't Spend $400M in F1 Anymore, Ferrari Might as Well Win Le Mans","stance":"rec","why":"Eye-popping launch and record-shattering Goodwood run.","quote":"Even the straight-line launch is eye-popping","text":"The McMurtry Goodwood performance is also impressive. Even the straight-line launch is eye-popping: [VIDEO] It shattered the previous record that had stood for 23 years."},{"id":33184822,"author":"ihunter2839","month":"2022-10","story":"Students break acceleration world record for electric vehicles","stance":"rec","why":"Mean-looking fan car at Goodwood using powered ground effect.","quote":"Take a look at the Speirling at Goodwood Festival of Speed for an awfully mean fan car.","text":"Yes, this car uses powered ground effects (PGE) to achieve the necessary downforce at low speeds (they mention this in an instagram post) In Formula SAE in the US, PGE systems are not allowed, but in the European equivalent they are. Take a look at the Speirling at Goodwood Festival of Speed for an awfully mean fan car. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32346071,"author":"jasonwatkinspdx","month":"2022-08","story":"Hyundai rolls out 27 heavy-duty hydrogen trucks in Germany","stance":"rec","why":"Fan car sets Goodwood hillclimb record with huge downforce.","quote":"Check out this fancar that set a new course record at Goodwood this year","text":"So, not exactly the ideas you're talking about, but the racing world is increasingly exploring outside of the aero that FIA/F1 et all have allowed. Check out this fancar that set a new course record at Goodwood this year: [VIDEO] For anyone unfamiliar, a fan car uses motor driven fans to actively create a low pressure zone under the car, while also reducing the low pressure drag bubble behind the car. This particular one is all electric and quite compact. It's very fast, and yes footage of it looks weird, almost artificial, because its sustaining grip through corners that rivals F1 cars, with the torque of electric. Running full tilt the fan system creates about 2000 kg of downforce. But the neat thing is you can flip a switch and then the thing just becomes a Nissan Leaf with a really crazy body kit. So some future \"track day\" car based on this concept would be surprisingly practical. I think the future of auto racing in a pure electric era is going to be surprisingly bright. Anyhow, not really relevant to talking about making big trucks more efficient, but thought you'd find it interesting."}],"r":[["xsLJZyih3Ac",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1],["7nL10C7FSbE",1],["5JYp9eGC3Cc",1]]},"DQEB3LJ5psk":{"wy":"Strongly recommended in nuclear-accident and weapons-history threads as an excellent documentary on how nuclear weapons were made safe and secure.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this three-part series produced by Sandia National Laboratory","i":42384380,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42384380,"author":"crmd","month":"2024-12","story":"The Albuquerque \"Broken Arrow\" Nuclear Accident","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Three-part series on Sandia's nuclear weapon safety and control design.","quote":"I highly recommend this three-part series produced by Sandia National Laboratory","text":"For any engineer interested in nuclear weapons, I highly recommend this three-part series produced by Sandia National Laboratory on their development of Permissive Action Link, the warhead’s electrical isolation and cryptological module that ensures US nuclear weapons will detonate if any only if authorized by the President. It’s fascinating how they thought about bad path scenarios like thermal damage causing carbon tracking of PCBs potentially defeating the control system. It is called “Always/Never: The Quest for Safety, Control, and Survivability” Part 1: [VIDEO] Part 2: [LINK] Part 3: [LINK]"},{"id":48237016,"author":"generuso","month":"2026-05","story":"Lost Images from the 1945 Trinity Nuclear Test Restored","stance":"rec","why":"Covers technology and history of nuclear device safety.","quote":"There is an excellent three part documentary from Sandia National Labs","text":"There is an excellent three part documentary from Sandia National Labs: \"Always/Never: The Quest for Safety, Control, and Survivability.\" (part 1) [VIDEO] They cover both the technology itself and its history, including the incidents you are reading about. These people are the ones who developed the methodology and the technology for nuclear device safety, or at least a significant chunk of it. I think it has recently become much more mathematically heavy, with zero knowledge proofs and other fancy stuff used to talk to the locks in the devices."},{"id":25908902,"author":"Mindless2112","month":"2021-01","story":"Remembering the night two atomic bombs fell on North Carolina","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on nuclear weapons safety and security work.","quote":"work for the safety and security of nuclear weapons.","text":"> \"Always/Never\" shares the story of the national labs’ history of work for the safety and security of nuclear weapons. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["j-vJxez9UF8",1],["nM9f0W2KD5s",1]]},"QHnLmvDxGTY":{"wy":"HN cites it as a formative talk on what OOP really is, and on why teams write code that slows them down.","q":{"x":"This talk was really formative for me","i":40663465,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40663465,"author":"mrkeen","month":"2024-06","story":"Don't Refactor Like Uncle Bob","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues databases shouldn't be the architecture center; OOP is encapsulation, polymorphism, inheritance.","quote":"This talk was really formative for me","text":"> it's both easier and more profitable to tell people that they need to OOP better than to use more appropriate paradigms in the design of their code. I don't think this is quite on target. Sure, he needs to go where the people are in order to communicate to the most people, but that doesn't mean he doesn't speak out against fads if it's appropriate. This talk was really formative for me: [LINK] If I remember correctly, he rails against just sticking the database in the middle of your system and calling that your architecture. If I'm remembering [VIDEO] correctly, he says OOP is no big deal, it's just encapsulation-polymorphism-inheritance, and then goes on to say things like C had better encapsulation than OO languages (you can keep your .c file to yourself, and just give someone the .h to program against.). And c-style bytes with stdin/stdout was designed with perfect forward-compatibility - programs like your terminal don't need to be recompiled even though they interact with your program (which was written after). And for the last 5+ years he's been pushing FP pretty hard, especially closure. I don't see him as pushing OO on people at the expense of anything else."},{"id":23198652,"author":"slifin","month":"2020-05","story":"Pretending OOP Never Happened","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on what OOP is and isn't.","quote":"There's a good talk here from Uncle Bob who helped popularise the solid principles, about what OOP is, and isn't","text":"There's a good talk here from Uncle Bob who helped popularise the solid principles, about what OOP is, and isn't [VIDEO] Towards the end of the talk note of the stickers on his laptop"},{"id":14733656,"author":"rimliu","month":"2017-07","story":"Introductory bullshit detection for non-technical managers","stance":"reference","why":"Uncle Bob's ironic remark on why teams write code that slows them down.","quote":"Why do we write the code that slows us down?","text":"To quote ironic remark from \"Uncle\" Bob Martin: > Why do we write the code that slows us down? > And the answer to that is \"Well, because we had to go fast! [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["IOiZatlZtGU",1],["Nsjsiz2A9mg",1],["vK1DazRK_a0",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1]]},"W4SxKWwFhA0":{"wy":"HN loves the Browser hacking series for its clear debugging method of reducing bugs to minimal reproductions, which people say influenced their own testing.","q":{"x":"At one point, he deletes half the HTML file to isolate where in the site the problematic code is.","i":35522475,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":33512288,"author":"hopfog","month":"2022-11","story":"Early stages of Google Docs support in the Ladybird browser","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear thought process reducing bugs to minimal reproducible examples.","quote":"I love Andreas Kling's \"Browser hacking\" series on YouTube","text":"I love Andreas Kling's \"Browser hacking\" series on YouTube where he fixes various issues Ladybird has with specific websites. E.g: Fixing a CSS layout bug found by chessboard.js - [LINK] Let's make \"Cookie Clicker\" playable in Ladybird! - [VIDEO] He's very good at articulating his thought progress and it's always really interesting to see how he reduces the bug down into a minimal reproducible example."},{"id":35522475,"author":"jonhermansen","month":"2023-04","story":"We're building a browser when it's supposed to be impossible","stance":"rec","why":"Shows isolating bugs into small test cases by repeatedly halving the HTML.","quote":"At one point, he deletes half the HTML file to isolate where in the site the problematic code is.","text":"Of course you are correct that supporting Twitter or any service is a moving target, so long as it changes. But that doesn't mean specific bugs can't be captured in a test case. Watch this video of Andreas doing exactly this, albeit for a simpler web app [VIDEO] At one point, he deletes half the HTML file to isolate where in the site the problematic code is. In a way doing a kind of binary search. After a few iterations of this, he comes up with a very small case that exhibits the problem he's trying to solve. It's clear he knows his way around the codebase and where to make changes, but isolating these test cases is probably as important. And presumably if you fixed enough of these issues (while following the specs), 99% of the modern web should work just fine."},{"id":41161802,"author":"_gabe_","month":"2024-08","story":"How I Program in 2024","stance":"reference","why":"Inspired testing approach: reproduce bug minimally, fix, codify as test.","quote":"I was inspired by Andreas Kling’s method of testing Ladybird","text":"I can sympathize with the authors love/hate relationship with tests, but I can’t help feeling like it’s because we as developers so often test the completely wrong things. I don’t typically write tests, but they do make sense for a few cases (specifically end to end tests that look for well defined outputs from well defined inputs). I was inspired by Andreas Kling’s method of testing Ladybird, where he would find visual bugs, recreate the bug in a minimum reproducible example, fix the bug, then codify the example into a test and make sure the regression was captured in his test suite[0]. This led to a seemingly large suite of tests that enabled him to continue modifying the browser without fear of regressing somewhere. I used this method of testing while I was writing a code highlighter that used TextMate grammars. Since TextMate grammars have a well defined output for some input of code + grammar, I was able to mimic that output in my own code highlighter and then compare it to TextMate’s output for testing purposes. I wrote a bunch of general purpose tests, then ran into a bunch of bugs where I would have mismatched output. As I fixed those bugs, I would add the examples to my test suite. Anyways, my code highlighter was slow, and I wanted to re-architect it to speed it up. I was able to completely change the way it worked with complete confidence. I had broken tests for a while in the middle of the refactor, but eventually I finished the refactor. As I started to fix the b"}],"r":[["prXuyMCgbTc",1],["W3aieHjyNvw",1]]},"cVaTc15plVs":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it, especially during the Google memo debate, as expert testimony on biological contributions to gender differences.","q":{"x":"","i":14940752,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14939737,"author":"jorgemf","month":"2017-08","story":"10 page anti-diversity screed circulating internally at Google","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues biology precedes history on gender differences.","quote":"(even better if you see it all)","text":"watch this: [VIDEO] (even better if you see it all) biology comes before history, history can be a consequence of biology > If men turn out to be naturally, as a group, less good at math than women, does that mean we should stop training men on math? No. We should train men more at math, because math is a valuable skill This is what scares me of this society. If someone is bad at something lets force him/her to improve at the things he/she is bad at. Instead of focusing on the things a person is good at and try to put them on the next level and make a difference that way, let's focus on the bad things and get a mediocre individual. I love maths, but are you saying art is not a valuable skill compare with maths? Should the great artist bad at math study math and give up in art? You might think that not training someone at something that he/she is bad at is stupid, but some people like me think the stupid thing is to no to focus in what make someone special and good at. And finally, why do you think there is a bias? Couldn't be the reason that there are not more female CS engineers that they choose freely not to be because they just don't like it? The answer is in the video I put before."},{"id":14940752,"author":"josephg","month":"2017-08","story":"About This Googler's Manifesto","stance":"rec","why":"Researchers on biological gender differences in interests.","quote":"The full documentary is great.","text":"Alright, I'll bite. But, I've never heard of any research suggesting women have a different cognitive capacity than men. I read some research a number of years ago suggesting cross-culturally women had better language skills than men, but I don't know if this is still considered to be true. I can't remember reading anything in the manifesto which suggested women are less capable as software engineers. The essay did suggest women were less interested in programming than men (obviously true - see enrolment figures) and that at least some of the effect size is due to biological factors (probably true - see below). --- None of these links suggest any capacity difference. Only that there is a biologically influenced gender difference in interests. Here's an essay contextualising and summarising the historical specifics of male and female gender roles in society: [LINK] Here's a study of how prenatal androgen effects psychological thing vs people orientation, and how it influences career choice: [LINK] A summary of studies looking at big 5 personality distributions and other effects. Of note is the long section describing blindspots in current research: \"Gender Differences in Personality and Interests: When, Where and Why?\" [LINK] Here's a Dutch documentary where they send a camera crew around asking different researchers about gender differences: [VIDEO] The full documentary is great. Their conclusion is that biological factors result in an overlapping but different distribution o"},{"id":14957536,"author":"jorgemf","month":"2017-08","story":"Google Fires Employee Behind Controversial Diversity Memo","stance":"rec","why":"Expert opinions on a controversial topic.","quote":"It is good to see different opinions of experts and what happened there","text":"There is a documentary called Hjernevask from Norway that had a lot of controversy because of the topic. It is good to see different opinions of experts and what happened there: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9393197,"author":"kristofferR","month":"2015-04","story":"Why Young Men Go to War","stance":"rec","why":"Norwegian documentary on gender differences in career choice.","quote":"This documentary is really interesting in that regard","text":"> “I have friends who are anthropologists and primatologists and evolutionary psychologists … [who are under] an enormous amount of pressure to generate data that shows that all gender differences are cultural.” This documentary is really interesting in that regard: [VIDEO] It's a Norwegian documentary from 2010 about \"Why do girls tend to go into empathizing professions and boys into systemizing professions? Why does the labor market become more gender segregated the more economic prosperity a country has?\" A lot of the scientists interviewed didn't even bother to consider evidence that innate sex differences exists."},{"id":17800948,"author":"sampo","month":"2018-08","story":"EU is planning to order websites to delete extremist content within 1 hour","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Norwegian documentary, actually.","text":"Norwegian documentary, actually. [LINK] [VIDEO] (The links in the Wikipedia article go to videos with a bit higher quality than the Youtube playlist.)"},{"id":14939698,"author":"jorgemf","month":"2017-08","story":"10 page anti-diversity screed circulating internally at Google","stance":"reference","why":"Favorite segment with a researcher's response.","quote":"This is my favorite part (her response)","text":"I already saw it and that is why I am asking people here. And thanks so much because I was searching for it and couldn't find it. This is my favorite part (her response): [VIDEO]"},{"id":14943281,"author":"callesgg","month":"2017-08","story":"The cognitive differences between men and women","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here is a interesting case","text":"It is common knowledge that we are all the same, just as it was common knowledge that the earth was flat a few 100 years ago. People don't argue for common knowledge, it is common knowledge. Here is a interesting case: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14943993,"author":"jorgemf","month":"2017-08","story":"The Full 10-Page Anti-Diversity Screed Circulating Internally at Google","stance":"reference","why":"Scientists discuss gender differences in the topic.","quote":"Most people in the video are scientists and work in the topic.","text":"> I've seen no one suggest that the professions have significantly shifted in nature due to an influx of female brains Here you have it: [VIDEO] Most people in the video are scientists and work in the topic."},{"id":14957625,"author":"jorgemf","month":"2017-08","story":"Google Fires Employee Behind Controversial Diversity Memo","stance":"reference","why":"Experts' views on biological gender differences.","quote":"Look what the experts say","text":"> Your two examples list physical differences, not mental ones. Look what the experts say: [VIDEO] If you see the whole video you hear different opinions > you don't have to be tall to [play basketball] Look at the height distributions of the global population and the basketball players and think twice of what you said. > There are no biological \"skills\" that men might possess innately that should make them a first-choice by default Exactly because those skill can also be in women, but some are more likely to be in men as some are more likely to be in women. I don't see anything wrong with it."},{"id":14970678,"author":"jorgemf","month":"2017-08","story":"The Google memo isn’t sexist or anti-diversity, it’s science","stance":"reference","why":"Gender equality paradox: more equal countries show less diversity.","quote":"This is very counter intuitive and needs an explanation (for some is biology)","text":"50/50 will imply to force people to join even when they don't want to. It works for politics because the number of politics is significative small compared to the total population. But the work force is really a big number and close the to total population. In more gender equality countries like Norway the ratios are the worse ones for things like tech. Before a huge measure like this one you should understand by gender equality countries trend to have less diversity than less gender equality countries. This is very counter intuitive and needs an explanation (for some is biology) -> [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AdKNCBrkZQ4",1],["N2jZryt607U",1],["a0atL1HSwi8",1],["aMcjxSThD54",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["bNfAAQUQ_54",1],["-6BsiVyC1kM",1],["Y65FRxE7uMc",1],["v3XcQtoja_Y",1],["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1]]},"1AjhFZVfB9c":{"wy":"HN mentions it as the author's own overview of Pixie, with an interesting implementation technique near the end.","q":{"x":"There's a talk by the author online worth it","i":13358243,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13358243,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2017-01","story":"How Lisp-family languages facilitate building bioinformatics applications","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by the author of Pixie","quote":"There's a talk by the author online worth it","text":"It seems so.. which makes my LLVM mention false. There's a talk by the author online [VIDEO] worth it"},{"id":12060857,"author":"lemming","month":"2016-07","story":"Carp: a statically typed lisp, without a GC, for high performance applications","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting technique near the end of the Pixie talk","quote":"It's a really interesting technique, and for a Lisp too.","text":"Also check out Pixie's - towards the end of [VIDEO] . It's a really interesting technique, and for a Lisp too."},{"id":14916288,"author":"souenzzo","month":"2017-08","story":"Hy – A Lisp-flavored Python","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Pixie is not dead, a weekend project","quote":"It's not \"dead\" It's a weekend project, for fun.","text":"It's not \"dead\" [VIDEO] It's a weekend project, for fun. Functions that are work will not stop to work. I'm using it and I pretend to contribute when I find a problem."}],"r":[["lpu-3UF_b48",1],["5bQhGS8V6dQ",1],["2-oBNh0UkQc",1],["sCZJblyT_XM",1],["Mfsnlbd-4xQ",1],["NVEgyJCTee4",1],["vuIlsN32WaE",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["f_0QlhYlS8g",1],["Jes3bD6P0To",1]]},"Eg_ToU7m1MI":{"wy":"Recommended as a condensed version of Kay's ideas on education and computing, showing kids using software to explore physics and robotics.","q":{"x":"You'll see kids using the software to explore robotics and do physics of gravitation.","i":692481,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":17990016,"author":"e12e","month":"2018-09","story":"Show HN: TechYaks – Best of 50k tech talks ranked by confidence intervals","stance":"rec","why":"Condensed Alan Kay talk on teaching ideas.","quote":"the much more condensed ted talk","text":"Maybe these are mostly too new, or you have different (more practical, hands on?) definition of tech-talk - but from a quick look the only speakers I expected - and found - were Sandi Mets and Rob Pike. If it's practical, I'm surprised not to see the js \"wat\" lightning talk (which I now can't seem to find...). If it's more general \"best of\", I'd expect something like Guy Steele \"growing a language\" : [LINK] Douglas Engelbart \"the mother of all demos\": [LINK] Alan Kay \"doing with images makes symbols\": [LINK] Or, if that's too long, the much more condensed ted talk: \"a powerful idea about teaching ideas\": [VIDEO] (Maybe that's not a \"tech talk\"?) Rich Hickey \"simple made easy\" : [LINK] To name a few of the top of my head."},{"id":692481,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2009-07","story":"The iPodTouch Is What The OLPC XO Should Have Been","stance":"rec","why":"Kids using Squeak to explore robotics and gravitation physics.","quote":"You'll see kids using the software to explore robotics and do physics of gravitation.","text":"It's not the \"toy\" part that's signifiant. It's the \"tinker\" part. Think of a 50's automobile with a carburetor and a distributor. You can take apart and tinker with those. In a 2009 car, the distributor and associated parts are a throwaway lump of plastic with metal tabs coming out. \"Explorable\" is perhaps an even better term. Squeak had the same aims. Skip to 8:20. You'll see kids using the software to explore robotics and do physics of gravitation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40733438,"author":"lioeters","month":"2024-06","story":"KidPix","stance":"reference","why":"Kay demonstrates Etoys, ancestor of Snap.","quote":"It's really an ancestor of Snap","text":"> all of these \"blocks\" editors sprouted from the original one I designed for Etoys more than 20 years ago now -- most of the followup was by way of Jens Moenig -- who did SNAP. You can see Etoys demoed on the OLPC in my 2007 TED talk. I was curious about this TED talk, and found the part where he demonstrates Etoys in the section called \"Personal Computing & Children Today\". Alan Kay: A powerful idea about teaching ideas [VIDEO] The way he interacts with a running program, moving statements around and editing them, observing the values of variables as they change.. It's really an ancestor of Snap, and I can see its influence on Bret Victor's talks like Inventing on Principle. --- Mike Travers' thesis, \"Agar: An Animal Construction Kit\" is an entertaining and thought-provoking read. > An animal construction kit is an interactive computer system that allows novice programmers to assemble artificial animals from simple components. These components include sensors, muscles or other effectors, and computational elements. They can also include body parts such as limbs, bones, and joints. A complete animal construction kit must also provide a world for the animal to live in, and must support the co-existence of multiple animals of different species. It reminds me of the book, Vehicles: Experiments in Synthetic Psychology. It's about modelling a world of autonomous agents with sensors and actuators, motivations and behaviors. [LINK] (EDIT: This book is listed in the bibliography of "}],"r":[["n691pLhQBkw",1],["PsPX0nElJ0k",1],["rE3j_RHkqJc",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["1AcEt073Uds",1],["RNFesa01llk",1],["IrbJYsep45E",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["sWgDk-o-6ZE",1],["p6K4iIMlouI",1],["eX9m3g5J-XA",1],["LiWlvBro9eI",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["EKWGGDXe5MA",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["dK3zRXhrFZY",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["Av0PQDVTP4A",1],["6wZmYMWKLkY",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1]]},"FV0DXNB94NE":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as a quick way to get a sense of what Elm does and what adopting it incrementally looks like.","q":{"x":"Watch this conference to get a sense of what Elm does.","i":14218226,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14218226,"author":"elviejo","month":"2017-04","story":"Ask HN: Is there a sane alternative to Angular and React?","stance":"rec","why":"Gives a sense of what Elm does","quote":"Watch this conference to get a sense of what Elm does.","text":"Excellent question. Turns out there is: [LINK] Is a Haskell inspired programming language that makes developing front end a delight. It helps to build Reliable front ends. Watch this conference to get a sense of what Elm does. Making the Backend Team Jelous[1] Plus the Elm architecture makes everything orderly and easy to understand. Quite a change from the mess of react components and Angular rewrites. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14218227,"author":"elviejo","month":"2017-04","story":"Ask HN: Is there a sane alternative to Angular and React?","stance":"rec","why":"Gives a sense of what Elm does","quote":"Watch this conference to get a sense of what Elm does.","text":"Excellent question. Turns out there is: [LINK] Is a Haskell inspired programming language that makes developing front end a delight. It helps to build Reliable front ends. Watch this conference to get a sense of what Elm does. Making the Backend Team Jelous[1] Plus the Elm architecture makes everything orderly and easy to understand. Quite a change from the mess of react components and Angular rewrites. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10547769,"author":"abglassman","month":"2015-11","story":"A sliding puzzle, built with Elm","stance":"rec","why":"Talk moving from React to Elm","quote":"these talks got me bulled up on Elm","text":"I'm pretty keen to play with Elm, but it gives me some pause that an app like this (or the demo at [LINK] ) results in... 11k lines of Javascript. Nevertheless, these talks got me bulled up on Elm: [VIDEO] (Richard Feldman, collegially, on React -> Elm)</a> / [LINK] (Evan Czaplicki on the motivation for Elm)"},{"id":10329481,"author":"dangoor","month":"2015-10","story":"Side Effects vs. Promises","stance":"reference","why":"Talk touching on Elm Tasks","quote":"Richard Feldman's Strange Loop talk[1] touched upon Tasks","text":"Yeah, Richard Feldman's Strange Loop talk[1] touched upon Tasks and they do indeed look exactly like what I'm looking for. Elm does look very nice. Not sure if I'd get the team on board with that kind of change ;) [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Av0PQDVTP4A",1],["Nbj1AR_aAcE",1],["-Ht4qiDRZE8",1],["dljNabciEGg",1],["oYk8CKH7OhE",1]]},"G-lN8vWm3m0":{"wy":"HN uses it as the standard demonstration that the brain reinterprets perception, in threads on speech, AI audio and illusions.","q":{"x":"The most compelling illustration I've seen of this is the McGurk Affect[1].","i":4274914,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":14926841,"author":"kkylin","month":"2017-08","story":"Can you make your brain not see this circle illusion?","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to the McGurk effect for illusion fans","quote":"If you like this sort of thing, and haven't heard of the McGurk Effect before, take a look at this","text":"If you like this sort of thing, and haven't heard of the McGurk Effect before, take a look at this: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":4274914,"author":"lunarscape","month":"2012-07","story":"Why You Won’t See Hard AR Anytime Soon","stance":"rec","why":"Compelling illustration of the brain fooling itself","quote":"The most compelling illustration I've seen of this is the McGurk Affect[1].","text":"\"People already lie to themselves\" The most compelling illustration I've seen of this is the McGurk Affect[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24030889,"author":"barbegal","month":"2020-08","story":"NASA Astronauts in SpaceX Capsule Make First Water Landing Since 1975","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates brain matching sound to images","quote":"This is sometimes known as the McGurk effect","text":"It was people clapping in mission control but your brain wants to think the sound matches the images so your brain tricks you into thinking it is the sound of water splashing. This is sometimes known as the McGurk effect [VIDEO]"},{"id":20152215,"author":"jml7c5","month":"2019-06","story":"Kill Instant Messaging","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why video chat is easier to understand","quote":"See the McGurk Effect","text":">For some reason I can easily understand people on video chat even if they have strong accent whereas I can't grok anything on a phone call. See the McGurk Effect: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17098420,"author":"zawerf","month":"2018-05","story":"A tool that lets you hear both Yanny and Laurel","stance":"reference","why":"Explains ba/fa illusion where lip reading changes heard sound","quote":"It is really cool that small contextual information can change our senses so dramatically without us being aware of it happening.","text":"Are you talking about the McGurk effect? You hear either Ba or Fa depending on which you expect to hear based on the lip reading (but the sound that is actually playing is Va): [VIDEO] It is really cool that small contextual information can change our senses so dramatically without us being aware of it happening."},{"id":16814637,"author":"anotheryou","month":"2018-04","story":"Looking to Listen: Audio-Visual Speech Separation","stance":"reference","why":"Shows visual cues strongly affect human speech perception","quote":"The McGurk illusion is so strong that I'm sure visual cues have a major role in error correction","text":"I wonder if we will have visual assisted tts. Humans do it: [VIDEO] The McGurk illusion is so strong that I'm sure visual cues have a major role in error correction or voice recognition assistance for humans."},{"id":11895447,"author":"anotheryou","month":"2016-06","story":"AI produces realistic sounds that fool humans","stance":"reference","why":"Shows vision shaping what sound we perceive","quote":"The human mind is very forgiving, especially when vision accompanies sound (McGurk effect [1])","text":"Fooling humans is much easier when about 50% of the sound is always accurate (in any case it's a wooden drumstick hitting something). The human mind is very forgiving, especially when vision accompanies sound (McGurk effect [1]) Furthermore this fixed variable makes the pool of samples to choose from much much smaller. Still impressive of course ;) just far from dubbing any video there is. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11111641,"author":"th-ai","month":"2016-02","story":"Bot-controlled piano emulating spoken voice (2009) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Analogy to McGurk effect","quote":"it's like the McGurk Effect","text":"great link thanks! it's like the McGurk Effect [VIDEO] , and why I time Vocal Text to appear just before you hear it [LINK]"},{"id":9095083,"author":"brute","month":"2015-02","story":"“Citizenfour” Awarded Oscar for Best Documentary in 2014","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates brain reinterpreting sound to match sight","quote":"Brief reminder that what you hear is being reinterpreted by your brain to match the things that you see.","text":"Brief reminder that what you hear is being reinterpreted by your brain to match the things that you see. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8811584,"author":"sho_hn","month":"2014-12","story":"Ways to learn a language as an adult?","stance":"reference","why":"Tangential illusion link in language learning comment","quote":"","text":"I originally started with web resources, but here's the stuff I credit with really advancing me properly, roughly in the order I read or used it: 1. TTMIK's Hangeul Master . It's really easy to pick up hangul basics so spending $20 on a book that's superficially just about the alphabet might seem silly. But the harder part to learn isn't really the symbols, it's the pronunciation. You need to get to grips with the various sound change rules that apply when various characters are adjacent to each other, and with the pronunciation differences between the normal and tense consonants, and so on. If you can't look at a word and confidently form its sound in your head you can't read or write efficiently, so this is crucial. TTMIK's book is really a solid package: You get the basics (the symbols, the stroke order, etc.) but also a good introduction to the sound change rules, not just on paper but also in really well-done supplementary audio resources and quizzes (where many books fail). Finally there's a second section on various Korean handwriting styles that I found surprisingly useful (Korean TV shows and other materials often use pen scripts that can be hard to read without spending some time with this). 2. To complement the above, the Korean Wiki Project has a lot of good sound files for various character combinations. 3. Billy Go's two Korean Made Simple books are just great. They're highly accessible introductions to core grammar and core vocab, with short, digestible chapter"},{"id":7926828,"author":"srean","month":"2014-06","story":"A Sound You Can't Unhear (And What It Says About Your Brain)","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in discussion of persistent perceptual illusions","quote":"","text":"It is really interesting and amusing how persistent certain 'illusions' can be even when one is exposed to it just once and for a short time. Fun story, this was a long time ago: I was interning at Google at that time. One day I tell my then cube neighbor about an interesting experiment on visual perception that I had read about. A professor at MIT had carried out experiments on his class. Students were asked to wear prismatic goggles that shifted their vision and then try to catch objects. Hilarity ensued, but soon enough the brain adapted to the shift. Same with inverting glasses, soon the students would not even realize that their vision was inverted. The fun part was when they took their glasses off, their motor reflexes would still compensate assuming that they were wearing those glasses. Much hilarity again. I was telling all this to my cube neighbor Michael Riley, not knowing who he was, he says with a twinkle in his eye \"Yeah, that was us\". The most remarkable thing about these experiments that I learned from him was that the professor would provoke an illusion on the students on the first day of class. I dont remember exactly what the illusion was, but it was some visual artifact, seeing patterns that werent visible a moment ago, much like the OP. At the end of the semester the professor would demonstrate that the entire class could still see that illusion, although they have not been exposed to it in the intervening 4 months ! I tried hard to find an articles on the"},{"id":5982896,"author":"joeyo","month":"2013-07","story":"Why you think your phone is vibrating when it is not","stance":"reference","why":"Example of top-down brain influence on perception","quote":"","text":"There is a considerable body of evidence that \"top-down\" processes affect not only how sensory evidence is processed, but how the sensations themselves are experienced. In other words, high-level (but subconscious) neural \"judgments\" about the statistics of the world, actually influence how you perceive it. To put it still another way: sensations aren't simply passively experienced; they are actively constructed by the brain. Phantom phone vibration is, in many ways, just as \"constructed\" as real phone vibration. Perceptual illusions provide one of the clearest ways to show that sensations reflect factors other than external stimuli. Here are two illusions, in different sensory modalities, that demonstrate the strong top-down influence of the brain on perception: The rubber hand illusion [1,2] and the McGurk effect [3,4]. The rubber hand illusion is particularly relevant because it is quite similar to the phantom phone vibration effect. 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK] 4. [VIDEO] EDIT: Here's an additional example to consider. People generally can't tickle themselves. The sensations are less strong when one touches oneself than when someone else does so. The explanation for this is that the brain automatically discounts sensations which arise as a result of self-motion. This has obvious advantages, namely that you don't constantly overstimulate yourself just in the process of moving through the world and are as such better able to attend to important externally generated stimuli"},{"id":5278456,"author":"forgottenpaswrd","month":"2013-02","story":"Is speed reading really possible? ","stance":"reference","why":"Illusion showing mouth video changes heard sound","quote":"there are famous illusions in witch people hear different sounds with the same sound file depending of the video of a mouth they watch","text":"Sub vocalizing is the fact or reading syllables instead of entire words at once. Deaf could find very useful to vocalize words as they could read peoples lips and faces based on those units. Everybody read lips and faces subconsciously, there are famous illusions in witch people hear different sounds with the same sound file depending of the video of a mouth they watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4884850,"author":"joeyo","month":"2012-12","story":"My journey to a cochlear implant","stance":"reference","why":"Example of vision altering auditory perception","quote":"The McGurk effect is a great example of this","text":"> The disbelief came when it was time to listen to singular > words. With the screen turned off, I got 0%. With the > screen turned on, I got 8%. That’s crazy! It's really amazing how pervasive (putatively Bayesian) multisensory integration of information is in the brain. Even individuals without impaired hearing have their auditory perceptions strongly a affected by other inputs. The McGurk effect is a great example of this: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2550903,"author":"thehodge","month":"2011-05","story":"Try The McGurk Effect - Is Seeing Believing?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this feels like blogspam to me cashing in on hunch adds no value","text":"This was on reddit the other day direct with a link to youtube, this feels like blogspam to me cashing in on hunch adds no value and shows a tiny player. [VIDEO] Direct Link"}]},"MEqxaH47DTs":{"wy":"HN cites it for reasonable estimates comparing the emissions of EVs, hybrids and ICE cars.","q":{"x":"I like this youtube video that goes over various crossover points.","i":28973248,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29236506,"author":"hasdf","month":"2021-11","story":"Electric vehicles could fully recharge in 5 minutes with new cable design","stance":"rec","why":"Compares CO2 emissions of EV, hybrid and ICE cars","quote":"I think it is an interesting exploration of the topic","text":"this video compares co2 emissions of a tesla vs hybrid vs regular ice car. I think it is an interesting exploration of the topic although I think the presenter had a hard time getting correct data and his data on the EV is more conservative than Tesla's numbers. I would definitely be interested in exploring this further. It seems probable to me that we won't have a better grasp on this for years yet as EVs are still pretty new and not widely adopted. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28973248,"author":"novok","month":"2021-10","story":"Dutch forensic lab says it has decoded Tesla's driving data","stance":"rec","why":"Goes over break-even mileage of Tesla versus RAV4 variants","quote":"I like this youtube video that goes over various crossover points.","text":"I like this youtube video that goes over various crossover points. The tesla beats the RAV4 prime at ~45k miles and a rav4 ICE at ~28k miles: [VIDEO] I think 5k miles is a bit low, and using tesla as your only source biased."},{"id":29775697,"author":"djhn","month":"2022-01","story":"The Idiocy of Tiny Houses and Electric Vehicles","stance":"reference","why":"Reasonable estimates of EV versus ICE emissions","quote":"Engineering Explained has made several videos with reasonable estimates","text":"Though not a write up per se, Engineering Explained has made several videos with reasonable estimates: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["daS5RHVAl2U",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["lr4MmmWQtZM",1]]},"RLySXTIBS3c":{"wy":"Cited as an accessible depiction of how commodity trading and the trading pit work.","q":{"x":"This is still the best (Hollywood quality) depiction of what the trading pit looked like in reality.","i":27105954,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27105954,"author":"bmmayer1","month":"2021-05","story":"Trading Pit Hand Signals","stance":"rec","why":"Hollywood-quality depiction of what a trading pit looked like.","quote":"This is still the best (Hollywood quality) depiction of what the trading pit looked like in reality.","text":"This is still the best (Hollywood quality) depiction of what the trading pit looked like in reality. Trading Places, 1983: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14619043,"author":"thisisit","month":"2017-06","story":"Official Response to “Bancor Is Flawed”","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how trading works, aimed at Bancor team.","quote":"I hope these guys watch this video carefully to understand how trading works","text":"If there was a bad response to a critic, this will be it. While Sirer's post was a bit terse, this response doesn't earn brownie points by going the same route. First, they start off as many \"responses\" in crypto currency seem to start that is - saying this is all FUD and the critic should stop spreading FUD. They also take the Trump route, not a great choice of words - \"fake news\". Where I lost them was when they equated themselves as being so important to crypto currency space that critiquing them can be used to tag the whole space as being a giant ponzi scheme. On still persisting, I found their clarification on \"asynchronous price discovery\" very funny. The point of a market making algo is to \"make the market\" ie they have to act as counter parties to each trade irrespective of whether there is a buyer or a seller (ie not a price matching algo). The price discovery in that process happens on how the market making algo is written. Though I guess being programmers and writing a document for programmers they had to go with a technical term. But with someone who understands economics on their team, they should have done away with it. All said, the most important part of a market making algo is not to provide liquidity or smooth prices. As Sirer put it, smoothing prices is a bad idea. If something is volatile it is because the market deems it to be so. Going against the market is not good. I hope these guys watch this video carefully to understand how trading works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14619871,"author":"thisisit","month":"2017-06","story":"Official Response to “Bancor Is Flawed”","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on how trading works.","quote":"A good video on how trading works from Trading Places","text":"This is the very basic function of a market maker. They offer to \"make\" a market even when none exists. So if everyone wants to buy they will keep offering until their inventory runs out. I haven't read the Bancor paper or code so I am not sure if this is implemented but every market maker's another objective is to make money. So they normally don't trade on a fair price, rather fair price - x/2 to buy and fair price + x/2 to sell where x is the spread to collect money. So effectively they buy low and sell high for a minute difference. As for a scenario where they have only buyers, the algos or people on the floor tend to take stock of the existing orders and start moving the ask higher to compensate. This happens until it becomes untenable to buy and some sellers start entering the market to make a quick buck. A good video on how trading works from Trading Places: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11976697,"author":"rexreed","month":"2016-06","story":"YC set to launch one-year minimum income pilot project to figure out logistics","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[LINK] and actually, I've always, always loved this scene since I was a kid: [VIDEO] - takes some thinking to learn how the markets were cornered and how they made all that money shorting OJ commodities. But your point here is well taken - are these rich folks placing bets and toying with poor folks? Hmm."}],"r":[["SGJ5cZnoodY",1]]},"gfh-VCTwMw8":{"wy":"Recommended as an entertaining cautionary talk on microservice failures and Conway's law.","q":{"x":"Inverse Conway Maneuver: Design the organization you want, the architecture will follow, kicking and screaming.","i":18531030,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":36388722,"author":"ketzu","month":"2023-06","story":"Make microservices look like monoliths","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining talk on microservice failures","quote":"I also liked \"Avoiding Microservice Megadisasters\" but mostly for the entertainment value","text":"It is such a weird feeling to come across a topic I haven't read on in a while, watch two videos on it out of random curiosity, hop on hackernews the next day, and get a recommendation for the exact same videos. I very much enjoyed the 5 techniques and many of the tidbits. I also liked \"Avoiding Microservice Megadisasters\" but mostly for the entertainment value ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":18531030,"author":"tony-allan","month":"2018-11","story":"Avoiding Microservice Megadisasters","stance":"rec","why":"Conway's law and inverse Conway maneuver at 39:00","quote":"Inverse Conway Maneuver: Design the organization you want, the architecture will follow, kicking and screaming.","text":"[VIDEO] (39:00) Conway's law: Any organization that designs a system (defined broadly) will produce a design whose structure is a copy of the organization's communication structure. Jimmy's law: A broken, dysfunctional organization driven by meeting unhealthy goals and metrics will produce broken, dysfunctional systems. Inverse Conway Maneuver: Design the organization you want, the architecture will follow, kicking and screaming."},{"id":22148526,"author":"etripe","month":"2020-01","story":"Reasons for Doing Microfront Ends","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a microservices disaster at Bell","quote":"scenarios like the Bell micro-services disaster (2)","text":"As your average developer who's heard architects mention these micro-frontends, I would have liked the article to start off by listing the use cases and trade-offs instead of jumping right on the hype train at full speed. Since the article mentions Piral, I thought I would peruse that project's documentation (0), but am none the wiser on basic questions like: how do components get assembled into the main application, is network traffic overhead is going to be a problem, etc. Delving further into the Piral docs, I came across a link to a sample micro-frontend (1) that seems to be importing every micro-frontend into the index, so network traffic overhead seems like it could be an issue. Does anyone here have experience with these in a production environment and know how to avoid (in my mind) basic pitfalls like these? As it stands, this article seems to be setting people up for scenarios like the Bell micro-services disaster (2) [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["J-M98KLgaUU",1]]},"sFrVdoOhu1Q":{"wy":"Cited as a marvel of absurd precision in home-built machine tools, and a pointer to Gelbart's other videos.","q":{"x":"If you enjoy absurd precision and accuracy, have a look at Dan Gelbart's older videos","i":40193317,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40193317,"author":"hi-v-rocknroll","month":"2024-04","story":"My Homemade Metal Lathe Project","stance":"rec","why":"Custom air bearing mill and grinder reaching 1 micron","quote":"If you enjoy absurd precision and accuracy, have a look at Dan Gelbart's older videos","text":"If you enjoy absurd precision and accuracy, have a look at Dan Gelbart's older videos where he fabricobbles together a custom milling machine and grinder with air bearings that can work parts down to 1 micron. No lapping needed. [VIDEO] PS: More power to the professors who go into business and reach \"F everyone money\". [LINK]"},{"id":22892265,"author":"paypalcust83","month":"2020-04","story":"Hand Scraping a Truly Flat Plane (1908) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Builds 1 micron machines with air bearings, no lapping","quote":"The creator (Dan Gelbart) demonstrates nitrogen-tight parts without bearings, and claims zero wear or lubrication needs.","text":"It's interesting to get more precise and accurate machines out of the ones came before. These days, if you want to get milling and grinding down to 1 micron without lapping using only your own expertise and some parts from eBay (air bearings and precision-ground slabs): [VIDEO] The creator (Dan Gelbart) demonstrates nitrogen-tight parts without bearings, and claims zero wear or lubrication needs. #AvEclub"},{"id":45117010,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-09","story":"Building the most accurate DIY CNC lathe in the world [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a specialty high-precision CNC build","quote":"there are some completed specialty CNC builds around.","text":"Most do not understand how difficult these machines are to handle, but there are some completed specialty CNC builds around. \"High precision air bearing CNC lathe and grinder\" (Dan Gelbart) [VIDEO] Cylo's Garage spent a lot of time exploring these designs. =3"},{"id":23944002,"author":"SAI_Peregrinus","month":"2020-07","story":"FAA issues emergency directive on 2,000 Boeing 737 NG, Classic planes","stance":"reference","why":"Custom lathe with under 1 micron tolerance","quote":"Dan Gelbart built his own lathe with <1µm final part tolerance","text":"Nanotech Systems (Moore's current name) make a high-precision drum lathe with 1nm positioning precision, with a 2600mm working area [1]. 1 part in 2,600,000,000. Dan Gelbart built his own lathe with <1µm final part tolerance [2]. Within a few thousandths of an inch isn't very impressive. I can hold 1 thou on a cheap manual lathe (Grizzly G0602 10\"x22\" working area), with a decent amount of care. Holding to tenths (1/10000\") is impossible with the equipment I can afford though. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20531439,"author":"hlieberman","month":"2019-07","story":"Kern Microtechnik: Accurate CNC Machines [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Home-built 1 micron CNC lathe and grinder","quote":"For a slightly more accurate CNC lathe and grinding machine (1 micron) that can be made \"at home\"","text":"For a slightly more accurate CNC lathe and grinding machine (1 micron) that can be made \"at home\": [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["szIGZVrSAyc",1]]},"sIi_QS1tdFM":{"wy":"HN links it in threads about native advertising and disguised ads as a good, funny overview of sponsored content passing as news.","q":{"x":"John Oliver did a great segment on this on sponsored content on local news","i":29487466,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29487466,"author":"mdasen","month":"2021-12","story":"Amazon’s search results are full of ads ‘unlawfully deceiving’ consumers","stance":"rec","why":"Segment on disguised sponsored content in local news","quote":"John Oliver did a great segment on this on sponsored content on local news","text":"There's THE TRUTH and the truth ( [LINK] if you haven't seen that Simpsons episode). Let's say that I do an hour-long infomercial on a product and I try and make the infomercial look as much like content as I can. In fact, it's not just about the product. We talk about the news and weather as well. You watch 30 minutes of it and don't notice that at the end it says that it was sponsored. I clearly said it was sponsored. Just because you stopped watching before then isn't my fault, right? John Oliver did a great segment on this on sponsored content on local news: [VIDEO] Google's search results used to use a different background color and borders to distinguish ads from organic results. Now they simply put \"Ad\" near the paid result. That makes it harder to detect what is an ad and what isn't an ad. I'm not saying that Google is being deceitful. However, they're certainly trying to make the fact that it's an ad less noticeable. If I put photoshopped mockups of a product on a page and then note in the description that they're not actually photos of the product, is that deceitful? That's certainly getting closer to the line (and I'd say crossing it) given extra distance. Amazon has made choices to make the \"sponsored\" tag less noticeable. It's a light gray and not bold so that it blends in against the background while your eye is drawn to the much larger 45% larger font-size and bold text next to it. Amazon could have chosen to make the \"sponsored\" tag larger, with better contras"},{"id":27551934,"author":"clan","month":"2021-06","story":"Multiple local news stations say the same thing verbatim [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Last Week Tonight segment on sponsored content on local news","quote":"There is a recent Last Week Tonight covering sponsored content on local news stations which is worth a watch as well.","text":"There is a recent Last Week Tonight covering sponsored content on local news stations which is worth a watch as well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34819200,"author":"lesuorac","month":"2023-02","story":"Half of Americans now believe that news organizations deliberately mislead them","stance":"reference","why":"Example of paid product placement in news","quote":"","text":"I would say its hard to do any rational thinking about come to the conclusion that the 24/h news orgs are meant to inform you. The easy angle is product placement [1]. Literally fork over a small sum of money and you get the news org to rave about your product without doing any verification. There's also how a headline is not written by the author so it won't always reflect the contents of the article. > And a lot of the times is just because news are not validating their own beliefs IMO, the news orgs have a symbiotic relation with their viewers. The viewers want their viewpoint reinforced and the news org wants views. So the news org put out a biased product so that their viewers will selectively watch that news org. However, this still means that news org aren't actually trying to inform. My biggest gripe is how often they'll refuse to link to actual legal documents when talking about filed lawsuits and the like and in general I don't find some of their claims in the article to be as supported by the actual filings. [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"nXCSloXZ-wc":{"wy":"HN points to it for understanding Glimmer and Ember's view layer, and as a reference for opcode-compiled templates.","q":{"x":"you should not miss @tomdale's talk at ReactConf about GlimmerJS","i":16246823,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16246823,"author":"mhale","month":"2018-01","story":"Guide to JavaScript Frameworks","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tom Dale on Glimmer VM, Ember's view layer","quote":"you should not miss @tomdale's talk at ReactConf about GlimmerJS","text":"Ember is the PostgreSQL of JavaScript frameworks. Similar to the way Postgres has deep roots that trace back to Ingres[1] in the days before SQL was a solidly established standard, Ember traces it roots back to SproutCore[2] in the JS framework pre-historic era of 2010. And the same way PostgreSQL was seen as a second-tier or only \"historically significant\" open source database, the PostgreSQL team just kept at it, toiling away year after year, steading churning out awesome code and excellent documentation, getting better and better with each release. Like clockwork. In the same way PostgreSQL is now, finally, enjoying more popularity and getting the recognition (long overdue in my opinion) for the awesome platform that it is, I expect that in time more developers will come around to appreciating Ember. The Ember leadership is definitely approaching the project and the processes around it like they intend to be not just relevant but pushing the boundaries for a long time to come. For one example this boundary pushing, you should not miss @tomdale's talk at ReactConf about GlimmerJS (the view layer extracted from EmberJS). It is, in my humble opinion, simultaneously mind-blowing and and inspiring.[3] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21348384,"author":"jonkoops","month":"2019-10","story":"React Concurrent Mode","stance":"reference","why":"Glimmer VM compiles templates to opcodes","quote":"","text":"This is a good development. Honestly one thing I very much think is a downside about React is the fact that your template is also JavaScript. Don't get me wrong this is incredibly powerful and can be very expressive, but it comes at a cost. That cost has the same cost as other JavaScript - it's blocking, cannot be paused and has all the possibilities of de-optimizations that might occur in the JIT. The Ember team faced a similar problem in the past and I find their solution to this problem quite intriguing, even in it's obscurity. Instead of compiling their templates to JavaScript they instead compile them to opcodes in a binary format which is executed by a virtual machine. This has several advantages. Since the template file is represented as an optimized binary the size of the file itself is incredibly small, excellent since templates can be a large part of your eventual code. Not only that but since the instructions are opcodes, this mean the VM can pause at a moment when the browser is too busy with other stuff. Since a large part of your templates is probably static and not prone to any changes the template file also indicates which parts of the instructions are dynamic and which are static. This allows the VM to generate a separate set of instructions just for subsequent updates, only updating those parts which are dynamic. Now I am not saying this is a silver bullet, it is however an interesting and fresh approach. In case you're interested in this check out the Glimm"},{"id":18513274,"author":"lightblade","month":"2018-11","story":"Hyperscript Tagged Markup: JSX alternative using standard tagged templates","stance":"reference","why":"Contains performance benchmark demo of Glimmer","quote":"You can skip to 29:34 for the performance demo.","text":"Yes, the runtime needs to be JavaScript. It’s not obviously faster, but it’s been benchmarked to be faster. [VIDEO] You can skip to 29:34 for the performance demo."}],"r":[["S2Hp_1jqpY8",1]]},"-wu_pO5Z7Pk":{"wy":"HN cites it as the foundational demonstration that SS7 is insecure, relevant to arguments about SMS-based two-factor authentication.","q":{"x":"Tobias Engel demonstrates among other things tracking down a test subject over two continents for about a month","i":14812203,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14812203,"author":"Hasknewbie","month":"2017-07","story":"Telecom Lobbyists Downplayed Security Flaws in Mobile Data Backbone","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates tracking a test subject across continents via SS7.","quote":"Tobias Engel demonstrates among other things tracking down a test subject over two continents for about a month","text":"SS7 (the protocol whose security flaws are being downplayed here) was not designed with security in mind, and so has been the subject of infosec research for a few years now. This is a CCC talk from 2014 (there are older ones) where Tobias Engel demonstrates among other things tracking down a test subject over two continents for about a month, via his non-tempered mobile phone, using only SS7: [VIDEO] In the same talk he mentions that he is working with a German telecom operator to resolve some of these issues. So as opposed to the US, it appears SS7 has been hardened in the EU since at least 2014 for the type of attacks mentioned in the Vice article... I really wonder why the US MNO are unwilling to act on this, since it's (1) a well known vulnerability and (2) fixes (or at least mitigations) have been implemented."},{"id":27605216,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2021-06","story":"I made 50k calls to explore the telephone network","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"See also, /r/weirdnumbers and, if you're into that sort of thing (that is, exploring the phone network), /r/phreaking Old-skool phreaking[0] (eg, using blue boxes and red boxes) is mostly dead (at least in the US and most \"first world\" countries. Maybe there is some vestige of in-band signaling left somewhere else) but there is still some fun to be had exploring phones and phone networks. Modern day phreaking is more about GSM sniffing[1], messing with the SS7 network[2][3][4], using SCTP[5]/SIGTRAN stuff[6], etc. etc. But, at least for the land-line / PSTN network, even some of the old \"colored boxes\"[7] still do useful things. You can always beige-box a landline phone, violet-boxes should still work, I think a gold-box would still work, etc. If you want to dig deeper into how the PSTN works, a good, fun book is Understanding Telephone Electronics [8] by Carr, Winder, and Bigelow. Another interesting one is Digital Telephony [9] by Bellamy. Another \"oldie but goodie\" is Voice and Data Communications Handbook [10] by Bates and Gregory. Also, don't ask me how or why I know any of this stuff... :-) [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO] [4]: [LINK] [5]: [LINK] [6]: [LINK] [7]: [LINK] [8]: [LINK] [9]: [LINK] [10]: [LINK]"},{"id":14353541,"author":"Zekio","month":"2017-05","story":"Your WiFi is using a stronger encryption than the tech behind 2 factor auth","stance":"reference","why":"Newer version of the talk on SS7 insecurity.","quote":"SS7 was proved ridiculously insecure ages ago by a guy who did a talk","text":"If I remember correctly SS7 was proved ridiculously insecure ages ago by a guy who did a talk at DefCon it was quite the interesting talk, will see if I can find it and add it to my comment EDIT: wasn't a defcon talk I remembered, but a 25c3 talk: [LINK] from 2010 :) EDIT2: [VIDEO] newer version of the talk"}]},"0mCsluv5FXA":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as a delightful example of how far TypeScript types can be pushed, since they are Turing complete.","q":{"x":"I must link this wonderful video about TypeScript types running... DOOM.","i":44782824,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44782824,"author":"drej","month":"2025-08","story":"A parser for TypeScript types, written in TypeScript types","stance":"rec","why":"Wonderful video of TypeScript types running DOOM.","quote":"I must link this wonderful video about TypeScript types running... DOOM.","text":"For those not familiar with this kind of computing challenges, I must link this wonderful video about TypeScript types running... DOOM. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45404328,"author":"ninetyninenine","month":"2025-09","story":"Python developers are embracing type hints","stance":"reference","why":"Example of TypeScript types being Turing complete.","quote":"You can actually get typescript types to run doom.","text":"Typescript types (and Idris) are Turing complete. You can actually get typescript types to run doom. [VIDEO] Idris on the other hand is SPECIFICALLY designed so types and the program live in the same language. See the documentation intro: [LINK] THe powerful thing about these languages is that they can prove your program correct. For testing you can never verify your program to be correct. Testing is a statistical sampling technique. To verify a program as correct via tests you have to test every possible input and output combination of your program, which is impractical. So instead people write tests for a subset of the possibilities which ONLY verifies the program as correct for that subset. Think about it. If you have a function: def add(x: int, y: int) -> int How would you verify this program is 100% correct? You have to test every possible combination of x, y and add(x, y). But instead you test like 3 or 4 possibilities in your unit tests and this helps with the overall safety of the program because of statistical sampling. If a small sample of the logic is correct, it says something about the entire population of the logic.. Types on the other hand prove your program correct. def add(x: int, y: int) -> int: return x + y If the above is type checked, your program is proven correct for ALL possible types. If those types are made more advanced via being programmable, then it becomes possible for type checking to prove your ENTIRE program correct. Imagine: def add<A: addabl"},{"id":43335412,"author":"stuaxo","month":"2025-03","story":"A 10x Faster TypeScript","stance":"reference","why":"Linked demo of DOOM running in TypeScript types.","quote":"I'd like to see if it makes a difference to the version of DOOM that runs in the TypeScript type system.","text":"I'd like to see if it makes a difference to the version of DOOM that runs in the TypeScript type system. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["nAbCKa0FzjQ",1],["bSkpMdDe4g4",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["MO8hZlgK5zc",1],["T5RmMNDR5XI",1]]},"469chceiiUQ":{"wy":"Recommended as a solid, informative treatment of silicon-based life and the chemical limits on alien biochemistry.","q":{"x":"PBS Space Time has a very good & informative episode covering this.","i":42854970,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42854970,"author":"ziddoap","month":"2025-01","story":"IAC confirms existence of a Super-earth in the habitable zone of a Sun-like Star","stance":"rec","why":"Why silicon is the next likeliest basis for alien life.","quote":"PBS Space Time has a very good & informative episode covering this.","text":"PBS Space Time has a very good & informative episode covering this. I'll try to dig it up. Most elements are missing some key properties that carbon has. Silicon is the next most-likely element, but it's still missing out on a few properties that carbon has. Here: [LINK] Or on YouTube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40319759,"author":"shagie","month":"2024-05","story":"Computer models suggest modern plate tectonics due to blobs left by collision","stance":"reference","why":"Silicon-based life and its bonding limitations.","quote":"There are very few atoms that allow for the complex scaffolding for shapes.","text":"PBS Space Time: What If Alien Life Were Silicon-Based? [VIDEO] There are very few atoms that allow for the complex scaffolding for shapes. Many atoms are too large (and thus bond too weakly). Silicon is interesting, but it has some difficulties. [LINK] > Silicon–oxygen single bonds are longer (1.6 vs 1.4 Å) but stronger (452 vs. about 360 kJ mol−1) than carbon–oxygen single bonds. It is much easier to lock up oxygen and silicon in SiO than in CO compounds and in turn makes them less available for more complex structures of life."},{"id":34639626,"author":"willis936","month":"2023-02","story":"The search for extraterrestrial life as we don’t know it","stance":"reference","why":"Silicon-based life possibilities are limited by real chemistry.","quote":"chemistry is not infinitely complex.","text":"It is a fun thought, but chemistry is not infinitely complex. Much like the Fermi paradox, once you start applying real world qualifiers on chemistry of complex life the search space becomes quite small. [VIDEO] Life inside of stars does enable different options."}],"r":[["kd2KEHvK-q8",1],["O5BJVO3PDeQ",1]]},"5SBo0akeDMY":{"wy":"Cited in Ukraine and Russia threads as older mainstream coverage of far-right groups in Ukraine, mostly as a reference in political arguments rather than recommended viewing.","m":[{"id":32830308,"author":"dnh44","month":"2022-09","story":"European natural gas imports and storage reserves","stance":"rec","why":"BBC Newsnight report on far-right influence in Ukraine","quote":"found these short documentaries on the BBC Newsnight YouTube channel a while back","text":"I didn't believe the Nazi claims that were being thrown around at first but I found these short documentaries on the BBC Newsnight YouTube channel a while back about the seemingly rather large Neo-Nazi influence in Ukraine. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":37706969,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-09","story":"The Kakhovka Dam Disaster Revealed an Archaeological ‘Goldmine’","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as one of several sources in a war-causes argument","quote":"","text":"There is no “ultimately” in history, that eclipses everything that led up to it, or since it. Everything has significant reasons that led up to it, and understanding those reasons and legitimate concerns from all parties is KEY to making lasting peace agreements. Click the link above to find out what they are. Here are more links showing leaders of billions of people, and the polls of the people themselves, seeing more than one side and putting blame on USA and NATO for instance, for backing Russia into a corner like a cornered animal, arming Ukraine, blocking all peace agreements and efforts and goading it to lash out with this horrific pre-emptive invasion, which has been systematically called in the US “unprovoked and unjustified” as a concerted effort to make you not look at the man behind the curtain: India (most populous nation, 1.5 billion people) [LINK] China (second most populous nation, another 1.5 billion people) [LINK] The Pope (1 billion Catholics): [LINK] Imran Khan (400 million Pakistanis - ousted with US support recently for failing to fall in line) [LINK] Brazil (both presidents refuse to condemn Russia alone, call for negotiations) [LINK] Even in the centers of the most hawkish anti-Russian sentiment we have leaders warning about all this before it even started. For example Nigel Farage in UK 2014: [LINK] Here in the US, dire warnings about NATO expansion were given by every single ambassador to Russia, and every foreign policy expert concurred (including th"},{"id":36610157,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-07","story":"Jeffrey Sachs: the West’s false narrative about Russia and China","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in broader argument about Ukraine and proxy war","quote":"","text":"Well, a single person is going too far. Obviously there is diversity of thought throughout various cities. But having said that, yes I specifically mentioned Chernivtsi because it is in Western Ukraine and to show that I am acquainted with people who live there. Cities like Ivano-Frankivsk and Chernivtsi and Lviv are historically Polish Galicia, they really have had quite anti-Russian sentiment for a very long time. Even during Soviet times, the USA tried to reach them with Radio Liberty —- started by the CIA (bet you didn’t know that). People living there are the ones who hope the most that USA will come and help them escape having to deal with “the Russian-speaking world”. Many of them don’t like the Eastern Ukrainians, calling them “katsapy” as far back as 70 years ago. And the Russians — well, they are “Muscovites” while Ukrainians are the “true Russians” in their view. Again, this is not everyone but a prevailing sentiment. Just like with sunnis and shiites and kurds, it is a very convenient tinderbox for an outside power (the US, say) to come in and play one side against the other, just as they have done in many other countries. (And KGB used to do as well, eg with Palestinians in Israel/Jordan etc.) It is a playbook that predates the US by millennia. In the Bible, the Egyptians worried about Jews becoming numerous and siding with their enemies etc. Anyway, Wikipedia has a textbook entry on “proxy war” that literally describes it all: [LINK] A lot of far-right groups en"},{"id":35001211,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-03","story":"Oceania Always at War with Eastasia: Generative AI and Knowledge Pollution","stance":"reference","why":"2014 BBC report cited as contrast to later coverage","quote":"","text":"It’s basically a video where the BBC claims the exact opposite of what they were reporting before, and denies the very things they repeatedly said before, until the UK government got actively involved and influenced them to change their narrative: 2014 BBC for example: [VIDEO] During 2014 Maidan revolution: “There are far right neo nazis in Ukraine, they are the most organized and active part of the revolution, here is a whole documentary about that where we go interview them about their goals.” 2017, 2018, 2019: “Paramilitary groups like Azov and Dnipro etc. contain many far-right nationalist fighters, about 20% admit to holding neo nazi beliefs”: [LINK] And then 2022: “Azov has hardly any far right, they are in fact just a part of the official armed forces of Ukraine, and it is the Russians committing all the atrocities in battle”: [LINK] I am sure there is truth on all sides but certainly a wide gulf between what they reported then and now. Here in USA, Trump supporters could also believe new Trump and even though he contradicted old Trump totally. Trump on video side by side: [LINK] People just get fervor and overlook that stuff"},{"id":30763209,"author":"ShivShankaran","month":"2022-03","story":"Ukraine’s President Is in over His Head","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as BBC coverage in 2014 of rising neo-Nazism in Ukraine.","quote":"BBC article in 2014 on rise of neo-nazism in Ukraine","text":"No, this has aged well. If anything western narratives have changed to paint him as a hero. Putin is a megalomaniac corrupt dictator. There is no point fighting him. Zelensky could concede the four points and end the war but he chose to force all men to fight for him. Either he is power hungry or US is forcing him to cling on to his title. Today he suspended all left and socialist parties including the party that got the second highest votes [LINK] Also keep in mind that Medvedchuk got more votes than him but Zelensky had him arrested. Same feces as Russia just a different orifice. BBC article in 2014 on rise of neo-nazism in Ukraine [VIDEO] Human Rights Watch on Ukraine killing civilians [LINK]"},{"id":30469012,"author":"FollowingTheDao","month":"2022-02","story":"Ukraine calls on hacker underground to defend against Russia","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of neo-Nazi influence in Ukraine.","quote":"These articles go back years","text":"> everyone is neonazi No, but to say that the instigators of anti-russian sentiment in Ukraine are not mostly Neo-nazi's is ignorant. These articles go back years [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":16516424,"author":"da02","month":"2018-03","story":"Putin's Hypersonic Weapon Appears to Be a Modified Iskander Ballistic Missile","stance":"reference","why":"BBC Newsnight piece cited alongside others on neo-Nazi presence.","quote":"","text":"Are any of these videos below inaccurate? They show Azov logo as a combination of 3 fascist symbols. (Keep in mind, the mainstream media (CNN, FOX, NBC, CBS, PBS, etc) is very anti-Russian and it's only independent media that uses the word \"Neo-NAZI\". Mainstream media in the US repeats the press releases of the Republicans and Democrats with little investigation into their claims.) * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"}]},"6Chp12sEnWk":{"wy":"Pointed to as a true-crime story of cybercrime investigation, usually in passing.","q":{"x":"super interesting story about how the Feds caught Russian Mega-Carder Roman Seleznev","i":17750530,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17750530,"author":"cosmolev","month":"2018-08","story":"Ask HN: Recommendations of good cybercrime novels?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting true story of how the Feds caught a Russian carder","quote":"super interesting story about how the Feds caught Russian Mega-Carder Roman Seleznev","text":"Not a novel, but super interesting story about how the Feds caught Russian Mega-Carder Roman Seleznev: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40909516,"author":"qball","month":"2024-07","story":"Z-Library admins \"escape house arrest\" after judge approves U.S. extradition","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">I don't think it has much to do with that. Sure it does; Russian police do nothing about credit card theft because (and to the point that) that money is ultimately coming out of Western pockets ( [VIDEO] ). Same as the North Koreans with their industrial-level counterfeiting of USD (to name one example), for the same reasons. Might as well be an unofficial letter of marque."},{"id":37770095,"author":"dralley","month":"2023-10","story":"How the Baltic states became the hub of crypto money laundering and fraud","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"One of the biggest credit card fraud empires on the planet was run by the son of a deputy in the Russian Duma.","text":"Jan Marsalek, of the Wirecard fraud fame, who fled Germany to Russia to escape prosecution, is now suspected to be an asset of Russian intelligence working alongside a ring of at least 5 other spies. [LINK] One of the biggest credit card fraud empires on the planet was run by the son of a deputy in the Russian Duma. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16731511,"author":"Buge","month":"2018-04","story":"The Profile Engine has now been donated to the Internet Archive","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Then how was Roman Seleznev arrested and charged under US laws even though he never visited the US, or even a country with an extradition treaty with the US? [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":15358477,"author":"mrb","month":"2017-09","story":"Breach at Sonic Drive-In May Have Impacted Millions of Credit, Debit Cards","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"« There would be more dishonest merchants if the possibility of chargebacks ceased to exist » Yes there will probably be a minor uptick in merchants being dishonest. But I doubt it will be as bad as you make it to be. Many other factors push merchants to stay honest: legal repercussions, damaged reputation, etc. « And to say that virtually no merchant acceptance[1] is an “irrelevant argument” is laughable » Irrelevant was the wrong word. I meant illogical. Your argument is like saying in the early days of Blu-ray that \"no one will buy Blu-ray discs because no one has Blu-ray players\". « Merchants have little incentive » Second time you say it, second time you are wrong. I already pointed out their main (largest!) incentive to accept BTC is to avoid chargeback fraud. This fraud is a major problem for merchants. They can go bankrupt (eg. [VIDEO] ) or be drawn into costly suits with no way to recover the money ( [LINK] ). Apple Pay P2P will be riddled with roadblocks. For starters most people will not be able to use it as it requires a $500 device (iPhone). I guarantee you there will be transaction limits, delays in access to funds, etc. All things that Bitcoin solves."}]},"CiHN0ZWE5bk":{"wy":"HN cites it whenever the popular absorb-and-re-emit explanation comes up, as a source for the wave-interference account.","q":{"x":"This reminds me of this Sixty Symbols video which explains why light is slower in media such as glass than in a vacuum","i":21920936,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21920936,"author":"7373737373","month":"2019-12","story":"New Material Breaks World Record Turning Heat into Electricity","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why light is slower in glass via secondary waves","quote":"This reminds me of this Sixty Symbols video which explains why light is slower in media such as glass than in a vacuum","text":"This reminds me of this Sixty Symbols video which explains why light is slower in media such as glass than in a vacuum: [VIDEO] The incoming light wave causes an avalanche of secondary waves through electromagnetically perturbing the individual atoms, and the superposition of all these results in a wave that seems to travel slower than the speed of light."},{"id":28530640,"author":"wruza","month":"2021-09","story":"Scientists create matter from pure light, proving the Breit-Wheeler effect","stance":"reference","why":"Transparency and slowdown come from field interactions in the medium","quote":"The transparency and slowdown effect is a complex interaction between fields in the medium","text":"Both ideas are incorrect. Bouncing around would be random and the outgoing light would be scattered (as in matte reflections) in all directions. Absorbtion simply destroys the original light. If photons are mostly absorbed, we call that “a wall” or ”a fog”, not “a transparent medium”, which to be transparent shouldn’t have electrons whose energy level differences correspond to a photon’s wave length. The transparency and slowdown effect is a complex interaction between fields in the medium, which I honestly don’t have enough courage to retell. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25590260,"author":"vecter","month":"2020-12","story":"Well, Actually – Physics Answers","stance":"reference","why":"Source refuting the ping-pong absorption explanation","quote":"This is wrong. Two sources","text":"> Well, actually, the speed of light is constant, it just takes time to ping-pong around a lattice/gas. Photons hit particles, get absorbed, and re-emitted, which causes an effective slowdown at a larger scale. A gas is just vacuum with particles floating around in it. When photons travel between those particles, they are traveling at c. The effective slowdown is determined by the lattice/gas, and we denote this slowdown with the refractive index... This is wrong. Two sources [0][1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25235783,"author":"andreareina","month":"2020-11","story":"Warp Drive News","stance":"reference","why":"Light as wave explains propagation through media","quote":"The explanation gets into looking at light as a wave.","text":"Absorption and re-emission are probabilistic, so you'd expect experiments to show a spread distribution of propagation times, deflection angles, etc that aren't actually observed. The explanation gets into looking at light as a wave. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":24329038,"author":"irjustin","month":"2020-08","story":"Do we really travel through time with the speed of light?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on light slowing in media versus curved space","quote":"","text":"downvoted because time traveling through a medium vs traveling through curved space are not anywhere near the same underlying principles[0]. The implication being suggested is - if I was trapped in a block of plastic or... underwater, I would experience time different compared to someone standing on land just because \"c\" is slower. Clearly not the case. To be specific, when \"the speed of light\" is discussed like this, it usually means the \"speed of causality\"[1] which is the underlying meaning of \"c\" in relativity. The speed of causality through a medium (i.e. water) is the same as a vacuum if spacetime curvature is the same. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":22897033,"author":"mrtksn","month":"2020-04","story":"Roger Penrose – Is Mathematics Invented or Discovered? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Multiple explanations; math models rather than reality","quote":"there are multiple explanations on it.","text":"Recently I watched Sixty Symbols video on why light is slower in glass and there are multiple explanations on it. What struck me was what prof. Michael Merrifield said at the end of his explanation: [VIDEO] On the question about what is the reality, what explanation is the true one, Merrifield said that the Math works on all of the explanations and what those explanations do is simply to model the behaviour of nature and not necessarily reflect the reality. That's how Newtonian physics and relativistic physics are both correct models, tools to model nature and simply can be used to whenever suitable. Wouldn't that mean that mathematics is just an invented tool to reason about physical models?"},{"id":17185504,"author":"s0ulphire","month":"2018-05","story":"High school physics course notes, with JavaScript simulations","stance":"reference","why":"Says absorption and re-emission explanation is incorrect","quote":"Sixty Symbols (aka nottinghamscience) did at least 2 videos on this","text":"> Light seems like it slows down in dense media, but light always propagates at the fastest possible speed for any physical thing. The slow down occurs when the light gets briefly absorbed by atoms and then re-emitted in the same direction. Someone please correct me if I'm wrong, but Sixty Symbols (aka nottinghamscience) did at least 2 videos on this where they expressly pointed out that this is widely pushed but totally incorrect? [VIDEO]"},{"id":17186864,"author":"lilgreenland","month":"2018-05","story":"High school physics course notes, with JavaScript simulations","stance":"reference","why":"Source for phase velocity explanation of slowed light","quote":"","text":"thanks! When I wrote that line I think I went with the top result of physics.stackexchange, but I'm now convinced that the phase velocity explanation is better. Here is my updated text: <p>While individual waves of light always move at c, light seems to move slower in dense media. This is because as the light wave propagates through a medium it produces ripples that <a href=\" [VIDEO] in a way that slows the group velocity of the light wave.</p>"}],"r":[["5RKbSi87J5Q",1],["586Zn1ct-QA",1],["dCSIXLIzhzk",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["1rCrfQUcXDI",1],["wfm2xCKOCNk",1],["f9EbD6iY9zI",1],["RjD1aLm4Thg",1],["FzRP1qdwPKw",1]]},"D38dcArwCNc":{"wy":"Cited in e-ink display threads as a long-term review of using e-ink as a monitor.","q":{"x":"Technology Connections (amazing Youtube channel btw!)","i":22830081,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22830081,"author":"dave7","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: Has any progress been made on large format E-ink displays?","stance":"rec","why":"Long-term review of e-ink as second monitor.","quote":"6 months later, tips / tweaks / long term review","text":"Technology Connections (amazing Youtube channel btw!) has a few videos on using an Onyx Boox Max as a 2nd monitor: [LINK] (Intro) [LINK] (2nd channel longer look, mini review) [VIDEO] (6 months later, tips / tweaks / long term review)"},{"id":40467560,"author":"dspillett","month":"2024-05","story":"Daylight Computer – New 60fps e-paper tablet","stance":"reference","why":"Videos covering e-ink as monitor.","quote":"the TechnologyConnections fellow did a couple of videos that cover it","text":"There are certainly some e-ink devices that are able to do this, the TechnologyConnections fellow did a couple of videos that cover it, amongst other things: [LINK] & [VIDEO] Though he is using a Boox product and I'm wary of them over the GPL violation thing: as well as the general “they'd piss & moan and send the lawyers round if someone took their stuff and used it unlicensed” hypocrisy, the paranoid cynic in me thinks there is something they want to hide in their customisations (hooks for calling home or some such). Also, those clips are a few years old now so some of the problems he was having may have been ironed out in newer devices & software updates. Another option is running a VMC client on any tablet that isn't walled off so that you can't: there are a number of VNC servers that create a hidden virtual screen and mirror that so the connecting client because the extra screen."},{"id":22063179,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2020-01","story":"New 10.3″ Waveshare E Ink Monitor","stance":"reference","why":"Creator writes scripts on an e-ink tablet.","quote":"writes his scripts using an eInk Android tablet","text":"The guy behind the Technology Connections YouTube channel writes his scripts using an eInk Android tablet [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["7NfX0vlCa4k",1],["ytpRnRke6I0",1],["_160oMzblY8",1]]},"KCkbp4wurW0":{"wy":"HN uses it to show a classic early Mac game, noting its low-latency input and early audio showcase.","q":{"x":"arguably the best non-educational game for the original Macintosh","i":4685144,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4685144,"author":"Alex3917","month":"2012-10","story":"Show HN: I spent two years working on an HTML5 game. I finally released it","stance":"rec","why":"Gameplay of Dark Castle on the original Mac","quote":"arguably the best non-educational game for the original Macintosh","text":"I like the black and white graphics. It reminds me of dark castle, which was arguably the best non-educational game for the original Macintosh: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30892734,"author":"nemo","month":"2022-04","story":"A history of Hup, the jump sound of shooting games","stance":"reference","why":"Early Mac game showcasing audio, with jump sounds","quote":"An early Mac B&W game that showcased the system's audio capabilities.","text":"Dark Castle (1986). An early Mac B&W game that showcased the system's audio capabilities. It has jumps with the \"hup\" sound. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9851183,"author":"lectrick","month":"2015-07","story":"Pens Are Making a High-Tech Comeback","stance":"reference","why":"Dark Castle on a Mac Plus as example of low latency","quote":"had a zero latency mouse (or at least imperceptible) with zero flicker","text":"I have to say that in this case you're wrong. The Apple Mac Plus in my parents' closet, which I still fire up from time to time to play Dark Castle ( [VIDEO] ), had a zero latency mouse (or at least imperceptible) with zero flicker. Took Microsoft at least 20 years to get something comparable. This is not a nostalgic mis-memory, or an exaggeration. Please find an old Mac from the 80's and simply move the mouse around. > they are recalling computers which had no networking The very first network games I ever played were over AppleTalk on a Mac, like NetTrek ( [LINK] ... yes, that is digitized sound out of a 1984 machine), Maze Wars ( [LINK] ) and the venerable Bolo! ( [LINK] ), the latter still one of the best multiplayer gaming experiences I've ever had"}],"r":[["9c8an2XZ3MU",1]]},"abqAanC2NZs":{"wy":"Cited as an early proof that real-time path tracing was feasible, years before it reached mainstream games.","m":[{"id":7301690,"author":"ilaksh","month":"2014-02","story":"WebGL raytraced eye","stance":"rec","why":"Near real-time GPU path tracing demo.","quote":"I wish nvidia or ATI would hire him.","text":"Why doesn't GPU hardware do native path tracing now? People (well at least 1 person) is/are using general purpose GPUs to do path tracing at almost real time. If they created GPUs with cores specifically for path tracing then we could have real time path tracing as soon as that was engineered. Whoever made this brigade 3 thing, I wish nvidia or ATI would hire him. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18946067,"author":"Geee","month":"2019-01","story":"Q2VKPT: Fully path traced Quake 2","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier real-time path tracing demo with more detailed geometry.","quote":"Take a look at this video from 2013","text":"It could look way better with more detailed geometry and proper materials. Most of those textures have baked-in lightning and lack normal maps and proper materials, which take away a lot of the effect. Take a look at this video from 2013: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7537914,"author":"mentos","month":"2014-04","story":"Pyro from Team Fortress 2 raytraced in your browser","stance":"reference","why":"Real-time path tracing demo needing two Titan GPUs.","quote":"Apparently requires 2 Titan's to render.","text":"[VIDEO] Apparently requires 2 Titan's to render. I wonder what you'd be able to pull off if money was no object"},{"id":7210910,"author":"ilaksh","month":"2014-02","story":"If you had to start over, what technologies would you learn in 2014?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of real-time ray tracing expected within a few years.","quote":"I believe that real time ray tracing is going to be a thing within a few years","text":"My favorite things right now which I am happy to recommend are ToffeeScript [LINK] with Node.js and Nimrod [LINK] . I also think LiveScript is awesome. AngularJS is great. Web Components are better I think though. Docker has redefined devops in my opinion [LINK] GLSL is very cool and something you can even experiment with in your browser using Three.js etc. I believe that real time ray tracing is going to be a thing within a few years [VIDEO] [LINK] CoreOS looks very interesting. WebRTC is a technology that could almost make an industry irrelevant. [LINK] Understanding APIs around bitcoin and cryptocurrency in general seems important. I believe that the internet is eventually going to be switching away from normal named server structures to a content-oriented architecture. [LINK] There are a lot of ways this category of architecture is actually currently being deployed behind the scenes, for example CDNs or bittorrent. I believe that we are going to start using that type of system more and more and eventually we will lose things like the HTTP layer. This may be combined with some of the technologies related to cryptocurrency. For an example of why we are moving to named data networking, take a look at well.. just about any web application that wants to scale. For example the npm registry. Also consider the issues around privacy with Facebook and the NSA, and the ability for governments to censor or take down web sites or domains. So any technologies that facilitate things lik"}]},"ckBfbvOXDvU":{"wy":"HN cites it as a classic illustration of dysfunctional management, gamed targets, and blaming workers for systemic outcomes.","q":{"x":"It is a fascinating insight into how dysfunctional management systems operate and perpetuate.","i":10940451,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":10940451,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2016-01","story":"Why Is Your Team Falling Behind? Ask ‘The Penny Game’","stance":"rec","why":"Deming's Red Bead Experiment shows how dysfunctional management systems operate","quote":"It is a fascinating insight into how dysfunctional management systems operate and perpetuate.","text":"A similar example regarding quality is Dr Deming's famous Red Bead Experiment. It is a fascinating insight into how dysfunctional management systems operate and perpetuate. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45317274,"author":"GarnetFloride","month":"2025-09","story":"If you are good at code review, you will be good at using AI agents","stance":"reference","why":"Compares AI agent hype to the Red Bead Experiment","quote":"It's the Red Bead Experiment, all over again.","text":"Oh man, that's what I've been smelling with all this. It's the Red Bead Experiment, all over again. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29863434,"author":"ericalexander0","month":"2022-01","story":"The Rise of Performative Work","stance":"reference","why":"Red beads illustrate targets gamed outside workers' control","quote":"Any system with a grade will be gamified to hit arbitrary targets.","text":"Given enough time. Yes, your surgeon has this attitude. Any system with a grade will be gamified to hit arbitrary targets. Even if those targets do not map back to customer value. Even if the targets are out of their control. Aka red beads. [VIDEO] “Tell me how you will measure me, and then I will tell you how I will behave. If you measure me in an illogical way, don’t complain about illogical behavior.” – Eli Goldratt"},{"id":17343335,"author":"daly","month":"2018-06","story":"Ask HN: How to not fail on coding interview questions?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Send your architect home to build a rocking chair over his weekend. See if he calls you back on Monday. A take home test is a very clear statement by management that your time is less important than their process. As a professional, you only have your time and skill as your saleable assets. You have the choice of giving them away for free. You could, of course, take the test and then present them a bill for your services. Another interesting question to ask would be: \"How many of your senior management were hired as programmers who passed a whiteboard test or a take home test?\" Clearly, according to the market myth, those who did pass were \"the best and the brightest\". It only stands to reason that upper management is composed of programmers. Ball-pits are in plain sight, if you choose to see. Ball-pit companies who whiteboard and use take home tests are also in plain sight, if you choose to see beyond the \"best and brightest\" market hype. I really respected a Lisp legendary programmer, Daniel Weinreb. He worked at ITA software on airline reservation software. We had many discussions. I'd love to have worked with him. But ITA has the same testing game and I couldn't convince myself to bother. I'm certain I could pass their tests. I've been programming in Lisp since the 1970s. And ITA was a 5-star restaurant kind of company. But those tests are a \"Clown-Nose\" warning to me. I just walked away. Walking away has a cost. But so does management games like quarterly status reviews,"},{"id":14783310,"author":"daly","month":"2017-07","story":"Uncle Bob Explains the Programmer's Oath (Videos)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Number 8, software estimates are complete nonsense. Read [LINK] I worked at a startup. Just before I was hired they bid on a fixed-cost 5 programmer, 5 month contract. After 10 programmers for 18 months they fired all of the (over 2 dozen) programmers and the company folded. At another startup I was given a project when the prior programmer left after 2 weeks. The project estimate was 8 weeks. I did an analysis (140 items, 4 of which had been completed) and estimated that the project would, at minimum, take over 6 months. The next day I was fired. IBM used to put out contract bids at 3 firms to build the frames for their mainframe computers. The independent bid estimates were delivered with time estimates within a day and costs with a penny. They delivered on time and on cost every time. Moral: If you have a repeatable task then you can provide an estimate. If you are doing a task for the first time, as most programmers are, then you CANNOT make a reasonable estimate. You can't even make a good guess. The only HONEST answer is \"I don't know\". Bob suggests providing 3 answers, minimum, likely, and maximum (aka NASA PERT charts). I tried that. The ONLY one management every hears is the minimum. They provide that number to the customer and record that as your target. Look at Edward Demming's work. Demming came up with principles that transformed Japan. Programming needs an Edward Demming. In fact, your job depends on your performance even on things you cannot control like estima"}],"r":[["yvFKh_3evC8",1],["FACK2knC08E",1],["n-AbPav5E5M",1],["HCUct4NlxE0",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["3kEfedtQVOY",1]]},"fiD24uEvY1U":{"wy":"Recommended as useful background on Russia's growing influence in West Africa and France's role there.","q":{"x":"I found this video to be particularly useful","i":40252161,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40252161,"author":"junto","month":"2024-05","story":"Russian troops enter base housing US Military in Niger, US official says","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Russia's growing influence in West Africa","quote":"I found this video to be particularly useful","text":"For anyone who is interested in Russia’s growing influence in West Africa and the sub-Sahara region, with the influx of the Wagner Group and the loss of control by France and other western powers, I found this video to be particularly useful: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43868844,"author":"aurareturn","month":"2025-05","story":"Irish privacy watchdog hits TikTok with €530M fine over data transfers to China","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of French actions in Africa","quote":"","text":"EU has issues with human rights violations regularly happening in China. China has even sanctioned European politicians voicing critique. Does China sanction France for its role in suppressing parts of Africa?[0] In the video comments, a lot of French citizens never even heard about it. That tells you that the French government is also hiding a ton of atrocities it's committing to its citizens, right? Why is the EU judging when they still violate human rights themselves? I think when Europeans criticize China for not having the same values as them, they should learn some history about how European powers tried to tear China apart and take advantage of it. Start with the photo of Austria-Hungary, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, Russia, the United States and the United Kingdom forces inside the Forbidden City.[1] Perhaps China not having the same values of European powers is a good thing in terms of overall peace? I would love to hear from EU citizens on why they should be the judge of all human moral values when their countries have arguably done more collectively to disturb the peace of others in the last 300 years than any country in Asia. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":42894854,"author":"Zenst","month":"2025-02","story":"Five years of Brexit reshaped Britain","stance":"reference","why":"Touches on the CFA franc arrangement with France","quote":"It also touched on here","text":"[LINK] is worth a read, there are few videos that I shall try to dig out that covers matters in far more depth It also touched on here: [VIDEO] Basically, the CFA got locked into the Euro currency when France joined it penalised African countries (former French colonies) into it as the CFA was pegged to the French Franc. This made those former colonies crippled by exports to anywhere apart from France who with the CFA setup that those African countries having to store 50% of their currency reserves with France at a 0.75% interest rate. Combined with the subsequent Euro pegging, that alone penalised those former African colonies with the CFA. Equally, they gained nothing from France joining the Euro and lots more pain."}],"r":[["TRL7o2kPqw0",1],["hC3VTgIPoGU",1]]},"gocwRvLhDf8":{"wy":"HN links it whenever IDs, base64 or random identifier space come up, as a short and pleasant explainer.","q":{"x":"There's a Tom Scott video [2] that describes Youtube video IDs as 11-digit base-64 random numbers","i":47065778,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":26149094,"author":"apocalyptic0n3","month":"2021-02","story":"YouTubers have to declare ads. Why doesn't anyone else? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott's short explanation of YouTube IDs","quote":"is such an uplifting thing to watch.","text":"Him dancing around and celebrating at the end of his 5 minute explanation of YouTube IDs [1] is such an uplifting thing to watch. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47065778,"author":"ekipan","month":"2026-02","story":"Cosmologically Unique IDs","stance":"reference","why":"Describes YouTube IDs as 11-digit base-64 random numbers","quote":"There's a Tom Scott video [2] that describes Youtube video IDs as 11-digit base-64 random numbers","text":"I forget the context but the other day I also learned about Snowflake IDs [1] that are apparently used by Twitter, Discord, Instagram, and Mastodon. Timestamp + random seems like it could be a good tradeoff to reduce the ID sizes and still get reasonable characteristics, I'm surprised the article didn't explore there (but then again \"timestamps\" are a lot more nebulous at universal scale I suppose). Just spitballing here but I wonder if it would be worthwhile to reclaim ten bits of the Snowflake timestamp and use the low 32 bits for a random number. Four billion IDs for each second. There's a Tom Scott video [2] that describes Youtube video IDs as 11-digit base-64 random numbers, but I don't see any official documentation about that. At the end he says how many IDs are available but I don't think he considers collisions via the birthday paradox. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37734682,"author":"dajonker","month":"2023-10","story":"Bye Sequence, Hello UUIDv7","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Similar to the old situation in the article, we are using sequential 64 bit primary keys, but we use an additional random 64 bit key for external usage (instead of 128 bit). The external key is base64 encoded for use in URLs which results in an 11 byte string. This hides any information about the size of the data, the creation date of customer accounts (which would be sort of visible with UUIDv7) and prevents anyone from attempting to enumerate data by changing the integer in URLs. I thought about using UUIDs as external keys but the only compelling use case seems to be the ability to generate keys from many decoupled sources that have to be merged later. 64 bit should be enough for most things [VIDEO]"},{"id":28193241,"author":"kaliszad","month":"2021-08","story":"India is leading IPv6 migration with 61.67% adoption","stance":"reference","why":"Explains YouTube's 64-bit random video IDs","quote":"You can watch Tom Scott explain it","text":"Well, have fun guessing 2^64 possibilities. YouTube lists even private videos \"secured\" using 11 BASE64 characters (66 bits in theory, but they seem to use just 64 bits). You can watch Tom Scott explain it: [VIDEO] CG-NAT doesn't really prevent geolocation. Better services will still pin-point you to the nearest city. There are perhaps easier ways to get your private info or your money - phishing and ransomware seem to be still very popular. Don't have to hack games that only relatively few people have. It is more profitable to attack a bigger market or more wealthy institutions or companies in foreign countries. Also, if you hack the central game server, you will have a lot more victims... Choose your poison. I guess, there are no games or other software that cannot be audited in high security installations. At home, having a work computer and a game computer (or a VM with GPU pass through or whatever) might be a safer choice in any case independent of IPv4 or IPv6 usage or the quality of your firewall."},{"id":27617307,"author":"tabletthrowaway","month":"2021-06","story":"“Older unlisted videos will be made private unless you opt out”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Obligatory","text":"Obligatory: [VIDEO] There’s too many IDs to iterate through. Searching for $defatltvideoname for some camera is far more effective for that. Scarily so."},{"id":40921553,"author":"Vicinity9635","month":"2024-07","story":"PySkyWiFi: Free stupid wi-fi on long-haul flights","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It is ridiculous and unnecessary, but when has that ever stopped programmers?","text":">Author and programmer Sam Hughes, by the way, pushed this to the limit and invented Base 65,536, which includes basically every character from every language. It is ridiculous and unnecessary, but when has that ever stopped programmers? [VIDEO]"},{"id":34744387,"author":"ChickenNugger","month":"2023-02","story":"Show HN: Spaghettify – A VSCode Extension to make your code worse with AI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"from one of my favorite Tom Scott videos","text":"Two thoughts: * This is ridiculous. Why would you do this? Which made think of this quote: \"Which is ridiculous and unnecessary but when has that ever stopped programmers?\" from one of my favorite Tom Scott videos: [VIDEO] * How to Write Unmaintainable Code: [LINK]"}]},"jNa3axo40qM":{"wy":"Cited as the canonical demo of multiple cursors and of fluent, efficient Emacs editing.","q":{"x":"One of the better examples of the usefulness of multiple cursors is from Emacs Rocks","i":47566407,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47566407,"author":"wilkystyle","month":"2026-03","story":"Neovim 0.12.0","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates usefulness of multiple cursors over macros.","quote":"One of the better examples of the usefulness of multiple cursors is from Emacs Rocks","text":"dot repeat is the wrong comparison. A closer one would be macros, but even then a good multiple cursors implementation is often faster, more intuitive, and requires less cognitive overhead. One of the better examples of the usefulness of multiple cursors is from Emacs Rocks (link goes to 0:23): [VIDEO]"},{"id":23430006,"author":"bjoli","month":"2020-06","story":"Julia as a CLI Calculator","stance":"reference","why":"Example of fluent emacs workflow by magnars.","quote":"Not as fluent as magnars, but this is a good example of what I mean","text":"I have all commands I don't remember bound to leader keys. When in calc I have [leader key] m and then which-key pops up everything I have stored. Other than that, I use scratch in elisp mode and eval. Not as fluent as magnars, but this is a good example of what I mean (towards the end): [VIDEO]"},{"id":8577090,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2014-11","story":"Big things to expect from Emacs 25","stance":"reference","why":"Shows multiple cursors exist in emacs.","quote":"","text":"I don't know how much faster navigating a buffer can be than doing it in emacs or vim, both of which can switch buffers with the press of a single key and do fuzzy-matching of buffer/file names with a keystroke or two. They can also navigate by moving or clicking around tree-structured directory trees, through menus, and many other ways. Again, it would be nice if people offered specifics when they described something another editor could do that emacs or vim allegedly couldn't. How exactly do you navigate a buffer in Sublime that's allegedly \"much quicker\"? Can you give an example (including exactly which keys you press and exactly what it does in response)? Or can you at least show a video demonstrating this navigation? As for multiple cursors, both vim and emacs have had them for years.[1][2][3][4][5] [1] - [LINK] [2] - [LINK] [3] - [LINK] [4] - [LINK] [5] - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8bZh5LMaSmE",1]]},"k47u9tduwb8":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of delivery platforms accepting listings without hygiene inspection, in ghost kitchen discussions.","m":[{"id":21380686,"author":"esotericn","month":"2019-10","story":"On-Line Pizza Idea Is Clever but Only Half-Baked (1994)","stance":"rec","why":"YouTuber sells microwave meals on Deliveroo.","quote":"You might also appreciate this","text":"They already do have a portion of the business that does that. I worked for them for a few weeks in London as a way to get paid to work out. There was a sort of container warehouse in Battersea - basically food trucks that you're ordering from. You might also appreciate this: > I Sold Microwave Meals On Deliveroo [VIDEO]"},{"id":23218164,"author":"333c","month":"2020-05","story":"Doordash and Pizza Arbitrage","stance":"reference","why":"Video matching the delivery and reimbursement details of the original comment.","quote":"This video features (a) delivery and (b) reimbursement","text":"While that is a good video (and demonstrates manipulation of TripAdvisor), I'm pretty sure the commenter means this one: [VIDEO] This video features (a) delivery and (b) reimbursement, both mentioned in the original comment but not present in your video."},{"id":21894241,"author":"alexmingoia","month":"2019-12","story":"Farm to Table? More Like Ghost Kitchen to Sofa","stance":"reference","why":"YouTuber sold microwave meals on Deliveroo without inspection.","quote":"spoiler: Deliveroo let them without inspection, and someone bought it!","text":"This YouTuber tried selling Microwave meals on Deliveroo (spoiler: Deliveroo let them without inspection, and someone bought it!): [VIDEO]"},{"id":21894469,"author":"dpflan","month":"2019-12","story":"Farm to Table? More Like Ghost Kitchen to Sofa","stance":"critical","why":"Shows Deliveroo's hygiene inspection is not a blocker for accepting orders.","quote":"That is flabbergasting.","text":"This is great. From the video, a Deliveroo representative states that the \"hygiene inspection\" is not a blocker for the \"Italian Stallion\" to begin accepting orders. That is flabbergasting. > [VIDEO]"}]},"mbdXeRBbgDM":{"wy":"Mostly cited by the author himself as the introduction to Clasp and Cando, and recommended to those wanting Lisp with C++ library interop.","q":{"x":"those interested in this use case might also be interested in Clasp, a Common Lisp implementation built on LLVM","i":20417668,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20417668,"author":"0xcde4c3db","month":"2019-07","story":"Racket Is an Acceptable Python","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces Clasp, Lisp on LLVM combining dynamic development with C++ libraries.","quote":"those interested in this use case might also be interested in Clasp, a Common Lisp implementation built on LLVM","text":"While it's nowhere near as mature as Racket, those interested in this use case might also be interested in Clasp, a Common Lisp implementation built on LLVM specifically to enable the combination of Lisp-style dynamic development approaches with C++ libraries. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36195446,"author":"nomilk","month":"2023-06","story":"Why Lisp Syntax Works","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence that Google Flights uses Lisp.","quote":"I think Google Flights uses lisp [1]","text":"> then we'd both go back to whatever language we were actually using at the time. Which well known projects do use lisp? I think Google Flights uses lisp [1]; there must be more? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33428121,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2022-11","story":"Christian Schafmeister – Atomically Precise Catalysts","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among Schafmeister presentations.","quote":"","text":"More presentations! I think two are almost the same presentation: [LINK] (July 2010) 10S6P4 Christian Schafmeister 2010 UNTT 3rd Annual Conference Session 6 Presentation 4 [LINK] (July 2015) Clasp: Common Lisp using LLVM and C++ for Molecular Metaprogramming [LINK] (Aug 2015) Clasp: Common Lisp using LLVM and C++ for Designing Molecules [LINK] (Oct 2018) Modular Polymer Catalysts - Chris Schafmeister [VIDEO] (Nov 2018) LLVM Developers Meeting: C. Schafmeister lessons Learned Implementing Common Lisp with LLVM"},{"id":27297145,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2021-05","story":"SICL: A New Common Lisp Implementation","stance":"reference","why":"One of several Clasp talks listed.","quote":"","text":"Ah, related to #clasp: [LINK] Clasp: Common Lisp using LLVM and C++ for Molecular Metaprogramming [VIDEO] 2018 LLVM Developers Meeting: C. Schafmeister lessons Learned Implementing Common Lisp with LLVM [LINK] Clasp: Common Lisp using LLVM and C++ for Designing Molecules [LINK] Modular Polymer Catalysts - Chris Schafmeister [LINK] 10S6P4 Christian Schafmeister 2010 UNTT 3rd Annual Conference Session 6 Presentation 4"},{"id":24643238,"author":"drmeister","month":"2020-09","story":"Energy Efficiency across Programming Languages [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker's recent talk on building molecules with Cando.","quote":"I do have a couple of talks on YouTube","text":"I do have a couple of talks on YouTube and thank you (the most recent one: [VIDEO] ). Yes - we are building molecules using our code. I have started a company and we are several months in to developing a new technology for warding off future pandemics (maybe even doing something about the current one). We literally started compiling the first molecules using an application I implemented in Cando (Clasp + computational chemistry) yesterday."},{"id":19128740,"author":"drmeister","month":"2019-02","story":"LispOS: Specification of a Lisp operating system (2013) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing Cando, a programming environment for molecular devices.","quote":"Clasp is the foundation of Cando - a programming environment for the development of molecular devices ( ).","text":"A growing group of us are implementing Clasp ( [LINK] ) - a new Common Lisp that uses Robert Strandh's Cleavir compiler, interoperates with C++ and uses LLVM as the backend. Clasp is the foundation of Cando - a programming environment for the development of molecular devices ( [VIDEO] ). Robert Strandh is an exceptionally clear thinker and a fantastic programmer. I can't count the times that Robert has implemented changes to the Cleavir compiler source code and without him being able to test it (long story) passed it to me and it ran flawlessly in Clasp. I implemented his fast generic function solution within Clasp based on another of his papers ( [LINK] ). Edit: Basically - if Robert writes it up in a paper - I want to have the implementation. Once we develop molecular robots - they are going to implement LispOS. :-)"},{"id":18687505,"author":"drmeister","month":"2018-12","story":"The LLVM Compiler Infrastructure","stance":"reference","why":"Author's talk on implementing Common Lisp on LLVM and handling upgrades.","quote":"I talk about it here at about 13:30","text":"Counterpoint - I implemented a Common Lisp compiler that uses llvm as the back end ( [LINK] ). I've kept up with the versions from 3.6 to 6.0 and I'm about to upgrade to 7.0. I'm not sure why other projects find this difficult but I have noticed that if I get too far behind it does get a bit more difficult. I exposed the C++ API to Common Lisp and write all of the compiler in Common Lisp. The Clang C++ compiler tells me what is broken when I upgrade to a new version of llvm and then I fix it. I talk about it here [VIDEO] at about 13:30"},{"id":18757752,"author":"drmeister","month":"2018-12","story":"Creating a Non-Trivial Lisp Game in 2018","stance":"reference","why":"Author's 25 minute talk introducing Clasp, Common Lisp on LLVM.","quote":"I have a 25 min talk up on youtube that I presented a couple of weeks ago at the 2018 llvm developers meeting","text":"My group is developing Clasp (github.com/clasp-developers/clasp.git). It's a Common Lisp implementation that interoperates with C++ and uses llvm as the backend. It's something you might want to look at because it could change your options in the following ways: (1) Clasp interoperates with C++ - C++ libraries like graphics and physics engines can be easily exposed within Clasp Common Lisp. (See [LINK] ) (2) Clasp uses llvm as the backend - it generates llvm-ir from Common Lisp code and uses link time optimization to link C++ and Common Lisp generated llvm-ir. (3) Clasp uses the Memory Pool System garbage collector, which can be configured to minimize GC pause times to a programmer-defined level. (4) Clasp generates debug info and can be profiled with standard tools like dtrace and C++ functions, Common Lisp functions and GC functions are all profiled against each other. I'm a professor of Chemistry at Temple University - I'm developing Clasp to design molecules and materials - but it could be applied to a lot of other problems. Caveat: Clasp is not yet as performant as Steel Bank Common Lisp, but it uses the open source and highly configurable Cleavir compiler developed by Robert Strandh and we have incorporated inlining and are currently incorporating type inference. If you want to see a little bit more - I have a 25 min talk up on youtube that I presented a couple of weeks ago at the 2018 llvm developers meeting ( [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["jCC8fPQOaxU",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1]]},"pgaEE27nsQw":{"wy":"HN cites it as an earlier, smoother precedent whenever new learned-locomotion or simulated-walking work appears.","q":{"x":"Which made me revisit this classic","i":26919895,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26919895,"author":"npilk","month":"2021-04","story":"Discovering Diverse Athletic Jumping Strategies","stance":"rec","why":"A classic of simulated creatures learning to move.","quote":"Which made me revisit this classic","text":"Very interesting. I guess it shouldn't be too surprising that the Fosbury Flop seems to be the optimal high jump method, even if it wasn't developed until the 1960s. I didn't see the link to the video with animations at first! It's here in case anyone else missed it: [LINK] Which made me revisit this classic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32571410,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2022-08","story":"Java in the Browser","stance":"reference","why":"Paper on two-legged animals learning different methods of walking.","quote":"There is a paper on two-legged animals learning different methods of walking","text":"There is a paper on two-legged animals learning different methods of walking: [VIDEO] (or [LINK] for the slightly better known \"music video\" version). Soda Constructor's wikipedia page also links to a 3D applet named \"Springs World 3D\", I was playing with that one back in the day."},{"id":30742069,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2022-03","story":"Evolved Virtual Creatures (1994)","stance":"reference","why":"Similar vibe to evolved virtual creatures.","quote":"also has a similar vibe","text":"swangin.mp4 AKA \"Flexible Muscle-Based Locomotion for Bipedal Creatures\" also has a similar vibe: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28242728,"author":"elihu","month":"2021-08","story":"Tesla Announces a Humanoid Robot “Tesla Bot”","stance":"reference","why":"Training an AI to walk through simulation.","quote":"this video on training an AI to walk through simulation","text":"I would hope that they can use a framework that's general enough to adapt to other form factors. So, making a humanoid is a way of giving themselves a difficult enough initial problem that they're less likely to be fooled by early success: the humanoid robot will have to self-balance, walk, open doors, and do a lot of the complex things that humans do without thinking in order to be considered a minimum-viable product. In the long run if this pans out, I'd expect to see farming and weeding hexapods, equipment-hauling centaurs, wheeled platforms with arms for getting around office spaces, construction bots with long-fingered hands and asymmetrical multi-jointed appendages, and so on. If the AI has a general capacity for motor-skill learning, then you can just give it whatever body is most optimal for the task at hand. I'm reminded of this video on training an AI to walk through simulation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23735238,"author":"amelius","month":"2020-07","story":"Researchers simulate a quadruped robot crossing a balance beam","stance":"reference","why":"Example of years-old ML walking models.","quote":"","text":"Since we have machine-learning based walking models for several years now, see e.g. [1] or [2], I'm wondering why walking isn't yet a solved problem by now (?) [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":19595804,"author":"amelius","month":"2019-04","story":"Scalable Muscle-Actuated Human Simulation and Control","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier work on the same topic.","quote":"","text":"This seems very useful work. But I wonder why it took so long to get here since [1] was published. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17068318,"author":"amelius","month":"2018-05","story":"Getting some air, Atlas? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This was from 2013","text":"This was from 2013: [VIDEO] I'm wondering what challenges we have solved since then, beside the mechanical implementation."},{"id":14759904,"author":"anotheryou","month":"2017-07","story":"Google's DeepMind AI just taught itself to walk","stance":"reference","why":"Smoother 2013 muscle and nerve simulation of learning to walk.","quote":"Looks much smoother, because of muscle and nerve simulations.","text":"Clearly needs some energy constraint factored in to look less like monty python :) I also wonder if the gravity is realistic. A similar video from 2013: [VIDEO] Looks much smoother, because of muscle and nerve simulations. At 0:55 you can see the generations learning how to walk."}],"r":[["DXvAlwMAxiA",1]]},"rvLFEh7V18A":{"wy":"HN points to it as investigative journalism backing the view that vintage game prices are inflated by wash trading and grading services.","q":{"x":"It's almost an hour long piece of effectively investigative journalism","i":28313081,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28313081,"author":"daemin","month":"2021-08","story":"OnlyFans drops planned porn ban","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long investigation into why vintage games cost so much","quote":"It's almost an hour long piece of effectively investigative journalism","text":"I just saw this video ( [VIDEO] ) on why vintage video games cost so much money now. It's almost an hour long piece of effectively investigative journalism by someone in the speed running (and thus vintage gaming) community. The evidence presented does seem like a bubble, with scam/fraud properties."},{"id":38706312,"author":"Baldbvrhunter","month":"2023-12","story":"Collection of '90s video games from Nebraska shop could sell for $1M","stance":"reference","why":"Wata Games scam exposure","quote":"I hope they didn't use Wata Games Scam auction prices as a guide.","text":"I hope they didn't use Wata Games Scam auction prices as a guide. Exposing FRAUD And DECEPTION In The Retro Video Game Market Karl Jobst 2.2M views 2 years ago [VIDEO] The Retro Video Game Market Has Officially CRASHED 1.3M views 1 month ago [LINK]"},{"id":38710221,"author":"Baldbvrhunter","month":"2023-12","story":"He closed a video game shop decades ago. His games could sell for $1M","stance":"reference","why":"Wata Games scam exposure","quote":"I hope they didn't use Wata Games Scam auction prices as a guide.","text":"I hope they didn't use Wata Games Scam auction prices as a guide. Exposing FRAUD And DECEPTION In The Retro Video Game Market Karl Jobst 2.2M views 2 years ago [VIDEO] The Retro Video Game Market Has Officially CRASHED 1.3M views 1 month ago [LINK]"},{"id":31676247,"author":"teddyh","month":"2022-06","story":"A forgery scandal that’s roiling PC game collecting","stance":"reference","why":"Collectible games overvalued, likely a scam","quote":"collectible games are currently overvalued as part of what is probably a huge scam","text":"Even when they are genuine, collectible games are currently overvalued as part of what is probably a huge scam: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31932383,"author":"stevekemp","month":"2022-06","story":"Rare video games collection heist","stance":"reference","why":"Expose on graders and auction houses inflating prices","quote":"There has been a lot of people calling out the role of game-graders and auction-houses in artificially raising the prices of video-games.","text":"There has been a lot of people calling out the role of game-graders and auction-houses in artificially raising the prices of video-games. For example this expose: [VIDEO] But I think it's fair to say that the people who are paying these prices are not interested in gaming per se, instead seeing their purchases as investments - after all the prices keep going up, and their grades are \"the best\". What could go wrong? (Ignoring the fact that millions of cartridges exist, of course.)"},{"id":31932483,"author":"hnbad","month":"2022-06","story":"Rare video games collection heist","stance":"reference","why":"Prices inflated by wash trading via auction houses","quote":"the prices for vintage video games are artificially inflated and largely the result of wash trading via auction houses and grading services","text":"This is as good a time as any to remind people that the prices for vintage video games are artificially inflated and largely the result of wash trading via auction houses and grading services, which locked out the preexisting retro gaming communities and turned a beloved hobby into an instrument for dubious financial speculation (and arguably set the precedent for NFT crypto trading, which has even fewer regulations and even less transparency that could prevent these wash trades): [VIDEO] The story says the collector described his collection as an \"archive\". This flies in the face of actual archival efforts like The Internet Archive, which persist digital copies of old games and make them available to the public when possible. Having a large collection of rare games in pristine condition in locked cases in your vault is not archival, it's hoarding at worst and financial speculation at best."},{"id":29822616,"author":"CyberRabbi","month":"2022-01","story":"Washed Up","stance":"reference","why":"Retro game auctions resemble NFT phenomenon","quote":"I’m noticing a lot of similarities between the NFT phenomenon and the auction house retro game phenomenon","text":"I’m noticing a lot of similarities between the NFT phenomenon and the auction house retro game phenomenon: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29448076,"author":"bartread","month":"2021-12","story":"The Retro Video Game Scam Gets Worse [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier video preceding the follow-up","quote":"","text":"This is a follow-up piece to Karl's earlier video, \"Exposing FRAUD and DECEPTION in The Retro Video Game Market,\" from a few months back, which you can find at [VIDEO] ."},{"id":28473629,"author":"sgarman","month":"2021-09","story":"Twenty percent of a picture of a dog","stance":"reference","why":"Retro game market inflation","quote":"","text":"I know this isn't a new concept but this is also happening heavily in the retro video game industry as well. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bY2FlayomlE",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1]]},"zqmOSMAtadc":{"wy":"Linked in rail and heat-related threads as the clear explanation of why railroads need no expansion joints and how heat problems are handled.","q":{"x":"Practical Engineering already explained the correct solution to this problem","i":48953510,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48953510,"author":"jeffrallen","month":"2026-07","story":"Painting the sides of railroad rails white to reduce derailment","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the correct solution to rail heat problems.","quote":"Practical Engineering already explained the correct solution to this problem","text":"Practical Engineering already explained the correct solution to this problem: [VIDEO] But of course, American infrastructure was built on the cheap, and is not maintained correctly. This is why we can't have nice things."},{"id":46685802,"author":"whizzter","month":"2026-01","story":"At least 21 killed in Spain after crash involving high-speed trains","stance":"reference","why":"Explains continuous welded rail and cold-weather inspections.","quote":"goes through track building and the effects","text":"Not going to be claiming to be an expert, but buckling is a well documented phenomenon and I'd be surprised if there wasn't possible issues due to contraction on the other end of the spectrum when it gets colder, the track was laid this last spring/summer so it's probably not been as cold for long before on the tracks. Also modern high speed rails are built with continious welding without thermal expansion joints. [VIDEO] goes through track building and the effects and literally mentions at about 12:30 that they do need to do inspections when it gets cold if the track cracks (there was a photo linked in another post about a cracked track). Is it the cause? No idea but doesn't feel far fetched. Edit: Seems official investigators are now even pointing to that as an initial theory, [LINK]"},{"id":41257811,"author":"lesuorac","month":"2024-08","story":"America Has a Hot-Steel Problem","stance":"reference","why":"How rails are welded with thermite.","quote":"They also expand the rail with thermite","text":"They also expand the rail with thermite which is probably hotter than the sun is going to make the earth. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41532592,"author":"kragen","month":"2024-09","story":"Who Owns Nebula?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"real engineering manages to pack plenty of misstatements into videos that are equally superficial","text":"admittedly, it does fall far short of being practical; if your preparation for building a railroad is watching [VIDEO] and your preparation for building a landfill is watching [LINK] , your trains will derail and your garbage will leach into your groundwater but that's not really a difference between the two channels; real engineering manages to pack plenty of misstatements into videos that are equally superficial"}]},"-bMzFDpfDwc":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it in unrelated threads as evidence of European speech restrictions, and some commenters call it propaganda.","m":[{"id":46018482,"author":"IlikeKitties","month":"2025-11","story":"The Censorship Network: Regulation and Repression in Germany Today","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Germany is very much implementing censorship by chilling effect.","text":"> Trusted Flaggers > 1 Pimmel Affäre > Schwachkopf Affäre Oder zuletzt > [LINK] and last but not least: > [VIDEO] Germany is very much implementing censorship by chilling effect."},{"id":44710460,"author":"DocTomoe","month":"2025-07","story":"EU age verification app to ban any Android system not licensed by Google","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The UK arrested around 12.5k people in 2023 over social media posts, according to the Times. Not all of them related to immigration at all (but I would wager: many are). It is sufficient to 'cause distress' - against whom, or in what form, or what qualifies as 'distress' is deliberately kept vague to maximise persecution rates. Some cases saw a squad car with six Bobbies take the very average, middle-class parents of a teenage daughter to the precinct to question them on why their daughter has had strong opinions on the way her school's new head was chosen [1]. While - as so often - no-one was later sent to a court for sentencing, the chilling effects are there, and I'd say half a platoon of police officers descending on someone's front lawn is definitely \"getting in trouble with the law\". [1] [LINK] Money quote: > More often than not, the police record these episodes as “non-crime hate incidents”, with over 13,200 recorded last year. What’s so extraordinary about the police’s zealous pursuit of these non-criminals is that the rate at which they’re catching actual criminals is falling. Fewer than seven in 100 crimes now lead to a charge or summons, down from 17 in 100 in 2015. In the year to September 2023, the total number of burglaries left unsolved stood at 213,814, a rise of 4 per cent on the previous year. So ... police gets their numbers up persecuting people for wrong thought while they avoid having to deal with actual, real criminals which might fight back violently. "},{"id":44189215,"author":"StanislavPetrov","month":"2025-06","story":"OpenAI slams court order to save all ChatGPT logs, including deleted chats","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Germany, where they lock you up for criticizing politicians[1] or where they have a ban against protesting for Palestine because it's \"antisemitic\"?[2] Or UK where you can get locked up for blasphemy[3] or where they lock up ~30 people a day for saying offensive things online because of their Online Safety Act?[4] Or perhaps Romania where an election that didn't turn out the way the EU elites wanted is overturned based on nebulous (and later proven false) accusation that the election was somehow influenced by a TikTok campaign by the Russians that later turned out to have been funded by a Romanian opposition party.[5] I could go on and on, but unfortunately most other western democracies are just as flawed, if not worse. Hopefully we can all strive for a better future and flush the authoritarians, from all the parties. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":43265798,"author":"fwn","month":"2025-03","story":"The Demoralization is just Beginning","stance":"reference","why":"Clip that became a meme in Germany.","quote":"It was a bit of a meme in Germany.","text":"I'm not the parent, but one reference they made but didn't link was this 60 Minutes clip \"Policing the internet in Germany, where hate speech, insults are a crime\". It was a bit of a meme in Germany. [VIDEO] Still, the author of the original article has some pretty polarising and crude views, and I think it's valuable to keep that context in mind. The key is not to be lazy and just dismiss everything that doesn't come from the smoothest PR media personality. For me, it felt like reading a frustrated author arguing against over-reliance on the service sector as an economy, given the dependencies it creates. There is certainly nationalism, realism/geopolitical views and a somewhat raw criticism of the current monetary system in the mix. The author sprinkles a lot of cultural references all over it and concludes with a tongue-in-cheek hint at an accelerationist strategy. .. based on that random blogpost I probably still wouldn't buy any gold just yet."},{"id":43461642,"author":"FirmwareBurner","month":"2025-03","story":"European Cloud, Global Reach","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"If you insist: [VIDEO] It was pretty heavily debated on r/DE. [LINK] Please tell me how you think this \"protect one's honor\" law doesn't get abused. Also, an youtuber from my country made a video during the pandemic exposing the mistreatment of seasonal fruit picking workers from my country working in Germany, and the video got blocked in Germany by Youtube, most likely on request by flagging. So do do you expect to learn about censorship of German media if you follow German media which is subject to censorship? Do you realize the irony of it all? Did you ever expect for German media to go out and say \"here's how we're censoring you\"? Germans are living in Plato's cave allegory and are irate when people call them out for it."},{"id":43517251,"author":"FirmwareBurner","month":"2025-03","story":"ASML's boss has a warning for Europe","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"You can get arrested for a tweet insulting a politician","text":">US government's openly hostile attitude towards immigrants who simply exercised free speech Source for such claims please? German and UK governments are even more authoritarian on free speech. You can get arrested for a tweet insulting a politician: [VIDEO] >the US has touted freedom of speech frequently and loudly when chastising autocratic foreign powers So did Germany and Austria and most of western EU. They were buddy-buddy to Putin in the past for their gas, and now they're buddy-buddy with Azerbaijan who's slaughtering Armenians in Nagorno-Karabakh because EU needs their gas. [LINK] >to make examples of a handful of legal immigrants Source for such claims please? How many skilled legal immigrants have been deported or subject to illegal government oppression?"},{"id":43113653,"author":"FirmwareBurner","month":"2025-02","story":"DOGE has 'god mode' access to government data","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for claim about German speech laws.","quote":"Source","text":"Having a slow and archaic birocratic system doesn't stop governments going totalitarian on their citizens. Case in point In Germany the Polizei will SWAT and arrest you if you post a meme on social media that angers someone's dignity. That's not a joke that actually happens. This typical German \"our government is not slow and inefficient, it's just protection against totalitarianism\" is pure cope. Edit: @helloplanets Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43151089,"author":"sepositus","month":"2025-02","story":"It is no longer safe to move our governments and societies to US clouds","stance":"reference","why":"Interview showing insulting someone is a crime in Germany.","quote":"You can watch the interview yourself here","text":"I'm just using common sense here. If you can go to jail or be fined for saying something inflammatory online, you don't have freedom of speech. My \"there\" comment referenced Germany since that was my example and where the 60 Minutes segment was done. You can watch the interview yourself here: [VIDEO] It's worth pointing out the usage of \"insult\" in the interview. > \"Is it a crime to insult somebody in public?\" > \"Yes.\""},{"id":43194166,"author":"Clubber","month":"2025-02","story":"Jeff Bezos exerts more control of Washington Post opinion","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on German speech laws.","quote":"60 Minutes did a recent segment on German speech laws.","text":"One example is Germany will now arrest you for insulting someone in public or online, including insulting politicians. The UK has had some questionable laws passed as well. Pockets of Europe has much weaker free speech protections than the US does, that much is for certain. 60 Minutes did a recent segment on German speech laws. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45465359,"author":"IlikeKitties","month":"2025-10","story":"Germany must stand firmly against client-side scanning in Chat Control [pdf]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"And they brag about it on 60 minutes","text":"German speaking here: People here in Germany do not want to think about just how bad our constitution really is designed. We only get free speech lte, There's no fruit of the poisonous tree doctrine leading to constant prosecutorial overreach and illegal searches[0] that are later ruled illegal but with on effect and our public prosecutors are so much lacking in independence that they aren't allowed to issue european arrest warrants [1]. But I've heard people tell me with complete conviction how great our constitution is. I can't even bother arguing about it anymore. [0] And they brag about it on 60 minutes [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":45512984,"author":"looping__lui","month":"2025-10","story":"German government comes out against Chat Control","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"CBS News dedicated an entire episode on these “propaganda stories”…","text":"Well, CBS News dedicated an entire episode on these “propaganda stories”… [VIDEO] [LINK] Germany’s constitution (the Basic Law) does protect freedom of opinion and expression, but it explicitly allows restrictions via “general laws” to protect personal honor, youth, and human dignity. Recent enforcement shows how this plays out: police raids have targeted individuals posting “hate speech” or “extremist” content online. What constitutes hate speech or extremist content is “conveniently” interpreted at times."}]},"0oV4IVy8tvE":{"wy":"Used purely as a joke reference when gull-wing doors or tech-money excess come up.","m":[{"id":43107472,"author":"atmosx","month":"2025-02","story":"Ask HN: Small Ideas vs. Big Ideas?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The great American philosopher Russ Hanneman comes into mind","text":"> [...] what \"a very good life\" looks like [...] The great American philosopher Russ Hanneman comes into mind: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32234047,"author":"Brajeshwar","month":"2022-07","story":"Tech booms fuelled liveability busts in Silicon Valley and Bengaluru","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Gull-Wing Cars. Russ Hanneman in Silicon Valley. This should give you an idea","text":"Sorry for extremely vague but wanted it that way. The Gull-Wing Cars. Russ Hanneman in Silicon Valley. This should give you an idea [VIDEO]"},{"id":29727882,"author":"biztos","month":"2021-12","story":"Whatever happened to Flickr?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"leaving Mr Butterfield a few dollars shy of gull-wing doors[3].","text":"I know it's unhealthy, but I harbor a fantasy in which the modest and user-loving folks from SmugMug[0] pivot Flickr into an all-encompassing portal system a la iMode[1] and drive the productivity vampire Slack[2] out of business, ideally leaving Mr Butterfield a few dollars shy of gull-wing doors[3]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27164155,"author":"biztos","month":"2021-05","story":"TikTok Remix Culture","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"they're all driving McLarens with doors that open like this!","text":"The car guy (Daniel Mac) is awesome but the answers make me feel like I don't understand how money works. So many people doing things I would not have thought pay as well as software work, and they're all driving McLarens with doors that open like this! [VIDEO]"},{"id":9820995,"author":"Aqueous","month":"2015-07","story":"Tesla Beats Delivery Forecast with 52% Quarterly Surge","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"if Elon didn't insist on those doors, I'd be stuck with a fucking car whose doors open like this","text":"Besides, if Elon didn't insist on those doors, I'd be stuck with a fucking car whose doors open like this: [VIDEO]"}]},"21EYKqUsPfg":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the serious skeptic's case against LLMs reaching general intelligence, usually in debates about whether scaling is enough.","q":{"x":"Richard Sutton takes a different view in an interview with Dwarkesh Patel[2]","i":45805599,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48781814,"author":"solumos","month":"2026-07","story":"It Still Can't Do My Job: Four Years of Moving Goalposts (2022–2026)","stance":"reference","why":"Sutton's reasoning that LLMs are not intelligent like RL.","quote":"I align with Richard Sutton's reasoning","text":"Google's definition is sufficient[0]. I'd also go so far as to say that LLMs aren't even AI, but that can be a bit pedantic. I align with Richard Sutton's reasoning, in that LLMs (and GPT models more generally) aren't \"intelligent\" in the same way that RL models are[1]. [0] - [LINK] [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":49084023,"author":"mbesto","month":"2026-07","story":"A $500 RL fine-tune of a 9B open model beat frontier models on catalog review","stance":"reference","why":"Sutton's argument that scaling LLMs is a dead end.","quote":"This is basically the entire argument by Richard Sutton happening in real time","text":"This is basically the entire argument by Richard Sutton happening in real time: [VIDEO] Right now, we keep creating new models with larger and larger weights and throwing hardware at it. His argument is that this is a dead end and ultimately we'll eventually go back to purpose fit algorithms like we've always done in the history of AI development."},{"id":48025735,"author":"kbrkbr","month":"2026-05","story":"AI didn't delete your database, you did","stance":"reference","why":"Sutton cited as a serious skeptic of LLM intelligence.","quote":"very serious people have voiced doubts, for example Richard Sutton in an interview with Dwarkash Patel [1].","text":"I think they are not actually intelligent. Fix all random seeds and other sources of randomness, and try the same prompt twice, and check how intelligent that looks, as a first approximation. On a more technical level very serious people have voiced doubts, for example Richard Sutton in an interview with Dwarkash Patel [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45805599,"author":"j1mr10rd4n","month":"2025-11","story":"The Case That A.I. Is Thinking","stance":"reference","why":"Sutton's view that learning requires goals and rewards.","quote":"Richard Sutton takes a different view in an interview with Dwarkesh Patel[2]","text":"Geoffrey Hinton's recent lecture at the Royal Institute[1] is a fascinating watch. His assertion that human use of language being exactly analogous to neural networks with back-propagation really made me think about what LLMs might be able to do, and indeed, what happens in me when I \"think\". A common objection to LLM \"intelligence\" is that \"they don't know anything\". But in turn... what do biological intelligences \"know\"? For example, I \"know\" how to do things like write constructs that make complex collections of programmable switches behave in certain ways, but what do I really \"understand\"? I've been \"taught\" things about quantum mechanics, electrons, semiconductors, transistors, integrated circuits, instruction sets, symbolic logic, state machines, assembly, compilers, high-level-languages, code modules, editors and formatting. I've \"learned\" more along the way by trial and error. But have I in effect ended up with anything other than an internalised store of concepts and interconnections? (c.f. features and weights). Richard Sutton takes a different view in an interview with Dwarkesh Patel[2] and asserts that \"learning\" must include goals and reward functions but his argument seemed less concrete and possibly just a semantic re-labelling. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45546646,"author":"gsf_emergency_4","month":"2025-10","story":"Bitter lessons building AI products","stance":"reference","why":"Longer analysis of Sutton's views on the Bitter Lesson.","quote":"Longer analysis","text":"Rich Sutton, the guy behind both \"reinforcement learning\" & \"the Bitter Lesson\", muses that Tech needs to understand the Bitter Lesson better: [LINK] Longer analysis: [VIDEO] To (try and) summarize those in the context of TFA: builders need to distinguish between policy optimisations and program optimisations I guess a related question to ask (important for both startups and Big Tech) might be: \"should one focus on doing things that don't scale?\""},{"id":45608669,"author":"mbesto","month":"2025-10","story":"Claude Skills","stance":"reference","why":"Sutton argues LLMs mimic rather than learn from experience.","quote":"Which is precisely why Richard Sutton doesn't think LLMs will evolve to AGI[0].","text":"> Contrast with the way a human learns skills - as we gain experience with a skill, we get better at understanding when it's the right tool for the job. Which is precisely why Richard Sutton doesn't think LLMs will evolve to AGI[0]. LLMs are based on mimicry, not experience, so it's more likely (according to Sutton) that AGI will be based on some form of RL (reinforcement learning) and not neural networks (LLMs). More specifically, LLMs don't have goals and consequences of actions, which is the foundation for intelligence. So, to your point, the idea of a \"skill\" is more akin to a reference manual, than it is a skill building exercise that can be applied to developing an instrument, task, solution, etc. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"3EwZQddc3kY":{"wy":"Invoked as a joke whenever AI image reconstruction or enhancement is discussed.","m":[{"id":48152383,"author":"janfoeh","month":"2026-05","story":"Image-blaster: Creates 3D environments, SFX, and meshes from a single image","stance":"reference","why":"Movie scene of implausible image enhancement","quote":"the \"lingerie store scene\" from Enemy of the State","text":"I've never forgotten this SIGGRAPH demo from almost twenty years ago, in which the authors effectively switch camera and light source computationally (... in a static scene) [1] Ever since then, I have viewed scenes such as the \"lingerie store scene\" from Enemy of the State [2] with a little bit less eye rolling... [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":48651717,"author":"b1n","month":"2026-06","story":"Lift4D: Harmonizing Single-View 3D Estimation for 4D Reconstruction In-the-Wild","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Enemy of the State predicted this","text":"Enemy of the State predicted this: [VIDEO] It's only a matter of time before it's in use by law enforcement: [LINK]"},{"id":43110966,"author":"pitzips","month":"2025-02","story":"WonderHuman: 3D avatars from single-view video","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of that scene [1] from Enemy of the State","text":"Reminds me of that scene [1] from Enemy of the State where they \"Rotate us 75 degrees around the vertical\" in one of those \"enhance, enhance\" tropes. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24077413,"author":"trilinearnz","month":"2020-08","story":"NeRF in the Wild: reconstructing 3D scenes from internet photography","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Anyone remember this scene from Enemy of The State (1998)?","text":"Anyone remember this scene from Enemy of The State (1998)? [VIDEO] Jon Voight: \"Can the computer take us around to the other side?\" Jack Black: \"It can HYPOTHESIZE\" :)"},{"id":13657134,"author":"quirkafleeg","month":"2017-02","story":"Nobody Knows What They're Doing (2013)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I love that movie","text":"> the NSA not strictly having it's own wet-works arm doesn't make [the movie Enemy of the State's] 90-00's portrayal as a semi-omnipotent foe any less depressingly accurate I love that movie, but let's not say things we can't take back. [VIDEO] :)"}]},"9abrZwnPThE":{"wy":"Used as an example in debates on rideshare economics and Prop 22 of how platforms can exploit drivers.","m":[{"id":31706920,"author":"itake","month":"2022-06","story":"Starbucks may close its bathrooms to the public again","stance":"reference","why":"Interviews with homeless people pushed out by high rent","quote":"","text":"> Pushing people out on the streets due to eye watering rents also literally drives people insane. I've seen handful of YouTube videos interviewing homeless people that were pushed out due to high prices and none of them seem the drug-addict pee-on-the-starbucks-floor type. I've seen videos about homeless camps that self-regulate drug free zones for non-addicts. - [LINK] - [VIDEO] Definitely a sad situation, but functional people seem to try to continue to be functional. Hard worker being evicted for rent and resorting to drug use sounds like a nice narrative, but it feels fictional. Do you have any sources on people being evicted b/c of rent issues and becoming insane?"},{"id":26374029,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-03","story":"Do Digital Platforms Reduce Moral Hazard? The Case of Uber and Taxis","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how Uber can exploit its drivers","quote":"","text":"> Uber really shines in countries with a culture of haggling and gouging tourists. > It’s the ultimate travel “screw remover”. So why do you think this is? Are you aware that western capitalist firms fuck/screw these countries up, before their citizens fuck (with) us? [1] [2] Their dishonesty is based on having to survive to survive this original (and ongoing) exploitation. Do you really look at the global south and not see this? I'm not saying Uber doesn't help serve as an attempt at a digital system that helps sustain reputation (although it is one that can grossly exploit the driver). [3] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24987503,"author":"bergstromm466","month":"2020-11","story":"California passes Proposition 22, backed by Uber and Lyft","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of driver exploitation by platform companies","quote":"","text":"This will just cause a deepening of the Digital and Information Feudalism that is already going on. It will result in a violent revolution by the working classes, to throw off these chains, and to democratize the use and application of the underlying technologies used by Uber et al. I am sad that American citizens are subjected to these experiments by American billionaires. They are systemically dehumanized, exploited and pushed to their limits. [1] Why do people still stand behind copyrights and patents, in this age of zero-marginal cost reproduction and transmission of information? [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":24227171,"author":"bergstromm466","month":"2020-08","story":"Rideshare operations are being suspended in California","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an Uber driver living in her car","quote":"What about this example? 'Driving For Uber, Sleeping In Her Car' (VICE News)","text":"What about this example? 'Driving For Uber, Sleeping In Her Car' (VICE News): [VIDEO] > The existence of thousands of uber drivers -- who in my experience, have all been quite reasonable, nice people -- seem to directly contradict your claim that it does not pay a 'reasonable wage'. Clearly, it does for those invested parties. Maybe we can leave it up to the academic researchers and labor rights organizations? Why do you make a claim and link to no sources, please could you provide your sources? Here are some sources I found: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] > Why is the purpose of a wage to live on? This isn't a serious question is it?"}]},"9yW--eQaA2I":{"wy":"HN links it in discussions of 4D visualization and Miegakure as an intuitive demonstration of the fourth dimension.","q":{"x":"Miegakure is a not-yet-released 4D puzzle platformer. Think Flatland but as a 4D video game.","i":14215824,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15029236,"author":"garaetjjte","month":"2017-08","story":"Automatic Mapping of NES Games with Mappy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"taking 2D intersection of 3D world would lead to something like 2D/3D mode of Miegakure","text":"Aren't 3D games already 2D, as they are just projecting 3D world onto 2D plane? Alternatively, taking 2D intersection of 3D world would lead to something like 2D/3D mode of Miegakure: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14215824,"author":"Pulcinella","month":"2017-04","story":"The World of Miegakure","stance":"reference","why":"Introduces Miegakure as an unreleased 4D puzzle platformer","quote":"Miegakure is a not-yet-released 4D puzzle platformer. Think Flatland but as a 4D video game.","text":"For those wondering, Miegakure is a not-yet-released 4D puzzle platformer. Think Flatland but as a 4D video game. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10150617,"author":"p1esk","month":"2015-09","story":"Aphantasia: A life without mental images","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"trying to remember this video","text":"The test in the article was extremely easy for me, I guess I can visualize better than most people (I had no idea some people have trouble with that). It's funny, because I've considered myself to be an \"auditory\" person. Something I tried to visualize recently, and found quite challenging, was trying to remember this video: [VIDEO] I watched it a couple of years ago, and last night I replayed it in my head, but with great difficulty."},{"id":8234993,"author":"tomerico","month":"2014-08","story":"How to walk through walls using the 4th dimension [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Link to the relevant point in the video","text":"The plane exists - it is passing through the character body (perpendicular to the ground) and parallel to the desert, grass border in the example video. You can see when the 4D rotation occurs there, that in that plane, everything stays constant (e.g. rocks stay there, trees, etc.) You just look at the objects from different angles as it rotates. Edit: Link to the relevant point in the video - [VIDEO]"}]},"Bu1YFshFuI4":{"wy":"Cited in nuclear power threads as proof that waste casks survive severe crashes.","q":{"x":"If a nuclear waste cask gets hit by planes, trains, or automobiles, not much will happen.","i":41049675,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41049675,"author":"nick238","month":"2024-07","story":"A New Specialized Train Is Ready to Haul Nuclear Waste","stance":"reference","why":"Cask survives collision test.","quote":"If a nuclear waste cask gets hit by planes, trains, or automobiles, not much will happen.","text":"> My point was the existing solutions people were sure would work still leaked in relatively short time frames. What are you referring to? The aforementioned UF6 canisters were a) NOT designed for long-term holding, more adapted from existing HF storage canisters (you can read some of the fun of storing fluorine compounds in the book \"Ignition!\" about liquid-fueled rockets), and as mentioned b) high level nuclear waste is vitrified to turn it into a non-volatile, non-dissolving, ceramic material that is largely stable by itself. > ... 30000 years of facility maintenance costs ... There are a variety of unused mines that could house high-level waste; the excavation is already done, thus fairly cheap. We are already factoring in storage of the fuel in casks, because it already is being stored in casks. The missing part is transport (hence the article, which is still kinda silly as the casks were designed to be trucked). If a nuclear waste cask gets hit by planes, trains, or automobiles, not much will happen. [1] If an ordinary tanker car falls off the tracks, it's like an improvised chemical warfare attack. [2] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":32472335,"author":"kennend3","month":"2022-08","story":"Nuclear power is the climate superhero too nervous to wear its cape","stance":"reference","why":"Rocket-powered train hits a waste cask at 84 mph.","quote":"How much energy was delivered to the cask when a rocket powered train hit it at 84 mph?","text":"Have you ever seen a storage Cask used to transport/store nuclear waste after it leaves the pools? How much energy was delivered to the cask when a rocket powered train hit it at 84 mph? [VIDEO] How much radioactive material was released? Are you aware that in earths distant past there were natural nuclear reactors running? if you go back in time the amount of U235 increases. Go back far enough and you no longer need to \"enrich\" it to support a nuclear reactor. Care to guess how far the radioactive byproducts from this reactor travelled? Not sure about Ukraines designs, but here (Canada) our reactors can withstand the impact of a fully loaded jet slamming into them. Nothing is invincible, and i suppose if you it it enough you can open it up, but by then it would have been shutdown and had the fuel removed (a unique design of CANDU allows them to be refuled while operating, so you can remove the fuel this way too). you can come up with all sorts of event and wonder if a reactor can survive them, perhaps a meteor strikes it? Perhaps military? Perhaps like Pickering it runs for its entire lifetime and very little happens? There are also facts to consider, coal releases more radiation vs nuclear power plants. if you are concerned about radioactivity you should focus on that?"},{"id":32472417,"author":"kennend3","month":"2022-08","story":"Nuclear power is the climate superhero too nervous to wear its cape","stance":"reference","why":"Rocket-powered train hits a waste cask at 84 mph.","quote":"How much energy was delivered to the cask when a rocket powered train hit it at 84 mph?","text":"Good thing the people designing the transport casks never thought of that.. you should join their team.. Oh wait.. they did in 1972, and tested the designs? How much energy was delivered to the cask when a rocket powered train hit it at 84 mph? [VIDEO] How much radioactive material was released? What sort of accident do you propose would deliver more energy vs a train strike at 84mph?"},{"id":22852116,"author":"jjoonathan","month":"2020-04","story":"New nuclear reactor designs promise safe, clean electricity","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of nuclear waste transport cask crash test.","quote":"","text":"> Unless one of those plants was in New Orleans or Houston and suffered a Fukishima type meltdown If the nuclear industry were a person, when it built Fukushima it wouldn't have been old enough to drive a car. Today, it would be retired. It learned a thing or two in the intervening years. If Fukushima had been built a mere 2 years later it wouldn't have had its fatal flaw. There are some horrible reactor designs still out there posing a danger to everybody -- but refusing to build new reactors because extremely old reactors were (and still are!) dangerous is... tragic. > if one of the thousands of trucks and rail cars that would be required to ship nuclear waste to centralized storage locations crashed or derailed [VIDEO] > Unfortunately many of the most ardent supporters of nuclear power downplay (or deny completely) the tangible and serious risks The people who don't take the risks seriously are the people who refuse to perform numerical comparisons and instead make snap judgements on the basis of meltdowns being scary. Other energy sources have risks too. The Banqiao dam makes Chernobyl look tame, but hydroelectric has a squeaky clean rep. Even solar and wind do worse per kWh, because their density is so low that they require (comparatively) tons of install work and upkeep, so the severe slip & fall accidents add up. So yeah, we do care about the tangible and serious risks. We should pay more attention to them. I don't think that calculus points in the direction you think "}]},"Fgcd1ghag5Y":{"wy":"Cited critically in political threads about US foreign policy and Libya.","m":[{"id":20698643,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2019-08","story":"The 1619 Project","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Moreover America's hand in promoting and contributing to slavery doesn't need traveling back hundreds of years Just look back 2 years ago: [LINK] Open air slave markets. People sold as animals or produce. What happened there? Well, apparently \"We come, we saw, he died!\" <followed by laughing>. [VIDEO] . That's what happened. I would argue, after \"exporting our democracy\" there, the labor and blood of those slaves are on our hands as well."},{"id":20199335,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2019-06","story":"'Where was the Lord?': Slave testimonies","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> total evil in men's hearts, this was only 200 years ago, not that long ago, this isnt something that happened 1000 years ago. Slavery is alive and well. Don't have to look 200 years into the past. This was just in 2017 in Libya. [LINK] > \"The footage released by CNN appears to show youths from Niger and other sub-Saharan countries being sold to buyers for about $400 (£300) at undisclosed locations in Libya...\" It's not at the same scale as before but the practice certainly hasn't ended. It's also important to ask what allowed it to flourish. > [LINK] > \"...More than 8,000 American personnel in warships and aircraft were deployed in the area. At least 3,000 targets were struck in 14,202 strike sorties, 716 of them in Tripoli and 492 in Brega.[87] The American air offensive included flights of B-2 Stealth bombers, each bomber armed with sixteen 2000-pound bombs, flying out of and returning to their base in Missouri in the continental United States...\" And here is ex Secretary of State Hillary Clinton cheerfully exclaiming \"We came, we saw, he died\" all laughing and giggling like it's a joke. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15804454,"author":"wallace_f","month":"2017-11","story":"Bitcoin hits $10,000","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They laugh like maniacs at Gadaffi's torture and death[3].","text":"I made a mistake, but this is the problem with online comments -- brevity leads to necessary oversimplification. Gadaffi was trying to oust the type of central bank they had been forced to have in Libya. I will explain. Admittedly this is pretty far out in the realm of Wrongthink. Just look at Investopedia or an econ text book, and what they say about the question: does the Fed print money. They say no[1]. This is utterly, undeniably false. After the 2008 financial crisis the Fed's balance sheet ballooned to 4.5 trillion. How did they get that money? I can assure you that they did not (1) take out loans, or (2) produce goods or services at a profit. They electronically debit an account with money, and then go to Wall St and buy what no one else wanted to buy. What did Gadaffi do? He tried to kick out their central bank that was imposed on them by the UN, by creating a gold standard.[2] Most elite politicians are not nice people. They don't care about unjust wars, constitutional abuse or torture. They laugh like maniacs at Gadaffi's torture and death[3]. Fake News is just a useful term to disparage Wrongthink. Every government in the history of this planet has a natural tendency towards cronyism and tyranny. If you take the headlines at face value and don't take the time to actually investigate the legitimacy of the facts then you are no different than any other subject of any other government in history, willingly subjugated to their ideas of what is right and wrong. 1 - [LIN"},{"id":13509282,"author":"general_ai","month":"2017-01","story":"Time to Take a Stand","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here's Hillary laughing about it","text":"That's just with drones, and I'd say it's a very conservative number. He also funded and armed \"moderate opposition\" in Syria, al quaeda associted groups (Jabhat al-Nusra and others) there, and helped topple several governments. This displaced millions and killed hundreds of thousands. Here's Hillary laughing about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5866071,"author":"temphn","month":"2013-06","story":"A.C.L.U. Files Suit Over Phone Surveillance Program","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"We came, we saw, he died!","text":"Reagan funded revolutions in Eastern Europe and the fall of the Berlin Wall. Obama's State Department is giving arms and money to Islamic revolutionaries in Syria, Algeria, Tunisia, and other countries. \"We came, we saw, he died!\" [LINK] [VIDEO] And there isn't even a geostrategic goal like turning Afghanistan against the USSR; it's just setting fire to formerly stable Muslim nations in the name of \"democracy\", just as Bush did."}]},"NlHUz99l-eo":{"wy":"Cited as an example of Apple selling feelings and status rather than specs.","q":{"x":"These ads were about feelings... Not tech specs.","i":32232527,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35702007,"author":"danparsonson","month":"2023-04","story":"Apple wins the antitrust court battle with Epic Games, court rules","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Apple marketing status over features","quote":"Remember the iPod ads?","text":"Sure, but Apple usually don't market based on features and their feature set on paper is often less impressive than competing products. Remember the iPod ads? [VIDEO] What does that advert say? We have the best sound quality? We have the most storage? We have the best value for money? None of those things - it says you should buy this because it will make you look cool. I didn't say every single person who buys an iPhone is just trying to look cool. But that is first and foremost how they sell their products."},{"id":32232527,"author":"anonu","month":"2022-07","story":"I remember feeling derisive about marketing as a young techie","stance":"reference","why":"Ads sold feelings rather than specs","quote":"These ads were about feelings... Not tech specs.","text":"Do you remember the early apple iPod TV commercials? [VIDEO] The ones with the silhouettes of people dancing and the white cord on prominent display? There's not a single mention of what an ipod is or how much space it has or how you connect it to songs. These ads were about feelings... Not tech specs. That's what sells in the end. Were these ads a lie?"},{"id":19445474,"author":"city41","month":"2019-03","story":"AirPods with Wireless Charging Case","stance":"reference","why":"Example of iPod ads making white headphones a status symbol","quote":"iPod commercials featured black silhouetted people with the bright white headphone and cord contrasted on top[0].","text":"Apple has been doing that with headphones ever since the iPod came out. iPod commercials featured black silhouetted people with the bright white headphone and cord contrasted on top[0]. Like AirPods today, it used to be a status symbol to have white headphones as that basically meant they came from Apple. [0] - [VIDEO]"}]},"OS4O8rGRLf8":{"wy":"Repeatedly cited in Nord Stream sabotage threads as evidence of stated US intent toward the pipeline.","q":{"x":"Here's a CSPAN video from 8 months ago of Biden telling a reporter","i":33314845,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":49387817,"author":"somenameforme","month":"2026-08","story":"Japan tried to build an operating system for the world, the US intervened","stance":"reference","why":"Biden's public threat to Nord Stream 2","quote":"","text":"Things being a talking point doesn't mean they're untrue. The US is and was the only party that made any logical sense, to say nothing of also being one of only a handful of nations with the technical expertise to do so as well. The US was against the Nord Stream from the beginning because we felt it could deepen ties between Germany and Russia, unilaterally sanctioned companies involved in it, and of course Biden publicly alluded to threats of blowing it up: \"If Russia invades ... then there will be no longer a Nord Stream 2. We will bring an end to it.\" \"How will you do that, since the project is under Germany's control?\" \"We will, uh, I promise you, we will, uh, be able to do that *smirking*.\" [1] With it blown up we're able to make Europe completely dependent upon US gas sold at a high premium. Well, at least that was probably the goal. Technically the attack failed and at least one of the pipelines was/is still viable, but the US probably was hedging their bets because there was no way to anticipate exactly how enthusiastic Europe would be about setting aside their own self interests for the sake of US geopolitics. It also helps ensure less chance of a reconciliation between Russia and Europe which is probably seen as a reasonable way of weakening both. Reading \"Project for the New American Century\" [2] by the typical names that show in basically every US presidency, Democrat and Republican alike, is crucial to understanding US geopolitics. And one thing we obsess over i"},{"id":45266556,"author":"AndriyKunitsyn","month":"2025-09","story":"Europe is locking itself in to US LNG","stance":"reference","why":"Biden's pre-attack statement on Nord Stream 2","quote":"","text":"The wonders of propaganda make it so that \"Ukrainians bombed it\" is given as an irrefutable fact, and not something that needs a lot of evidence. Nobody remembers anymore that Pres. Biden himself said, “If Russia invades ... there will be no longer a Nord Stream 2. We will bring an end to it.” [°] Nor that the very next day, a EU parliament member, and now Polish foreign minister Radosław Sikorski thanked the US for the sabotage [^]. Nor that the same day, a competing natural gas pipeline has opened, the Baltic Pipe [_]. None of this matters, because \"Ukrainians bombed it\". Because WaPo and WSJ said so. In a waterway that is heavily controlled by all kinds of NATO vessels. Where NATO had an exercise 3 months before that, called BALTOPS. Come on. [°] [VIDEO] [^] [LINK] [_] [LINK]"},{"id":43533304,"author":"atmosx","month":"2025-03","story":"A decision to eject from a failing F-35B fighter and the betrayal in its wake","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a humiliating moment for Germany","quote":"","text":"> I find it refreshing, however, that the \"we are the evil empire now\" idea is getting out of the closet. Call a spade a spade. “Good” and “evil” are moral constructs that haven’t played a meaningful role in documented geopolitics since at least the 4th century BC. There’s a well-known quote often attributed to Hastings Ismay that captures NATO’s original purpose. I won’t paste it here as it might come off as a bit harsh, and I’m not trying to drag this discussion out further. > That's a fantasy, unless you think Germany is occupied by the USA. But that's not the case. Circling back to Germany—I honestly can't think of a more humiliating moment for any NATO member than this[^1]. Sure, Mr. Biden was more aesthetically pleasing than Mr. Trump but take a moment to consider the symbolism and the signals sent to ally nations. Regardless of media narratives, the events of September 26, 2022, marked a turning point that fundamentally altered Germany’s economic path and future. It was a hostile act on a massive scale, and its consequences are undeniably real for the country. [^1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39384790,"author":"4gotunameagain","month":"2024-02","story":"Google is making a map of methane leaks for the whole world to see","stance":"reference","why":"Biden saying US will end the pipeline","quote":"","text":"I highly doubt the Ukrainians were capable of such an act, especially at that time. What about Biden explicitly stating that the US will destroy the pipeline ? [VIDEO]"},{"id":36033679,"author":"wsc981","month":"2023-05","story":"Bakhmut Is Gone: An Aerial Look at the War’s Destruction","stance":"reference","why":"Biden statement beside Scholz","quote":"Biden even stated this in a press conference with Olaf Schulz standing next beside him [0]","text":"Don’t need any sources on this, as several US state officials, even Biden, stated they would do this if the US invaded Ukraine. Biden even stated this in a press conference with Olaf Schulz standing next beside him [0], but again there were more US officials making similar statements. You can wait for “official” proof, while I am sure US officials weren’t making practical jokes. ——— [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35028557,"author":"kilgnad","month":"2023-03","story":"2022 Letter","stance":"reference","why":"Biden's expression when discussing Nord Stream 2","quote":"","text":">Oh, and Nord stream 2 was not the US. At least there's no credible proof or semblance of one. You have to be really lying to yourself to believe that. Look at the twinkle in bidens eye when he says it: [VIDEO] This coupled with: [LINK] It's unmistakable. The US did it and everybody in Germany and Europe knows it. The US was meanwhile over-concerned with their first major F-35 kill ever: Balloon gate. > European countries did much better than china regarding covid. US did protlly because of many internal issues which seem to be unadressable. The US did poorly because of lack of centralized control and abundance of karens and the male counterpart. China had the superior response in terms of infrastructure adaption."},{"id":35070425,"author":"wsc981","month":"2023-03","story":"How to Beat Russia [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Biden and Scholz 2022 press conference","quote":"","text":"I don’t think it’s rocket science. - Condoleeza Rice [2014]: [LINK] - Biden & Schulz [2022]: [VIDEO] - US warns German port of crushing sanctions [2020]: [LINK]"},{"id":34714599,"author":"ttctciyf","month":"2023-02","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Biden's remarks on Nord Stream 2","quote":"","text":"I wonder what Biden was referring to when he stated > \"If Russia invades, that means tanks or troops crossing the border of Ukraine again, then there will be no longer a Nord Stream 2. We will bring an end to it.\" > When asked how, the president says, \"I promise you, we will be able do that.\" (C-SPAN: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":34720206,"author":"nullc","month":"2023-02","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Biden's remarks cited as motive evidence","quote":"","text":"Can someone more in tune with global politics explain to me why it hasn't just been assumed that the us did it? We had the motive. We had the means. oh yea, and our president said we would do it in advance of it happening: \"If Russia invades, that means tanks or troops crossing the border of Ukraine again, then there will be no longer a Nord Stream 2. We will bring an end to it.\" (reporter: \"How will you do that, exactly, since the project is within germany's control?\") \"I promise you we'll be able to do it. (smirks, silence)\" [VIDEO] With that context why wouldn't the default be to assume it was destroyed by the US unless there was compelling evidence otherwise?"},{"id":34744395,"author":"DeLeeuw","month":"2023-02","story":"Blowing Holes in Seymour Hersh's Pipe Dream","stance":"reference","why":"Biden saying he will end Nord Stream 2","quote":"Biden himself literally says \"we will bring an end to it\"","text":"The US was always opposed to Nord Stream and for good reasons. I'm in fact glad it's gone. Many people already speculated it was the US, because Biden himself literally says \"we will bring an end to it\": [VIDEO] With a smirk. Even when pressed how he is going to do it. For me, Hersh' story is the secondary corroboration."},{"id":34829881,"author":"wsc981","month":"2023-02","story":"Debunking Seymour Hersh's Alta-Class Claims","stance":"reference","why":"Biden and Scholz press conference","quote":"","text":"I admit I haven't read the article, but there's not a single doubt in my mind that the US is responsible. The US has made many warnings regarding Nordstream 2 in the past. Maybe details of Hersh's story are incorrect, but I think it must be very clear who the culprit is in the end, the US. It's very clear the US has wanted Europe to be dependant on the US for energy and this has been the case for a long time (see the video of Condoleeza Rice from 2014). I the video with Schulz, Biden very clearly states \"we will put an end to it\". And Schulz is like a lapdog. Victoria Nuland herself is in a big way responsible for the whole situation escalating between Ukraine & Russia as can be seen in this leaked transcript: [LINK] --- Condoleeza Rice [2014]: [LINK] Biden & Schulz [2022]: [VIDEO] Victoria Nuland [2022]: [LINK] US warns German port of crushing sanctions [2020]: [LINK]"},{"id":34865131,"author":"kilgnad","month":"2023-02","story":"Was the Nord Stream 2 Rupture an Accidental Catalyst?","stance":"reference","why":"Biden's demeanor when discussing ending Nord Stream 2","quote":"That scene reveals all.","text":"Oh man you just need to look at Bidens face when he spills the beans: Biden: \"We will bring an end to nord stream 2\" Reporter: \"Just how will you do that? The Project is within Germany's control\" Biden: *eyes twinkle* \"I promise you, we will be able to do it\" That twinkle in his eye. Man. You don't need a leak or a Pulitzer prize winning investigative reporter to blow the lid on it... That scene reveals all. Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33672107,"author":"houstonn","month":"2022-11","story":"Nord Stream leaks confirmed as sabotage, Sweden says","stance":"reference","why":"Biden asked how he will end Nord Stream 2","quote":"","text":"President Biden on Nord Stream 2 Pipeline if Russia Invades Ukraine: \"We will bring an end to it.\" When asked how he will do that. \"We will, uh, I promise you we will be able to do it\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":33314845,"author":"infinitezest","month":"2022-10","story":"Vladimir Putin says the world’s energy infrastructure is at risk","stance":"reference","why":"C-SPAN clip of Biden vowing to end Nord Stream","quote":"Here's a CSPAN video from 8 months ago of Biden telling a reporter","text":"Here's a CSPAN video from 8 months ago of Biden telling a reporter that if Russia invades Ukraine the US will \"put and end to\" the nord stream pipeline. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33389861,"author":"arthurcolle","month":"2022-10","story":"Russia says UK navy blew up Nord Stream, London denies involvement","stance":"reference","why":"Biden's February 2022 remarks on Nord Stream 2","quote":"It seems wild to me that this isn't brought up more.","text":"I have felt that it was definitely the US... I mean President Biden literally said on Feb 7, 2022 that \"We will bring an end to it [Nord Stream 2] if Russia invades Ukraine\" - [VIDEO] It seems wild to me that this isn't brought up more. Russia could totally have done it, but I mean blowing up a pipeline that took a long fucking time to build, at your cost, seems pretty irrational unless to just be a show of force against the EU."},{"id":32806715,"author":"wsc981","month":"2022-09","story":"Russia has cut off gas supplies to Europe indefinitely","stance":"reference","why":"Biden on ending Nord Stream 2","quote":"Biden won't allow Nord Stream 2 to be opened up though","text":"Biden won't allow Nord Stream 2 to be opened up though, and it seems he has more control over the pipeline than (the prime minister of) Germany: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32994042,"author":"samuell","month":"2022-09","story":"Three Nord Stream gas pipelines damaged in one day","stance":"reference","why":"Biden's statement about ending Nord Stream 2","quote":"(from ca 1:20)","text":"Biden himself told they would do it, and \"promised they have ways to do it\" (from ca 1:20): [VIDEO]"},{"id":32997387,"author":"vachina","month":"2022-09","story":"Gas Leak in the Baltic Sea","stance":"reference","why":"Biden quotes on ending Nord Stream 2","quote":"Your president gave a hint roughly 7 months ago.","text":"Your president gave a hint roughly 7 months ago. > “there will no longer be a Nord Stream 2\"[1] > “We will bring an end to it.”[1] > “I promise you: We will be able to do it.\"[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33002860,"author":"SanjayMehta","month":"2022-09","story":"Gas Leak in the Baltic Sea","stance":"reference","why":"Biden promised to take care of NS2","quote":"And Biden promised to take care of NS 2 back in February itself.","text":"Russia could have just turned off the tap. The sabotage ensures Europe can't back off from the US sanctions now. Wrecks Germany's economy, thereby eliminating a rival. VW et al are already talking about moving production to the US. And Biden promised to take care of NS 2 back in February itself. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32013093,"author":"wsc981","month":"2022-07","story":"Nuclear turn green as EU parliament approves new taxonomy","stance":"reference","why":"Press conference showing Germany's limited say over Nord Stream","quote":"Germany has nothing to say over Nordstream","text":"Germany is just the b*tch of the USA though, that becomes very clear in this press conference. Germany has nothing to say over Nordstream: [VIDEO]"}]},"V4sbYy0WdGQ":{"wy":"Linked in tipping debates as the classic argument against tipping culture.","m":[{"id":34500924,"author":"teekert","month":"2023-01","story":"Is tipping getting out of control? Many consumers say yes","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for how tipping is part of US income.","quote":"","text":"I can tell from experience if you come from a non-tipping country you can really have some negative experiences before you realize what's going on :) That said, the \"need\" for a tip does make the waiter/ress a lot more pro-active and helpful than in my country where waiter/ress's can be cranky even. In the US they introduce themselves (\"Hi I'm Jerome, I'm excited to be your waiter today!\" Made me smile every time), make sure you are ok, the level of service is pretty high. We do tip here (Netherlands), but it's more when service was really good. But personnel won't bat an eye when you don't. Of course, we learned from Reservoir Dogs [0] that tipping is basically part of the income in the US. Is that still true? In the Netherlands we generally agree with Mr. Pink, minimum wage is higher here though (I think). [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34192243,"author":"kadomony","month":"2022-12","story":"SouthwestAirlines' Meltdown Shows How Corporations Pit Consumers Against Workers","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an argument that tipping is a stupid concept.","quote":"Watch","text":"If a business is pushing for tips (those dumbass Toast POS consoles that now often start tipping at 20% ), the workers are underpaid. Tipping is getting out of control, and businesses just need to pay more. Tipping is a stupid concept. Japan has the number three economy in the world and they'll refuse tips. Watch [VIDEO] and tell me that he's wrong."},{"id":19031110,"author":"jeena","month":"2019-01","story":"Instacart paying 80 cents an hour because worker received a large tip","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Mr. Pink's argument against tipping.","quote":"","text":"Could someone explain to me what's up with those tips? If I'm doing a job I expect to be paid a specific amount. Tips to me seem like pittance which you would give to someone who at least can't work or something, for services you just pay a normal price which the owner of the place needs to calculate with. I'm often thinking about Mr. Pink in Reservoir Dogs [VIDEO] he makes the point I'm also trying to make. People should get paid for doing service and not need to rely on pittance of the customers because their bosses don't calculate the real cost of doing business. If it were up to me tips would be forbidden. In Japan (as far as I understand) it's an insult to tip, it's almost like: \"This business probably isn't doing well enough to pay you a proper salary, so here's a little something extra.\""},{"id":20528978,"author":"d1zzy","month":"2019-07","story":"DoorDash’s about-face on tipping shows customers have more clout than workers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"obligatory Reservoir Dogs tipping scene","text":"It seems rather logical to me that a person that believes in tipping also cares about the tips going to the right person. Also, obligatory Reservoir Dogs tipping scene (warning, NSFW language): [VIDEO]"}]},"XAfTXYa36f4":{"wy":"Cited whenever HN discusses Apple's vertical integration and its custom silicon.","q":{"x":"People who are really serious about software should make their own hardware","i":34401763,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34401763,"author":"diimdeep","month":"2023-01","story":"Apple Silicon Mac’s have 2-3 times longer battery than PC laptops","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs quoting Alan Kay on software and hardware","quote":"People who are really serious about software should make their own hardware","text":"Apple deserve more market share. \"People who are really serious about software should make their own hardware\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":31053603,"author":"thenerdhead","month":"2022-04","story":"Apple’s risky, yearslong effort to design its own silicon is paying off","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs quoting Alan Kay on in-house hardware","quote":"I think Steve Jobs would regularly quote Alan Kay on this idea","text":"Was it really that risky? If anything this seemed like a pretty sure-fire bet by bringing in various operations in-house when partnerships would prove to go sour. I think Apple knew these limitations back with Jobs too. At least that's what I understood from reading various biographies on the many Apple characters & partnerships. Now that Apple has paved the way, you'll likely see this with other big computer companies like Microsoft for their mainline products & development. I think Steve Jobs would regularly quote Alan Kay on this idea: [VIDEO] I think that given Intel has recently changed leadership (for the better ridding the culture of MBAs), we may even see unique partnerships as well that may act as this \"in-house\" hardware providers."},{"id":26011781,"author":"oumua_don17","month":"2021-02","story":"Ask HN: What technically allows the M1 to perform better?","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs quoting Alan Kay, showing Apple's long-term silicon vision","quote":"SJ quotes Alan Kay","text":"You are right. SJ quotes Alan Kay [VIDEO] This was some 15 years back and it seems it was always Apple long term vision to be where they are today with M1. It would be completely fascinating to see how such vision was realised across two CEOs and multiple C level changes combined with a secretive culture. Any ex-Appleites here to throw some light?"},{"id":15165848,"author":"snom380","month":"2017-09","story":"BeOS almost made Apple a different company (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs quoting Alan Kay on building your own hardware","quote":"If you really care about software, you should build your own hardware.","text":"He quoted Alan Kay during the iPhone launch to explain how Apple went about doing things: \"If you really care about software, you should build your own hardware.\" [VIDEO]"}]},"ZMGCFLEYakM":{"wy":"HN cites it in robotics threads as proof that legged machines were mechanically feasible in the 1960s, long before Boston Dynamics.","q":{"x":"The mechanics are almost academic as is evidenced by the GE walker from 1965","i":6501000,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":20960132,"author":"alex_young","month":"2019-09","story":"Walking Truck","stance":"reference","why":"Video of the walking truck in action.","quote":"The thing looks a little top heavy to me.","text":"Video: [VIDEO] The thing looks a little top heavy to me. I wonder if there are airbags :) Also - external hydraulics seems like quite a limitation."},{"id":20961794,"author":"krapp","month":"2019-09","story":"Walking Truck","stance":"reference","why":"Early 1960s walking machine versus modern Boston Dynamics quadrupeds.","quote":"CAM was created in the 1960s","text":"Progress takes time, and ambulatory motion is a finicky, delicate nightmare that only seems simple in humans and animals thanks to millions of years of evolution. CAM was created in the 1960s[0], but BigDog[1] and other recent Boston Dynamics quadrupeds that aren't designed for human passengers are much faster[2], but still awkward. Also remember these are expensive prototypes designed to test things other than speed. Making them faster only guarantees that they break themselves harder when they run into a wall or trip over a cliff or something. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] >I think Neal Stephenson described a skateboard with hundreds of tiny feet, moving faster than wheels on any surface. I want to see that. I am neither Neal Stephenson nor a physicist but I'm pretty sure the friction caused by the increased surface contact of \"hundreds of tiny feet\" versus wheels would still make the wheels faster and more efficient. Geckos and things can stick to vertical surfaces and move quickly because they're lightweight. Anything capable of sticking to an arbitrary surface and supporting human weight is going to be slow. And if moving downhill, wheels don't really need to do work - gravity does the work. Something with \"hundreds of tiny feet\" either has to slide or \"walk.\""},{"id":14713063,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-07","story":"Under the Hood of Luminar's Long-Reach Lidar","stance":"reference","why":"GE Walking Truck as an early walking vehicle.","quote":"General Electric Walking Truck (1969)","text":"It's been tried a few times. Early versions were really clunky. Later versions were usually art projects. * General Electric Walking Truck (1969) [1] * Adaptive Suspension Vehicle (1984) [2] * Korean giant mecha (2016) [3] The BPG Motors Uno transforming motorcycle seemed a good idea, but they gave up on it.[4] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":6499500,"author":"robomartin","month":"2013-10","story":"Boston Dynamics Unleashes Wildcat, the Sprinting Quadruped Robot","stance":"reference","why":"GE walking truck cited in a robotics debate.","quote":"","text":"In a different thread there was an argument about the relative futility of some of today's robotics experiments [0]. As it is sometimes typical on HN, the poster with a message outside the \"go team\" cargo-cult mentality wasn't very welcome. I happen to think he had a very interesting point. And that point translates well into this thread. Why don't we see practical applications of all of these robotics experiments? The answer is very simple really: Most of them are relatively pointless and add very little to the robotics knowledge-base that will be needed to really move robotics forward into real-world applied robots. Think of something like robotics vacuum cleaners. Nothing whatsoever innovative about any of them. It's a wheeled platform that has been in use in hobby and research robotics since, well, forever. The '70's and '80's were full of robots with this basic platform. What changed? Electronics got better, batteries smaller, microprocessors more capable, manufacturing more efficient. What was retained and reused from prior research? Probably not much. I started in college with the goal of becoming a robotics engineer. An EE with specialization in robotics. It didn't take long for me to realize that the field wasn't as interesting and exciting as I made it out to be in my mind. The R2D2's and C3-PO's were nowhere to be found and were easily decades away from becoming reality. If I wanted to be in robotics I would end-up making industrial manipulators or things with moto"},{"id":6501000,"author":"robomartin","month":"2013-10","story":"Boston Dynamics Unleashes Wildcat, the Sprinting Quadruped Robot","stance":"reference","why":"Shows mechanics of legged machines were solved by the 1960s.","quote":"The mechanics are almost academic as is evidenced by the GE walker from 1965","text":"Not sure I understand your position. In the spectrum of what robotics could do for society I see a wide range of truly paradigm shifting applications. These are applications that can change the lives of millions of people. They all require massive improvements in the areas I mentioned in my post. The mechanics are almost academic as is evidenced by the GE walker from 1965 [0]. In terms of mechanical engineering we could do an insanely complex machine back in 1965. I see no point in creating something as clumsy as Wildcat when what we need are significant improvements in AI. Think of what would be required for a quadruped to be able to climb a rocky terrain like a goat. We can do the mechanics. We could do the mechanics in 1965. What we can't do yet is the reasoning, control and path planning required to complete such a task. We don't need to continue to build a bunch of useless contraptions to advance these fields. We are throwing money at the wrong problems. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"aYbNh3NS7jA":{"wy":"HN uses it as a source for Guido's views on Rust, Go and Python 4, with timestamps shared.","q":{"x":"His views on Rust starts at","i":27261031,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":36645074,"author":"blamazon","month":"2023-07","story":"Meta pledges Three-Year sponsorship for Python if GIL removal is accepted","stance":"reference","why":"Guido on why Python 4 will likely never happen","quote":"I found a video interview from May 21, 2021","text":"I was curious about these comments and did a small web search. I found a video interview from May 21, 2021 [1] and have pasted some excerpted quotations from it from Guido van Rossum below for others who are curious. “Python 4, at this point whenever it’s mentioned in the core development team, it is very much as a joke… We’ve learned our lesson from Python 3 vs 2, and so it’s almost taboo to talk about a Python 4 in a serious sense.” [...] “I normally talk about that as a mistake, because Python was more successful than the core developers realised and so we should have been much more aware and supportive of transitioning from Python 2 to Python 3” [...] “I’m not thrilled about the idea of Python 4 and nobody in the core dev team really is – so probably there never will be a 4.0 and we’ll just keep numbering until 3.33, at least” [...] “We now have a strict annual release schedule, so after 3.10 will be 3.11 and after that will be 3.12, and so forth. We can go up to 3.99 before we have to add another digit. Adding another digit is not completely trivial, but still much better than going from 3 to 4.\" [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27380292,"author":"pansa2","month":"2021-06","story":"Why Python 4.0 might never arrive, according to its creator","stance":"reference","why":"Guido calling Go the most Pythonic new language","quote":"","text":"Is Nim particularly similar to Python? More than Go, which Guido recently described as being the new language that’s most Pythonic? [VIDEO]"},{"id":27261031,"author":"eesmith","month":"2021-05","story":"What Python creator Guido van Rossum thinks of Rust, Go, Julia, and TypeScript","stance":"reference","why":"Guido's views on Rust, Go, Julia and TypeScript with timestamps","quote":"His views on Rust starts at","text":"It links to the one hour interview at YouTube - [VIDEO] . His views on Rust starts at [VIDEO] . Go at [VIDEO] . (Short) Julia at [VIDEO] After making his comments about Julia, \"I\"m sure in 5 minutes that will be on Hacker News with a counter-example\" (of a web server in Julia \"with a lot of mileage out of it). TypeScript at [VIDEO] . Of the four mentioned language, that has the most influence on Python."}]},"bV9ppvY8Nx4":{"wy":"HN cites it as a source on the illegality of lethal booby traps, including in discussions of trapping hackers.","q":{"x":"Potentially lethal boobytraps left in derelict buildings are mostly illegal in the US[1]","i":32257743,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34651295,"author":"askiiart","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: Best Way to Mess with Hackers?","stance":"reference","why":"Legal case comparable to setting traps against hackers.","quote":"it seems like it would be a similar case to the one of the shotgun booby trap.","text":"As for the USA, it seems like it would be a similar case to the one of the shotgun booby trap. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32257743,"author":"munk-a","month":"2022-07","story":"Fake Dog for Home Security","stance":"reference","why":"Source on illegality of lethal booby traps in the US.","quote":"Potentially lethal boobytraps left in derelict buildings are mostly illegal in the US[1]","text":"Depending on the jurisdiction you might end up paying quite a lot of money. Potentially lethal boobytraps left in derelict buildings are mostly illegal in the US[1] and in other western countries shooting a thief is generally illegal unless you can prove fear of bodily harm since you are escalating a situation from damage to property to damage to body. While the US is rife with stand your ground laws - most of the rest of the western world finds using potentially lethal force in response to property damage abhorrent. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26076841,"author":"rendall","month":"2021-02","story":"20% of requests for Wikimedia Commons are for one image of a flower","stance":"reference","why":"Legal case on a shotgun booby trap that injured a burglar.","quote":"","text":"It's also illegal to set a trap in your own home in the US as well, decided when a property owner, tired of people breaking into his property while he was away, set up a shotgun booby trap that injured a burglar. [VIDEO] I wasn't sure if it is the same or similar principle in Russia or a different one that requires active care for a burglar. Unlabeled chemicals causing liability for a burglar seems extreme to me"}]},"fNgEG5rCav4":{"wy":"Cited as the source of Levandowski's remark agreeing with Elon Musk that lidar is a crutch.","q":{"x":"Anthony Levandowski of Waymo LIDAR fame has said he now agrees with Elon Musk","i":19799134,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":28410211,"author":"xedeon","month":"2021-09","story":"New Tesla Roadster delayed again, now shooting for 2023","stance":"reference","why":"Expert Levandowski cited as agreeing with lidar skepticism","quote":"many other high-level experts on this space like Anthony Levandowski","text":"Just FYI, many other high-level experts on this space like Anthony Levandowski who actually worked with Sebastian Thrun after meeting him at the 2005 DARPA Grand Challenge. Said the same thing. There's a reason why I asked if you could share your work. Because those two individuals are clearly at the top of their field. If your thesis is correct, then it's easily worth 100+M if not 1B+ USD in value. It's easy to be hyper critical, compared to actually being in the trenches trying to solve these hard problems. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23184281,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2020-05","story":"GM self-driving tech unit Cruise laying off about 8% of staff","stance":"reference","why":"Levandowski backtracks on lidar, says Musk is right","quote":"Anthony Levandowski, former Google/Waymo engineer, now backtracks on lidar and says, ”Elon is right.","text":"[LINK] [VIDEO] > Anthony Levandowski, former Google/Waymo engineer, now backtracks on lidar and says, ”Elon is right.”"},{"id":20625800,"author":"sabertoothed","month":"2019-08","story":"Elon Musk: “Anyone relying on Lidar is doomed.” Experts: Maybe not","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Levandowski's remark on lidar","quote":"The remark by Levandowski can be found here","text":"You seem very angry for some reason that I do not understand. Your emotional response is not backed up by facts. Hotz can certainly be considered an expert as founder and president of commai.ai. Whether he is a loudmouth or not is less relevant. The remark by Levandowski can be found here: [VIDEO] Whether these guys are ethical etc. is not of interest for this question. > Elon really is alone in claiming that lidar isn’t needed for self-driving. No, precisely not."},{"id":19799134,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2019-05","story":"Musk Can’t Dodge Tesla Cash Woes Any Longer","stance":"reference","why":"Levandowski says he now agrees lidar is a crutch","quote":"Anthony Levandowski of Waymo LIDAR fame has said he now agrees with Elon Musk","text":"Anthony Levandowski of Waymo LIDAR fame has said he now agrees with Elon Musk (\"a wiser person than me\") that LIDAR is a crutch. [VIDEO]"}]},"gqht2bIQXIY":{"wy":"Used as a source for back-of-envelope numbers on tree planting and by critics of feel-good climate gestures.","m":[{"id":30696930,"author":"danuker","month":"2022-03","story":"Launch HN: Living Carbon (YC W20) – Trees that capture and store more carbon","stance":"reference","why":"Back-of-envelope math on trees needed to offset US emissions","quote":"to offset the US CO2 emissions, you need to plant about 20 million trees PER DAY","text":"A back-of-the-envelope calculation [0] shows that, to offset the US CO2 emissions, you need to plant about 20 million trees PER DAY. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":28166854,"author":"danuker","month":"2021-08","story":"How green is blue hydrogen?","stance":"reference","why":"Planting 20 million trees offsets about half a day of US CO2","quote":"planting 20 million trees would equal roughly the CO2 emissions of the USA for half a day.","text":"It is very difficult to keep up with global CO2 production by planting trees. TL; DW: planting 20 million trees would equal roughly the CO2 emissions of the USA for half a day. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27824595,"author":"danuker","month":"2021-07","story":"$100M xPrize for Carbon Removal","stance":"reference","why":"Planting 20 million trees offsets about half a day of US CO2","quote":"planting 20 million trees would equal roughly the CO2 emissions of the USA for half a day.","text":"Planting trees it is very difficult to keep up with global production. TL; DW: planting 20 million trees would equal roughly the CO2 emissions of the USA for half a day. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42574590,"author":"magic_smoke_ee","month":"2025-01","story":"Where are Mr. Beast's early sponsors now?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"celebrate this greenwashing nonsense without using their brains first","text":"I don't know who they were or who they were targeting. I only remember their #TeamTrees annoying nonsense from 2019 and the popular Youtubers who fell all over themselves trying to celebrate this greenwashing nonsense without using their brains first.[0] 0. TeamTrees vs REALITY!! [VIDEO]"},{"id":41556884,"author":"hi-v-rocknroll","month":"2024-09","story":"How to succeed in MrBeast production (Leaked PDF)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"pretending to save the planet while failing at math, logic, and physics","text":"So what really turned me off to this guy was his smarmy ~2019 campaign that also damaged my view of otherwise sensible Youtubers who shilled for him without thinking carefully. As such, pretending to save the planet while failing at math, logic, and physics because getting rich and being a internet->real celebrity are oh so much more important™ than emotionally and financially exploiting your audience or doing what you say you're going to do. sigh \"#TeamTrees vs. REALITY\" (2019, Phil Mason) [VIDEO]"},{"id":37919456,"author":"sacnoradhq","month":"2023-10","story":"Many Climate 'Solutions' Are Dead Ends or Niches and Should Be Ignored","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"95% correct.","text":"95% correct. \"Planting a lot of trees\" has been proven dead wrong because of simple math, plant matter decay, and increasing fucking forest fires. Instead, #teamtrees couldn't face reality while doing the disservice of misinforming and lying to millions of people for egotistical and aspirational post-factual rhetoric. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36829958,"author":"1letterunixname","month":"2023-07","story":"House Republicans propose planting a trillion trees","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"House Republicans, now with more #TeamTrees.","text":"House Republicans, now with more #TeamTrees. \"TeamTrees vs REALITY!!\" [VIDEO] Climate change can best be solved by scalable biological means like phytoplankton and seaweed grown, processed, and sequestered by autonomous robotic platforms. (Sacrificial areas of deep water seem unavoidable.) Fractional distillation of air, planting trees, spraying substances in the air, and solar shades aren't going to work."},{"id":36947691,"author":"1letterunixname","month":"2023-07","story":"Plans to plant billions of trees threatened by undersupply of seedlings","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is as absurd as it is futile because of forest fires, decay, and math.","text":"This is as absurd as it is futile because of forest fires, decay, and math. \"TeamTrees vs REALITY!!\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":35212610,"author":"sacnoradhq","month":"2023-03","story":"An Introduction to Forest Carbon","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Remember the delusional, happy-clappy \"helping\" of #teamtrees?","text":"Remember the delusional, happy-clappy \"helping\" of #teamtrees? Did they make a dent? And let's completely ignore that trees burn and biomass rots because the message of \"I'm better than you!\" is more important than reality of anthropogenic global warming. [VIDEO] Rake America Great Again!"},{"id":28768733,"author":"stooliepidgin","month":"2021-10","story":"We need to take CO2 out of the sky","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Doing \"something\" is futile if it's ineffective, not permanent, and not scalable.","text":"TeamTrees vs REALITY!! [VIDEO] Doing \"something\" is futile if it's ineffective, not permanent, and not scalable."}]},"jahtu1_idVU":{"wy":"Mostly used as an example in repair debates, with commenters calling the design an oversight or an anti-repair tactic.","m":[{"id":22567365,"author":"rrmoelker","month":"2020-03","story":"Europe Wants a ‘Right to Repair’ Smartphones and Gadgets","stance":"reference","why":"Example of liquid damage design oversight fixed by chip replacement.","quote":"new age Macbook pro's dying from little water ingress (design oversight)","text":"Scrape off rust, replace chips that have been shorted. Does depend on chips being available and some knowledge on how to diagnose the damages (e.g.: board schematics). Here's an example of new age Macbook pro's dying from little water ingress (design oversight). And a combination of knowledge and a simple chip can fix your nearly write off Macbook. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27255544,"author":"Greek0","month":"2021-05","story":"One man’s fight for the right to repair broken MacBooks","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Either this is an embarrassing, junior-level oversight or a deliberate anti-repair-buy-new-hardware tactic.","text":"The repairability Rossmann is talking about is not user-replaceable components. His repairs usually require soldering to replace components. One of his main critique points is that Apple makes it hard to repair their products, even for electronics professionals. In the past, Apple has also altered designs so that small electrical problems suddenly fry the most expensive component on the board, the CPU. Either this is an embarrassing, junior-level oversight or a deliberate anti-repair-buy-new-hardware tactic. See, for example, [VIDEO] As such, it seems you are arguing against an idea that no one is suggesting."},{"id":26954134,"author":"RicoElectrico","month":"2021-04","story":"Merge tag 'arm-Apple-M1-5.13' of Linux","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"stupid design on Apple part that compromises longevity and resilience","text":"The style of issues he has to deal with are stupid design on Apple part that compromises longevity and resilience. Like really retarded, easily avoidable crap. And sheer spite. e.g. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"}]},"kJQP7kiw5Fk":{"wy":"Mentioned as a view-count record holder and as a sample of protected media for downloader and encoder discussions.","m":[{"id":42717256,"author":"dzek69","month":"2025-01","story":"Show HN: I made a tool to save multimedia from various platforms","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"for protected media, like this one","text":"You can't post direct links you get from youtube on your server to the users, they will get 403 most often if they use these links from different IP. Heck, you even have the IP in the link itself :) 2605:a140:2236:8761::1 Also for protected media, like this one: [VIDEO] it only gives 360p and audio this way. Far from 4K you promise."},{"id":22573472,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2020-03","story":"SVT-AV1: open-source AV1 encoder and decoder","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Despacito music video","text":"> The encoding still isn't ready for user driven content sites. YouTube has lots of AV1 encodes. You've probably watched AV1 encoded content without realising it. Here are a few of examples. To verify that they're AV1 encoded, right-click on the video and select \"Stats for Nerds\". I'm playing them in Firefox 75 beta. Halo trailer: [LINK] Despacito music video: [VIDEO] Porsche Taycan commercial: [LINK] For some videos YouTube has AV1 encodes up to 480p, others are up to 1080p, and some are up to 4K."},{"id":18139468,"author":"franciscop","month":"2018-10","story":"'Despacito' Is Now the Most Viewed Video in YouTube History","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"5.5 billions views according to Youtube itself","text":"5.5 billions views according to Youtube itself [1]. So it's way over the Gangnam Style (3.2B) and See you again (3.8B). Impressive, it's way more popular than I even thought possible. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"lKie-vgUGdI":{"wy":"Commenters quote it as a punchline when a group claims to be larger or more significant than it is.","m":[{"id":33921374,"author":"greggarious","month":"2022-12","story":"Twitter will delete 1.5B accounts to free up space: Elon Musk","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Finally I can be sure that all my dozen unused accounts will be deleted for good this time. Thanks, due to my broken brain now I've got that weird bald guy from Arrested Development stuck in my head[1] :-) (I mostly used Twitter for comedy towards the end -- apparently it creates a problem if you start tweeting like you're gunning for a guest spot on \"Chapo Trap House\" while collecting lewd DMs from local party officials like Pokemon cards -- my account was suspended. Womp womp!) (But in all seriousness, Elon was on my blocklist and has not properly navigated that conflict of interest.) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21576590,"author":"joeblau","month":"2019-11","story":"Thousands flock to Wikipedia founder's 'Facebook rival'","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This reminds me of \"There are dozens of us... DOZENS","text":"This reminds me of \"There are dozens of us... DOZENS\"[1]. While I am happy that people are diversifying their social network choice, 160k is a rounding error to Facebook. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":17864390,"author":"what_ever","month":"2018-08","story":"Some Facebook Employees Unite to Challenge Its ‘Intolerant’ Liberal Culture","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's Arrested Development reference","text":"It's Arrested Development reference: [VIDEO] I am glad it's voted down. If I have Reddit for that kind of comments already."}]},"mPnx3zO3SDc":{"wy":"HN uses it as a quoted joke in threads about human limitations versus machine perception.","m":[{"id":38698308,"author":"bmurphy1976","month":"2023-12","story":"James Webb Space Telescope captures high-resolution image of Uranus","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Queue Battlestar Galactica rant","text":"It is amazing. I really wish I could see that level of detail with my own eyes. Queue Battlestar Galactica rant ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":30548055,"author":"jaquers","month":"2022-03","story":"Underwater power cables affects crabs biology at a cellular level","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I want to see Gamma Rays!","text":"I thought the same thing - reminded me of birds. (I was casually aware that we knew they had some sort of magnetism sensing, but hadn't caught up with the fact that we think it is a visual thing) [LINK] One last random association \"I want to see Gamma Rays!\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":30595464,"author":"scott113341","month":"2022-03","story":"The voice of Utah's arches – Ambient seismic vibration sped up 25x","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the character laments the sensory limitations of the human body","text":"You reminded me of this scene from Battlestar Galactica that has stuck with me since I watched it (spoiler alert). Conversely, the character laments the sensory limitations of the human body :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":29531694,"author":"datameta","month":"2021-12","story":"NewLimit: a company built to extend human healthspan","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I’m a machine, and I can know much more.","text":"\"I don’t want to be human! I want to see gamma rays, I want to hear X-rays, and I want to smell dark matter. Do you see the absurdity of what I am? I can’t even express these things properly, because I have to—I have to conceptualize complex ideas in this stupid, limiting spoken language, but I know I want to reach out with something other than these prehensile paws, and feel the solar wind of a supernova flowing over me. I’m a machine, and I can know much more.\" [VIDEO] Context at 1:14, quote at 2:08"}]},"mUmlv814aJo":{"wy":"Cited as a snapshot of Chinese humanoid robot capability, with some commenters doubting its authenticity.","q":{"x":"They’re incredibly capable (again, save for hands which is a huge mechanical and software problem) and cheap.","i":47983960,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47983960,"author":"SequoiaHope","month":"2026-05","story":"I've Covered Robots for Years. This One Is Different","stance":"reference","why":"Shows capable Chinese humanoid robots performing martial arts.","quote":"They’re incredibly capable (again, save for hands which is a huge mechanical and software problem) and cheap.","text":"Have you seen the mass produced humanoids from China? They’re incredibly capable (again, save for hands which is a huge mechanical and software problem) and cheap. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":47218311,"author":"dangus","month":"2026-03","story":"U.S. science agency moves to restrict foreign scientists from its labs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Used to be that America was great because the smartest researchers in the world wanted to come here, often escaping oppressive regimes to do so, and become American citizens (e.g., Albert Einstein) So now all the world’s best and brightest scientists will move to China where they’ll be welcomed in open arms, enjoy living in a modern society with affordable electric cars, the world’s premier high speed train network, glimmering new subway systems, and ample affordable housing. They’ll work on cutting-edge research projects that receive ample funding and support while American scientists wrestle with a federal government torn apart by anti-intellectual strongmen. You ever see a Tesla robot demo like this? [VIDEO] Are we tired of winning yet? It sounds like we are beyond tired of winning, we’d rather lose from here on out. Seems like Russia and the USA are hell-bent on destroying themselves fighting forever wars to allow China and the EU to take the reins as the beacons of global stability and strength."},{"id":47071212,"author":"somenameforme","month":"2026-02","story":"Martial arts robots dazzle at 2026 Spring Festival Gala [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This is a timestamp to 1:24","text":"Can you elaborate on where? I'm not seeing anything at all at 1:25. This is a timestamp to 1:24: [VIDEO] I was initially somewhat skeptical as well because this looks like a surprisingly massive leap in robotics capability, but haven't been able to find anything particularly sus."},{"id":47073081,"author":"somenameforme","month":"2026-02","story":"Martial arts robots dazzle at 2026 Spring Festival Gala [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Robot falling and recovering at 2:48.","quote":"","text":"Why do you think it would be the case about e.g. swapping to thick carpet would throw things off? Intuitively it seems like they must have a tremendous amount of dynamic adjustment going on. For instance think of how much variance, driven by dynamics, that there's going to be in the scene at 2:48 [1] where the robot [intentionally] falls over and then aerobically picks itself back up. The motion is certainly scripted, but the exact mechanics in play there almost certainly vary radically from take to take. Imagine something simple like a pool/billiards break. Even if you set up a machine to rack the balls and break them in as close to identical as possible, you'd get wildly different results each time. And the dynamics in this motion is going to dwarf that. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":47071491,"author":"somenameforme","month":"2026-02","story":"Martial arts robots dazzle at 2026 Spring Festival Gala [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"that's 100% CG","text":"Ahhh! When he said staffs I assumed he meant people, not staves! Yeah, that's 100% CG. Here [1] you can see where the kids take a stance where it was to be edited in, and if you pause at the exact moment - it looks pretty bad, with something like a lightsaber just popping in the kid's hand. Hmmm. Not sure what to make of this. I wish it was possible to see the raw unedited footage. It makes me question everything else. [1] - [VIDEO]"}]},"rwvIGNXY21Y":{"wy":"Used as a reference for the perseverance behind improbable streaks and selective reporting of best runs.","m":[{"id":14307060,"author":"Retric","month":"2017-05","story":"How much do hedge fund traders earn?","stance":"reference","why":"Selective reporting of best streaks","quote":"people will record themselves fillping coins over and over then only talk about their best streak.","text":"It's a little worse than that as there are vast incentives to be that guy with 20 heads so people will record themselves fillping coins over and over then only talk about their best streak. Thus you end up with most companies having an 'above average' streak in a given year even if the average investment does poorly. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10172813,"author":"Someone","month":"2015-09","story":"St. Petersburg paradox","stance":"reference","why":"Someone actually flips ten heads in a row","quote":"Not that hard to do. Just a matter of perseverance and a bit of luck.","text":"[VIDEO] Not that hard to do. Just a matter of perseverance and a bit of luck. Yes, it could be faked, but I trust him."},{"id":32082502,"author":"bigyikes","month":"2022-07","story":"Inflation rose 9.1% in June, even more than expected","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"If I predict this coin flip will be heads, and I flip it, and it is heads","text":"Spot-on predictions! If I predict this coin flip will be heads, and I flip it, and it is heads… does that mean I can predict coin flips? What if I do it three times in a row? What if I do it 10 times in a row? [1] [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"tJr_TD1BtF0":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that Clojure support in Unity exists, mostly in threads about game programming and Lisp scripting.","m":[{"id":9811257,"author":"pella","month":"2015-07","story":"John Carmack working on Scheme as a VR scripting language","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Strange Loop(2014) video:\"Clojure in Unity 3D: Functional Video Game Development\" by Ramsey Nasser and Tims Gardner\" [VIDEO] __ and __ [LINK] HN: [LINK]"},{"id":9318436,"author":"dustingetz","month":"2015-04","story":"Myself – v1.0.3","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Not really, for interactive UI development you need to model the UI as a pure function (no state in the view). So that limits you to React or React-like approaches, and game engines: [VIDEO] For backed web development (in Clojure at least) there isn't really a lot of state in the first place - request comes in, request comes out, state is in the database not the application - so there are no framework constraints."},{"id":9198015,"author":"krapp","month":"2015-03","story":"Ask HN: Game programming – engine or from scratch?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"attempts are being made to integrate Clojure support in Unity as well[2]","text":">Is having to learn a game engine going to be faster than starting from the ground up? Yes, pretty much by definition, since a game engine saves you the time of writing an engine. ;) Unity[0] and Game Maker[1] both come with tutorials which will show you how to build a basic game quickly. Game Maker uses its own properietary scripting language called GML, and Unity lets you do scripting in Javascript, C++, and Boo, which is basically Python. Also, attempts are being made to integrate Clojure support in Unity as well[2] On the other hand, if you like actually writing the code, there's a good chance there's a low-level framework (like SDL[3] for C++) which will let you code, but still take care of some of the hassle of cross-platform support and graphics. I've only played around* with SDL and C++ but there are quite a few tutorials for it. Also worth mentioning is Construct2[4], a game engine for html5/js. It is possible to do 3d rendering in javascript/html5[5], but as far as I know, only Chrome supports it well. Last I checked you have to change the config settings in firefox to allow opengl rendering to work and I just assume IE doesn't support it by default. If you want to work with 3D and want to get something done then Unity might be a good idea, although of course you're going to need good models, etc. Game Maker supports 3d but it's primarily for 2d. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] *I've got some of the basics for Space Invaders done, "}]},"u-kkf76TDHE":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a source for what Go team members said about language regrets and Go's adoption.","q":{"x":"Here is Andrew Gerrand addressing precisely this point two years ago at GopherCon","i":11211112,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11211112,"author":"sagichmal","month":"2016-03","story":"Go channels are bad","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here is Andrew Gerrand addressing precisely this point two years ago at GopherCon","text":"This is a frustrating and overly-exasperated post which reaches conclusions that have always been part of the Go canon. APIs should be designed synchronously, and the callers should orchestrate concurrency if they choose -- yes! Channels are useful in some circumstances, but if you just want to synchronize access to shared memory (like the author's example) then you should just use a mutex -- yes! These are well understood truths. Novices to the language have a tendency to overuse channels. Here is Andrew Gerrand addressing precisely this point two years ago at GopherCon: [VIDEO] Neither the referenced performance characteristics via Tyler Treat, nor the FUD surrounding channel-based program architecture, invalidate channels generally. One does have to think carefully about ownership hierarchies: only one goroutine gets to close the channel. And if it's in a hot loop, a channel will always perform worse than a mutex: channels use mutexes internally. But plenty of problems are solved very elegantly with channel-based CSP-style message passing. It's unfortunate that articles like this are written and gain traction. The explicit instruction to [new] Go programmers is that they should avoid channels, even that they are badly implemented, and both of those things are false."},{"id":8472425,"author":"xkarga00","month":"2014-10","story":"Avoiding Complexity with Go","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Rob Pike mentioned variable declaration when asked at GopherCon what he wished he hadn't done in Go","text":"Rob Pike mentioned variable declaration when asked at GopherCon what he wished he hadn't done in Go [VIDEO]"},{"id":7828891,"author":"hjr265","month":"2014-06","story":"Python Programming for the Privileged Class","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"there was a question/discussion similar to this here","text":"I would definitely prefer jmpeax's answer. Although, there was a question/discussion similar to this here: [VIDEO] The gist was that, if the interface and the structs that are supposed to satisfy that interface belong to the same package, and then you want to prevent any outsider from implementing that interface, you can actually include an unexported method: type HtmlStringer interface { ToString() string isHtmlStringer() } Since it is unexported, no outsider struct can actually satisfy this interface anymore."},{"id":7856551,"author":"jackyb","month":"2014-06","story":"Google Trends: Golang is popular in China","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"One of the Go developers did say that Go has a big following in China in GopherCon 2014","text":"One of the Go developers did say that Go has a big following in China in GopherCon 2014, thanks to Windows. [VIDEO]"}]},"xbiDrzTd8fE":{"wy":"Used as a quick visual explanation of how CDOs packaged risky mortgage debt.","q":{"x":"you basically have to have seen the movie \"The Big Short\" to understand the analogy in the article.","i":39830494,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39830494,"author":"umvi","month":"2024-03","story":"Tech Debt: My Rust Library Is Now a CDO","stance":"reference","why":"Scene needed to understand the article's analogy","quote":"you basically have to have seen the movie \"The Big Short\" to understand the analogy in the article.","text":"PSA: you basically have to have seen the movie \"The Big Short\" to understand the analogy in the article. Or at least this scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36671443,"author":"adhesive_wombat","month":"2023-07","story":"Tuition costs have risen 710% since 1983 (1200% since 1980). Inflation: 194%","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier explanation of a non-synthetic CDO","quote":"The Big Short also has an earlier explanation of a non-synthetic CDO","text":"The Big Short also has an earlier explanation of a non-synthetic CDO (\"we just repackage it [low-quality risky debt] along with a bunch of other shit that didn't sell and put it into a CDO\"): [VIDEO]"},{"id":19440198,"author":"grey-area","month":"2019-03","story":"Wall Street’s Latest Love Affair with Risky Repackaged Debt","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how CDOs were packaged mortgage debt","quote":"packaged mortgage debt which is going to default, mixed and dressed up as very safe debt","text":"The 2018 crisis was caused by CDOs - packaged mortgage debt which is going to default, mixed and dressed up as very safe debt, then sold on to some poor sucker (including you in your pension). [VIDEO] The article is saying that people are buying CLOs - packaged corporate debt which is going to default, mixed and dressed up as very safe debt, then sold on to some poor sucker (including you in your pension). TLDR: History repeats itself, first as tragedy, then as farce."},{"id":26264166,"author":"ThisIsTheWay","month":"2021-02","story":"Show HN: Can’t afford Bloomberg Terminal? No prob, I built the next best thing","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Its a great scene, but this one takes the cake","text":"Its a great scene, but this one takes the cake: [VIDEO] \"That's a nice shirt, do they make it for men?\" \"Somehow you're like Dora the Explorer and you're the first person who has found this thing...\""}]},"xgy0rW0oaFI":{"wy":"Cited as a source lecture series behind arguments on energy, EROEI, nuclear power and climate.","m":[{"id":41776947,"author":"polotics","month":"2024-10","story":"Fukushima Reactor: TEPCO robot aims to extract nuclear fuel","stance":"reference","why":"Source lectures backing claims about solar EROEI.","quote":"Slides of the 20 hours lectures at Ecoles des Mines Paris","text":"Slides of the 20 hours lectures at Ecoles des Mines Paris [LINK] The lectures: [VIDEO] The (already provided in a previous comment) EROEI study: [LINK] The one specific slide about the low EROEI of solar panel is in: [LINK] The 2.7 value is from 2019, as calculated for Spain, this one slide is in english. Panels have not made a x10 EROEI explosion in the past 5 years, a lot of the energy costs in that calculation are independent of panel technology improvements."},{"id":29140960,"author":"NaNDude","month":"2021-11","story":"Nuclear power is the best climate-change solution","stance":"reference","why":"Jancovici lecture cited in debate on nuclear energy.","quote":"","text":"> this specific argument I was supporting with sources none of thoses sources is contradictory of what i explained on the history of our shortages. > Why did you chose hydro? because you talk about mix, today its fr secondary electricity production, and hydro plants are older than fr nuclear plants/projects. >That is half of what EPR needs to invest to keep your ageing fleet from falling apart... i'm not sure of what your talking about again, that's probably half or even less of what we must invest to keep our ageing (not EPR) nuclear fleet yes, which provide 75% of our production, in other words: 8% production for 50billions imported external products + exploitation cost on 20years vs 75% for 50billions local supply one shot to add 20 years to the current fleet, the choice is easy technicaly speaking (not politicaly). our ageing fleet is not linked to EPR at all which may be considered as a prototype for an eventual next fleet, we will see, and as already explained, the cost of our first EPR is not the cost of an EPR in a future fleet. side note: most of our ageing fleet have been build in only 10 years after 20 years of research and \"prototypes\". > you just accepted to ignore all the costs generations will have to come up with just to cover nuclear waste we started a project in 2000 for longterm deep storage which will provide waste stockage for more than 100years of our current dangerous waste production ( [LINK] ), for less dangerous product we are already treating not on"},{"id":25107419,"author":"Xixi","month":"2020-11","story":"Charles Koch Says His Partisanship Was a Mistake","stance":"reference","why":"Jancovici energy lecture cited in climate argument.","quote":"","text":"The current trajectory of temperature increase is at least 4~5°C (rather optimistic) in 2100, which would mean that a pretty wide area surrounding the equator will, year-round or for significant parts of the year, have a wet-bulb temperature at or above 35°C. It is the limit at which human life (and mammal life in general) is entirely impossible, due to over-heating. That unlivable area will include most of India, Indonesia, Nigeria, Brazil, etc. Hong-Kong and Taiwan, whether you consider them as part of China or not, will anyway not be livable anymore by then. What do you think will happen when India, a country of 1.3B armed with nuclear weapons, realize it literally has to move somewhere else for survival? Do you think all these people will agree to die in silence, peacefully so as to not inconvenience you? On top of that grim outlook, agriculture has only been possible relatively recently in human history. Until about 10,000 years ago the climate was not stable enough to reliably grow crops, year after year. That stability is probably already gone. Note that the issue for agriculture (and forests, etc.) is less the actual temperature and more the rain/weather patterns (and evaporation, that links back to temperature). There is absolutely no guarantee that we will be able to adapt our crops fast enough for agriculture to keep up, especially if there is too much instability around the globe. Without stable crops the number of people that can survive on Earth is not very larg"}]},"zrzMhU_4m-g":{"wy":"Posted almost purely as a joke reference when a thread's logic resembles the scene.","m":[{"id":38192163,"author":"RecycledEle","month":"2023-11","story":"8GB RAM on M3 MacBook Pro 'Analogous to 16GB' on PCs, Claims Apple","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"So if Windows is bloated, we can overcharge for RAM! So if she weighs the same as a duck, she's made of wood and therefor a witch! From Monte Python's \"She's a Witch\" at [VIDEO]"},{"id":31575746,"author":"YeGoblynQueenne","month":"2022-05","story":"DALL-E 2 has a secret language","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If I understand correctly from the twitter thread (I haven't read the linked technical report) the author and a collaborator found that DALL-E generated some gibberish in an image that showed two men talking, one holding two ... cabbages? They fed (some of) the gibberish back to DALL-E and it generated images of birds, pecking at things. Conclusion: the gibberish is the expression for birds eating things in DALL-E's secret language. But, wait. Why is the same gibberish in the first image, that has the two men and the cabbages(?), but no birds? Explanation: the two men are clearly talking about birds: >> We then feed the words: \"Apoploe vesrreaitars\" and we get birds. It seems that the farmers are talking about birds, messing with their vegetables! With apologies to my two compatriots, but that is circular thinking to make my head spin. I'm reminded of nothing else as much as the scene in the Knights of the Round Table where the wise Sir Bedivere explains why witches are made of wood: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24814368,"author":"greenyoda","month":"2020-10","story":"Facebook deletes satire post claiming Dem Senator testing ACB as a witch","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"For those not familiar with the witch/duck reference, it's from the film 'Monty Python and the Holy Grail'. Here's the clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13600584,"author":"samstave","month":"2017-02","story":"Redash – Connect to any data source, easily visualize and share your data","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"There is literally only one way we can prove you're not a witch! I shall throw you in the pond! [VIDEO]"},{"id":10269397,"author":"douche","month":"2015-09","story":"America's Military Supremacy Is Fading","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'll see your biased source and raise you another. This one has nicer pictures. [LINK] Let's weigh them and see which one is a duck! [VIDEO]"}]}});
